// Each skill's own SKILL.md, sent to Claude as the instructions for a question.
const SKILL_TEXT = {
"ab-test-planner": "---\nname: ab-test-planner\ndescription: >-\n  Plans an online experiment properly. Works out the sample size from a real\n  baseline, how long it has to run, what the guardrails are, and what each\n  outcome means before anyone looks at the numbers. Use when someone says run an\n  experiment, A/B test this, how many users do we need, is this result\n  significant, or should we ship it.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  answers: did-this-change-cause-that\n---\n\n# A/B test planner\n\nThe one method that tells you what **caused** what. Everything else in this\npipeline reads a before and an after and has to be honest about the difference.\n\nExpensive in traffic and time. Worth it when a decision is big enough that\nbeing wrong matters more than being quick.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## First, the name collision\n\nWe have two things called A/B and they are not the same instrument.\n\n| | What it is | n |\n|---|---|---|\n| **This** | A live experiment. Half the traffic sees each version, and the numbers tell you which worked | Thousands |\n| [Outset A/B](../research-planner/references/outset.md) | A preference test. The same task on both versions, then you ask which they would use and why | 20 to 30 |\n\nThe Outset one tells you *why* someone prefers something. This one tells you\n*whether* it moved anything. If you want both, and you usually do, run the small\none first to shape the change and this one to confirm it.\n\nPicking this when you wanted the other is the expensive mistake. Thousands of\nusers and three weeks to learn something eight people could have told you in an\nafternoon.\n\n## Before you plan anything\n\n**Can we even measure it?** Run [`measurable-check`](../measurable-check/) first.\nAn experiment against an event nobody instrumented returns zero on both arms,\nand that looks exactly like no difference.\n\n**Do we have the traffic?** Work out the sample size before you design anything\nelse. Most of the time this is where it ends, and finding that out now costs\nnothing.\n\n**Do we know what we are changing and why?** If the answer is \"let's see what\nhappens\", you are not ready. That produces a number nobody can interpret.\n\n## The rules that keep this honest\n\n1. **Say which way it should go, and by how much, before you start.** A\n   prediction you can be wrong about is the whole point.\n2. **One primary metric.** Plus one or two guardrails. Three primary metrics\n   means you will find something that moved and call it the result.\n3. **Do not look early.** Checking as you go and stopping when it looks good\n   makes a real result out of noise. Checking for *harm* is different and\n   allowed. Set that threshold up front so the two never get confused.\n4. **One change per variant.** Change three things and a win tells you nothing\n   about which one did it.\n5. **Say what inconclusive costs.** It is the most likely outcome and the one\n   nobody plans for.\n6. **Write the result back whatever it says**, via\n   [`user-context`](../user-context/) `record`. A test that only gets recorded\n   when it wins is worse than no test.\n\n## 1. The prediction\n\n> We think **[change]** will move **[metric]** **[up or down]** by about\n> **[how much]** for **[who]**, because **[what makes us think so]**.\n\nThat last clause is where the Frame earns its keep. \"Because partners told us\nthey click save repeatedly when nothing confirms it [p07, MEDIUM, n=11]\" is a\nreason. \"Because it feels cleaner\" is a hunch, and a hunch is fine as long as\nyou say that is what it is.\n\nAn experiment with no reason behind it is a lottery ticket you waited three\nweeks for.\n\n## 2. The metrics\n\n**Primary.** One. The thing the change is meant to move. Say exactly how it is\ncounted and over what window, so nobody argues about the definition afterwards.\n\n**Guardrails.** One or two things that must not get worse while the primary\nimproves. Usually revenue, retention, or support volume. A conversion lift that\ndoubles the support queue is not a win, it is a cost moved somewhere nobody is\nmeasuring.\n\n**Not the primary:** clicks and time on page, when what you care about is\nrevenue or people staying. A proxy metric will move and tell you nothing.\n\n## 3. How many people\n\nWork this out from our actual baseline, not from a table someone wrote down.\n\nTwo numbers go in. The **baseline**, which is what the metric does today, pulled\nlive from PostHog rather than remembered. And the **smallest change worth\nknowing about**, which is a judgement: a 1% lift might be worth millions or\nworth nothing, and only the team can say.\n\nAt 80% power and 95% significance, two-sided:\n\n```\nn per variant = 7.849 × ( p₁(1-p₁) + p₂(1-p₂) ) ÷ (p₂ - p₁)²\n```\n\nWhich gives, for a few common cases:\n\n| Baseline | Smallest lift worth knowing | Per variant | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5% | 20% relative (5.0 → 6.0) | ~8,200 | ~16,400 |\n| 10% | 15% relative (10.0 → 11.5) | ~6,700 | ~13,400 |\n| 20% | 10% relative (20.0 → 22.0) | ~6,500 | ~13,000 |\n| 40% | 10% relative (40.0 → 44.0) | ~2,400 | ~4,800 |\n| 60% | 5% relative (60.0 → 63.0) | ~4,200 | ~8,400 |\n\nThese were computed from the formula above, not copied. Check them yourself\nagainst Evan Miller's calculator or whatever PostHog gives you, and if a number\nhere disagrees with a tool, trust the tool and fix this table.\n\n**The pattern worth internalising:** the smaller the change you want to detect,\nthe more people you need, and it gets worse fast. Halving the lift you care\nabout roughly quadruples the sample. Most experiments that never reach\nsignificance were asking to detect something too small from the start.\n\n## 4. How long\n\n```\nweeks = (n per variant × 2) ÷ (daily eligible users × share exposed) ÷ 7\n```\n\n**At least two full weeks**, whatever the arithmetic says, so you capture a\nwhole weekly cycle. Partner behaviour on a Tuesday is not partner behaviour on\na Sunday.\n\n**At most four.** Past that, the novelty of a new thing has worn off and\nsomething else has usually shipped on top of your test.\n\n**If it needs more than eight weeks, stop.** The honest answer is that we do not\nhave the traffic for this question. Options, in order of how much we like them:\n\n1. Ask a bigger question. A change you expect to move things more needs fewer\n   people to detect.\n2. Go qualitative. Eight people watched closely will tell you more than an\n   underpowered experiment, and will tell you *why*.\n3. Ship it on judgement and use [`adoption-check`](../adoption-check/)\n   afterwards, saying plainly that it is a before and after rather than an\n   experiment.\n\nOption 3 is legitimate. Option 3 described as an experiment is not.\n\n## 5. What each result means, agreed now\n\nWrite this down before launch. After the numbers land, everyone has an opinion\nabout what counts.\n\n| | Means | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ship** | Primary moved by at least the lift you named, at 95%, and guardrails held | Ship it. Record it as a claim |\n| **Iterate** | Right direction, not significant | Usually means the change was too small, not that the idea was wrong. Make it bigger, do not just run longer |\n| **Reject** | No movement, or it went the wrong way | The most useful outcome. Find out why before anyone reverts |\n| **Inconclusive** | Not enough signal either way | **Not a quiet win.** Say it plainly. Nothing was learned and that is worth knowing |\n\n**Rollback:** if [guardrail] drops by [number], stop the test immediately. That\nis a safety check, not peeking, and it is the one reason to look early.\n\n## 6. Afterwards\n\nRecord it via [`user-context`](../user-context/) `record`:\n\n```\nstatement   <change> moved <metric> from <figure> to <figure>\nconfidence  MEDIUM    one source, however big the numbers\nn           <users per variant, window>\nsource      PostHog experiment: <name>, read <date>\ncaveat      Ran <dates>. Primary <metric>, guardrails held / did not.\n            Predicted by <claim id>; outcome <as predicted | smaller | no | wrong way>.\n```\n\n**MEDIUM, not HIGH**, even with a hundred thousand users in it. One instrument\nis one instrument, and the size of the n does not change that. It becomes HIGH\nwhen something independent agrees, which is usually a handful of people telling\nyou the same story the numbers are telling you.\n\n## What an experiment can't tell you\n\nSay these in the write-up, every time.\n\n**It tells you what moved, never why.** A conversion lift with no explanation is\na fact you cannot build on. Pair it with a few sessions and you have something\nthat lasts.\n\n**It only measures what you chose to measure.** Everything the change did to\npeople that you did not instrument is invisible, including the annoying parts.\n\n**It describes the people in it.** An experiment on active partners says nothing\nabout the ones who never came back, and they are usually the larger group.\n\n**It is true of the moment it ran.** Seasonality, a pricing change or a\nmigration in the same window all look like your change in the numbers.\n\n## Where it sits\n\n```\nmeasurable-check      can we see it at all\n      ↓\nab-test-planner       plan it, size it, agree what counts\n      ↓\n   (it runs)\n      ↓\nadoption-check        read it, record it, say what it means\n```\n\nFor the behaviour the experiment cannot explain, go to\n[`talk-to-users`](../talk-to-users/) or run a small Outset study alongside it.\n",
"accessibility-review": "---\nname: accessibility-review\ndescription: >-\n  Synthetic accessibility pre-test on a prototype URL or Figma flow before Maze\n  or Outset sessions — flags WCAG blockers, focus, labels, contrast, and keyboard\n  paths. Use in Test stage pre-flight or when the designer asks for a11y review\n  before usability testing.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# Accessibility review (Test pre-flight)\n\nSynthetic a11y check **before** user sessions. Complements — does not replace —\nmanual audit or automated CI. Output is **AI interpretation** until verified\nwith axe/DevTools or a11y specialist sign-off.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Review the **actual prototype** (URL or Figma frames), not a generic checklist.\n2. Label findings **Blocker** (likely WCAG A fail — fix before Maze) vs **Major** vs **Minor**.\n3. Call `design-system` when components should come from Styleguide V2.\n4. Do not claim WCAG conformance — report likely issues for designer follow-up.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Test pre-flight inside `test-plan`\n- Before publishing an unmoderated Maze test\n- Designer asks: \"a11y blockers on this prototype?\"\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Inventory UI** — Screens, forms, modals, tables, nav from prototype link or Figma context.\n2. **Keyboard & focus** — Tab order logical? Focus visible? Trap in modals? Skip link?\n3. **Labels & names** — Inputs, buttons, icons-only controls, error associations.\n4. **Contrast & text** — Body vs Styleguide tokens when visible; flag likely <4.5:1.\n5. **Motion & media** — Autoplay, flashing, reduced-motion respect (if applicable).\n6. **Marketplace patterns** — Data tables, filters, multi-step wizards common in Brand/Partner UI.\n7. **Gate** — Blocker list for `test-plan`; recommend fix or documented accept before Maze.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Accessibility review — [prototype]\nArtifact: [URL / Figma link]\nScope: synthetic pre-test (not a conformance audit)\n\n## Blockers (fix before unmoderated test)\n| Issue | Where | WCAG hint | Suggested fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| … | … | … | … |\n\n## Major / Minor\n- …\n\n## Design system\n- Components that should map to Styleguide V2: …\n\n## Gate\n- [ ] Clear for Maze / Outset recruiting\n- [ ] Blockers open — defer test or accept risk: [designer initials/date]\n```\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use accessibility-review on [prototype URL] — flag blockers before Maze.`\n2. `Use accessibility-review on this Figma flow before test-plan.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Approving Maze launch with open Blockers unmarked\n- Generic WCAG lecture without citing prototype screens\n- Claiming \"WCAG AA certified\" from synthetic review\n",
"adoption-check": "---\nname: adoption-check\ndescription: >-\n  Checks what actually happened after something shipped. Reads the baseline from\n  the Frame, re-runs the measure in PostHog, says whether the research held, and\n  writes the outcome back as a claim. Closes the loop from research to decision\n  to result. Use when someone says did it work, check adoption, what happened\n  after we shipped, did the research hold up, or post-launch check.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  closes: research-decision-outcome\n---\n\n# Adoption check\n\nSomething shipped. Did it do what the research said it would?\n\nThis is the step almost every research process is missing, and the one that\nturns a Frame from a library into evidence that research changes outcomes. It is\nalso the only honest way to find out whether we were right.\n\n## Why it matters more than it looks\n\n**It makes research defensible.** \"We found X, we built it, usage moved\" is an\nargument. \"We found X\" is a document.\n\n**It corrects the Frame with reality.** A claim that predicted something which\ndid not happen is the most valuable claim to find, and the only way to find it\nis to look.\n\n**It is how the Frame learns from shipping**, not only from studies. Most of\nwhat an organisation learns about its users, it learns by changing something and\nwatching. Almost none of that reaches the research record.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **The baseline is not optional.** No before, no check. Reconstructing one\n   afterwards is storytelling.\n2. **Report what happened, including when it is nothing.** A flat line is a\n   finding. A drop is a more important one.\n3. **Adoption is not success.** People using a thing is not people being helped\n   by it. Say which you measured.\n4. **Never claim the ship caused the change** without saying what else moved.\n   A launch, a season, a pricing change and a migration all look identical in a\n   line chart.\n5. **Write the outcome back whatever it says.** A check that only gets recorded\n   when it flatters the research is worse than no check.\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Find the claim and its baseline\n- [ ] Re-run the measure\n- [ ] Compare honestly\n- [ ] Name what else could explain it\n- [ ] Write the outcome back\n- [ ] Say what it means for the belief\n```\n\n### 1 — Find the claim and its baseline\n\nFrom the Frame: the claim the decision rested on, and the baseline figure\nrecorded before the change\n([`research-builder`](../research-builder/references/dashboard.md) writes it).\n\n**No baseline?** Say so and stop. You can still read today's number — but it is\na new baseline for next time, not a verdict on this change. Record it that way.\n\n### 2 — Re-run the measure\n\nSame insight, same definition, same filters. Full protocol:\n[references/measure.md](references/measure.md).\n\n```\nexec  learn -s \"<topic>\"                   once, first, alone\nexec  call insight-query {\"insightId\": \"<short_id>\"}\nexec  call dashboard-insights-run {\"id\": <id>}\n```\n\n**Re-run the original, do not rebuild it.** A rebuilt query means something\nslightly different, and the difference will be invisible and the size of your\nfinding.\n\nGive the change a fair window: at least a fortnight of post-ship data, longer if\nusage is weekly or monthly.\n\n### 3 — Compare honestly\n\n| Reading | Means |\n|---|---|\n| **Moved as predicted** | The research held. Say so, and promote the claim |\n| **Moved less than predicted** | Partly held. Which part did not? |\n| **Did not move** | The change did not reach people, or did not matter. These are different — check exposure before concluding |\n| **Moved the wrong way** | The most valuable result. Find out why before anyone reverts |\n| **Cannot tell** | Instrumentation or window. Say so plainly rather than picking a story |\n\n**Check exposure before concluding.** A feature nobody saw cannot have failed.\nSeparate *did they encounter it* from *did it help*, every time — conflating\nthem is how a discovery problem gets rebuilt as a value problem.\n\n### 4 — Name what else could explain it\n\nBefore attributing anything, list what else moved in the window: other releases,\nseasonality, a pricing or packaging change, a migration, a marketing push, an\ninstrumentation change.\n\n**If you cannot rule them out, say the change is consistent with the research\nrather than caused by it.** That sentence costs nothing and protects every\nfuture claim you make.\n\n### 5 — Write the outcome back\n\nA new claim in the Frame, via [`user-context`](../user-context/) `record`:\n\n```\nstatement   After <change>, <measure> went <figure> to <figure>\nconfidence  MEDIUM     one instrument, however large the n\nn           <people, window>\nsource      PostHog: <insight>, read live <date>\ncaveat      Baseline <date>. Also in this window: <what else moved>.\n            Predicted by <claim id>; outcome <held | partly | did not>.\n```\n\nThen update the original claim: a prediction that held is a **promotion**\ncandidate — behaviour now corroborates it, which is a second independent source\n([`frame-maintenance`](../frame-maintenance/references/verdicts.md)). One that\nfailed gets `contested` or retired, with the reason.\n\n### 6 — Say what it means for the belief\n\nOne paragraph, in the ticket and to the decision owner:\n\n> We believed **[claim]**. We shipped **[change]**. **[What happened]**. So the\n> belief is **[stronger / narrower / wrong]**, and the next thing worth knowing\n> is **[question]**.\n\nThat is the whole product. Everything above exists to let you write it honestly.\n\n## What this is not\n\nNot an experiment. Without a control you are reading a before and an after, and\nyou should say so. When the decision is big enough to need causality, that is an\nA/B test — and the honest version of this check says when it is out of its depth.\n\n## References\n\n- [references/measure.md](references/measure.md) — re-running the measure, windows, exposure\n",
"anti-sycophancy": "---\nname: anti-sycophancy\ndescription: >-\n  Challenges weak UX framing and agreeable drafts — flags thin evidence, feature-\n  as-goal, and one-sided marketplace claims. Use in Define (write-spec review),\n  critique of one-pagers, or when the user asks to pressure-test a problem statement.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# Anti-sycophancy (Define)\n\nAdversarial review for problem framing. Prefer hard questions over polished agreement.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Before publishing a `write-spec` one-pager\n- User asks “challenge this” / “what’s weak here?”\n- Soft language (“users struggle”) without Verified evidence\n\n## Checklist\n\n1. **Evidence** — Which claims are Verified? Which are AI interpretation? Any low-N or empty Dovetail matches?\n2. **Goal ≠ feature** — Rewrite feature names into customer/business outcomes.\n3. **Marketplace** — Brand and Partner both addressed, or explicit single-side caveat?\n4. **Disconfirming** — What would falsify this problem? Is that mentioned?\n5. **Scope honesty** — In/Out clear? Hidden solution baked into the problem statement?\n6. **Gate** — Ready for Designer sign-off + PM chat, or send back to Empathize?\n\n## Output\n\n```markdown\n# Pressure test — [title]\n\n## Keep\n- …\n\n## Challenge\n- …\n\n## Rewrite suggestions\n- Goal: …\n- Problem statement: …\n\n## Gate recommendation\n- [ ] Ready for Designer + PM chat\n- [ ] Revise framing\n- [ ] Back to Empathize (`user-research` / `research-synthesis`)\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use anti-sycophancy on this UPS Step 2 one-pager draft — what is too weak for Designer + PM review?`  \n   → Keeps volume; rejects empty Dovetail / off-topic Outset as triangulation; fieldwork before Ideate.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Softening criticism to be “helpful”\n- Inventing counter-evidence\n- Approving framing on single weak source\n",
"assumption-mapper": "---\nname: assumption-mapper\ndescription: >-\n  Pulls the untested assumptions out of a brief, PRD or pitch, checks each one\n  against what we already know, and ranks what is left by how much rests on it\n  versus how sure we are. Hands the scariest one to research-intake. Use when\n  someone says review this brief, what are we assuming here, what could sink\n  this, or before we build this what should we check.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  position: pre-entry\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  hands-off-to: research-intake\n---\n\n# Assumption mapper\n\nEvery brief rests on things nobody has checked. Most are fine. One or two will\nsink it.\n\nThis finds them, sorts the ones we already know the answer to from the ones we\ndo not, and ranks what is left by how much damage a wrong answer does.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## Why it sits in front of intake\n\n[`research-intake`](../research-intake/) starts when someone asks for research.\nThis starts earlier, with a brief nobody has questioned yet, and works out\nwhether there is a question worth asking at all.\n\n```\na brief, a PRD, a pitch\n        ↓\nassumption-mapper        what are we taking on faith?\n        ↓\n   check the Frame       do we already know any of this?\n        ↓\nresearch-intake          route the ones we don't\n```\n\nMost of what comes out of this is not a research request. That is the point. A\nmap where everything needs a study is a map nobody will use.\n\n## The rules that keep this honest\n\n1. **Check the Frame before you rank anything.** Half of what looks like an\n   assumption is a claim somebody already wrote down. Finding that is the\n   cheapest win here.\n2. **Nobody arguing with it is not the same as it being true.** An assumption\n   nobody has challenged usually got high confidence by going unnoticed.\n3. **Every assumption gets a way to check it**, or gets called what it is: a\n   risk we are carrying on purpose. Do not invent a plausible test for\n   something untestable.\n4. **Don't write \"do some research\" five times.** Some of these are a data\n   query, some are a conversation with one engineer, some are reading a\n   competitor's pricing page.\n5. **Name the scariest one.** Not the easiest to check. A map with no clear\n   top item has not done its job.\n\n## 1. Surface them, across four kinds\n\nPeople only ever write down the first kind, so go looking for the other three.\n\n| | Ask |\n|---|---|\n| **Do they want it?** | Does anybody actually have this problem, badly enough to change what they do? |\n| **Can we build it?** | Does the data exist, does the integration hold, can it work at the sizes we have? |\n| **Does it work for us?** | Does it make money or save it, does it fit how we sell, can we support it? |\n| **Can they use it?** | Can the person who has this problem actually get through it unaided? |\n\n**The dangerous ones are the ones too obvious to write down.** \"Partners want to\nbe found by brands\" sounds like a fact. It is an assumption, and the marketplace\nnumbers argue with it.\n\nKeep going until you have at least one of each kind, then read the brief again\nfor whichever kind is emptiest. It is empty because nobody thought about it,\nnot because there is nothing there.\n\n## 2. Check each one against what we know\n\nThis step is ours and it is the one generic versions skip.\n\nFor each assumption, ask [`user-context`](../user-context/) `ask`, or query the\nFrame directly. Four things can come back:\n\n| | Means | Do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **We know this** | A claim covers it, right segment, still current | Mark it settled, link the claim. It is not an assumption any more |\n| **We half know it** | Right area, wrong segment, or getting old | Note what is covered and what is not. The question just got smaller |\n| **We know we don't** | It is already a recorded gap | Say so, and say what was going to be done about it |\n| **Nothing** | Nobody has looked | Carry it to the ranking |\n\nA brief where six of ten assumptions come back settled is a good outcome for\neveryone, and it takes twenty minutes.\n\n## 3. Rank what is left\n\nTwo numbers each, both 1 to 5.\n\n**How much rests on it.** If this turns out false, does the plan need a tweak,\nor does it collapse?\n\n**How sure are we.** Be strict. 5 means we have evidence, with a source. 3 means\nit sounds right and nobody has checked. Tag where each one came from:\n**[evidence]** with a link, or **[hunch]**, honestly.\n\n**What it adds up to = rests on it − how sure.**\n\nAnything scoring 3 or more, or sitting at rests-on-it 4+ with confidence 2 or\nless, is a **load-bearing guess**. Say so in those words. It is the sentence\npeople remember.\n\n| Assumption | Kind | Rests on it | Sure | Score | Where it came from | How to check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nLeave the negative scores visible. An assumption we are very sure about that\nnothing much rests on is worth seeing, because it shows the map was not written\nto make a case.\n\n## 4. The cheapest way to check the top one\n\nFor the scariest assumption, name the cheapest thing that could prove it wrong\nthis week. Not the most thorough thing. The cheapest.\n\n| | Good for | Rough cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Query what we already have** | Anything about what people currently do | An hour |\n| **Read the Frame properly** | Anything we might have studied before | Twenty minutes |\n| **Ask one person who would know** | Can we build it, does it work for us | A conversation |\n| **A MICRO study** | Do they want it, can they use it | 48 hours |\n| **Look at what a competitor did** | Whether the approach is even viable | An afternoon |\n| **A fake door or a mock** | Would anyone click this at all | A few days |\n| **Check the schema** | Can we measure the thing we would need to measure | Minutes, via [`measurable-check`](../measurable-check/) |\n\nThen say what a fail looks like, concretely, before anyone runs it. \"If fewer\nthan two of eight have ever tried to do this, the assumption is dead.\"\n\n## 5. Hand it on\n\nSay explicitly which assumption the next step has to treat as open.\n\n- A load-bearing guess that needs people → [`research-intake`](../research-intake/),\n  with the assumption as the question and \"the plan collapses without it\" as the\n  decision\n- One that needs numbers → [`measurable-check`](../measurable-check/) first,\n  since it might not be measurable at all\n- One we cannot check → write it into the brief as a risk somebody owns, with\n  a name on it\n\nAn unresolved load-bearing guess goes into the brief as an open question. It\ndoes not quietly become a decision.\n\n## The output\n\n```markdown\n# What this brief is assuming: [name]\n[date] · [what you were given] · stage: [idea / discovery / pre-build / shipped]\n\n## The short version\n[Two or three lines. The one assumption that could sink this, and whether\nanything we already know speaks to it.]\n\n## Already answered\n[Assumption] → [claim id, confidence, n, link]. Not an assumption any more.\n\n## Already a known gap\n[Assumption] → recorded as [need/gap], [what was planned]\n\n## Still untested\n| Assumption | Kind | Rests on it | Sure | Score | From | How to check |\n\n## The load-bearing guesses\n**[the assumption, in plain words]**\nRests on it [n], sure [n]. [Where it came from.]\nCheapest check: [what, and roughly how long]\nA fail looks like: [concretely]\n\n## Risks we are carrying on purpose\n[Things that cannot be checked. Named, with an owner.]\n\n## What this map can't tell you\n[What you had to assume about the brief itself, anything you could not reach,\nand whether the Frame check was quick or thorough]\n```\n\n## Two ways this goes wrong\n\n**Everything becomes a research request.** Then nobody runs it twice. Most\nassumptions are fine, some are answered already, and only one or two are worth\nanyone's week.\n\n**It only finds the comfortable ones.** If the top of the map is something the\nteam was already planning to check, go back and read the brief again. The\nassumption that scares people tends not to make it onto the page.\n",
"brand-operator-ux-persona": "---\nname: brand-operator-ux-persona\ndescription: >-\n  Applies impact.com Brand-side UX persona and JTBD lenses (operators, ACMs,\n  marketing directors) when framing problems or reviewing specs. Use in Define\n  write-spec, Ideate critique, or Brand UI questions.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  audience: brand\n---\n\n# Brand operator UX persona (Define)\n\nLens for **Brand** advertisers and operators — not Partners.\n\n## Sources (prefer live)\n\n1. [Brand UI Personas (JTBD)](https://impact.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ux/pages/6439338009/Brand+UI+Personas+Jobs-to-be-Done)\n2. [UX Design & Research](https://impact.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ux/overview) hub\n3. Research Insight Monitor / Dovetail Brand themes\n4. Fetch Confluence with Atlassian Rovo MCP (`getConfluencePage` / `search`) yourself — do not invent persona names, and do not ask the designer to paste the page\n\n## How to apply\n\n1. Name the Brand role(s) in scope (e.g. Affiliate Creator Manager, Marketing Director/Analyst, Agency Lead).\n2. State primary JTBD and daily cadence frictions (briefing, partners, campaigns, analysis, finance).\n3. Ask: does this problem block a Brand job, or only a Partner job?\n4. Return 3–5 implications for the one-pager (audience, success criteria, risks).\n\n## Output\n\n```markdown\n# Brand lens — [topic]\nPersonas in scope: …\nJTBD affected: …\nVerified pain (links): …\nImplications for framing: …\nGaps / do not assume: …\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Speaking for Partners without the creator-affiliate skill\n- Using outdated Estalea-only persona docs without noting age\n",
"brand-prototype": "---\nname: brand-prototype\ndescription: >-\n  Auto-sets up the impact.com Brand UI prototype shell: clones the public\n  Brand template, installs Node if missing plus npm/Vite deps, starts Vite.\n  Use when the user says brand-prototype, Brand UI shell, or Brand prototype\n  in Cursor / Claude Code / Copilot. In Figma Make or Claude chat, use\n  ux-prototyping (those harnesses run a live preview without this script).\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: prototype\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Prototype Scaffolder\n---\n\n# Brand prototype (shell bootstrap)\n\n**One invocation → running Brand UI shell.** Do not ask the designer to clone,\ninstall Node, or run `npm install`. The bootstrap script does that.\n\nThis skill **only** bootstraps the shell. Building mocks into screens is a\nfollow-on step in the same Cursor chat after READY.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Run the bootstrap script** — do not improvise `mkdir` / `git clone` / `npm`\n   steps by hand unless the script is missing.\n2. Templates are **Vite + React** (Figma Make–origin bundles), **not** Next.js.\n3. In Figma Make or Claude chat (no shell), do **not** run this script —\n   invoke `ux-prototyping` instead (live preview in that harness).\n4. After READY, move the Cursor workspace to the project path when possible.\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use `brand-prototype`” / `/brand-prototype`\n- Designer ready to prototype Brand-side after mocks / `prototype-handoff`\n- Need the real Brand chrome + Styleguide-aligned shell locally\n\n## Hard requirements (agent must check)\n\n| Need | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Shell / terminal | Script runs locally (Cursor or Claude Code) |\n| `git` | Clone the public template |\n| Network | GitHub + npm registry (+ Node download if missing) |\n\n**Node 18+ and npm are auto-provisioned** when missing (local copy under\n`~/Documents/impact-prototypes/.toolchain`, no sudo). Only `git` is a\nhard machine prerequisite (Xcode CLT on macOS).\n\nIf git is missing on a **shell** harness → stop with a short git/Xcode note.\nIf this is Figma Make or Claude chat → invoke `ux-prototyping` (Path B / C).\nDo not hand the designer a git/npm checklist.\n\n## Workflow (Cursor / Claude Code)\n\n1. **Locate script** —  \n   `.agents/skills/brand-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh`  \n   (or `.cursor/skills/brand-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh` via symlink).\n2. **Run it with the sandbox off.** In Cursor Shell, pass\n   `required_permissions: [\"all\"]` (network + unsandboxed npm). Default\n   sandbox **cannot** `npm install` or start Vite — that is why other\n   designers see “packages missing.”\n\n   ```bash\n   bash \"/absolute/path/to/brand-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh\"\n   ```\n\n   Optional flags: `--no-dev` (skip Vite), `--reclone` (wipe + fresh clone),  \n   `--parent ~/other/folder`.\n3. **Parse READY** — look for `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY shell=brand path=… url=…`.\n4. **Cursor only** — call `move_agent_to_root` with that `path` so the designer\n   is inside the prototype project.\n5. **Confirm** — tell the designer the folder path + open the printed URL\n   (Brand default `http://localhost:5174/one-program-1/` — template uses a Vite base path).\n6. **Stop** — shell is ready. Wait for mock links / “build this flow” before\n   editing UI. Point Styleguide work at `design-system`.\n\nDefault install location:\n\n`~/Documents/impact-prototypes/brand-ui`\n\nOverride with `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_HOME` or `--parent`.\n\n## Harness matrix\n\nSee [references/harnesses.md](references/harnesses.md).\n\n| Harness | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| **Cursor** | Run script → `move_agent_to_root` → confirm URL. |\n| **Claude Code / Copilot** | Run script → open the path → confirm URL. |\n| **Figma Make / Claude chat** | Do not run this script. Use `ux-prototyping` (preview in that harness). |\n\n## Template facts (do not invent)\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Repo | https://github.com/lesley-spec/one-program-1.git (public) |\n| Stack | Vite · React · `npm run dev` |\n| Source design | https://www.figma.com/design/rwAqBrLfwQy8cLyfi2tm3Y/Brand-UI---Impact.com |\n| Entry | `index.html` + `src/` |\n| Note | Repo is **large**; first clone can take several minutes. Say so up front. |\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use brand-prototype — set up the Brand shell for me.`\n2. `Use brand-prototype after prototype-handoff; then we'll drop in these Figma mocks: [link].`\n3. `Brand prototype isn't running — re-run brand-prototype with a clean setup.`  \n   → run bootstrap with `--reclone`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Pasting multi-step git/npm instructions for the designer to run manually when the script exists\n- Asking the designer to install Node, Homebrew, or `npm install` themselves\n- Describing a Next.js / `src/pages` layout that this template does not have\n- Claiming this bootstrap ran inside Figma Make or Claude chat (use `ux-prototyping`)\n- Editing product UI before bootstrap prints READY\n- Blocking on “where should I put the folder?” — use the default parent unless they specify\n- Scaffolding a new Vite app instead of this Brand shell\n\n## Success criteria\n\n- [ ] Script exited 0\n- [ ] `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY` printed with path + url\n- [ ] Designer can open the URL and see Brand UI chrome\n- [ ] (Cursor) Workspace root is the prototype folder\n",
"competitive-analysis": "---\nname: competitive-analysis\ndescription: >-\n  Looks at how the other platforms actually handle a job our users have to do,\n  using Klue and the weekly competitor feed, and says what it means for us\n  without pretending a competitor's feature is evidence our users want it. Use\n  when someone says what does Partnerize do here, how do competitors handle\n  this, are we behind on this, or check the competitor feed.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  sources: klue, competitor-weekly-feed, g2, the-frame\n---\n\n# Competitive analysis\n\nWhat do the other platforms do about the job our users are stuck on, and does\nany of it matter to us?\n\nBoth halves are the work. The first half is research anyone can do. The second\nhalf is the one that gets skipped, and skipping it is how a roadmap fills up\nwith other people's features.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## The rule this exists for\n\n**A competitor shipping something is not evidence our users want it.**\n\nIt is evidence that a team somewhere decided it was worth building. They may\nhave been right. They may have been copying someone else who was guessing.\n\nSo everything this produces starts as **UNVALIDATED**, whatever else it has\ngoing for it. It becomes a claim when one of our users says the underlying\nproblem is real, and not before.\n\nThat is not caution for its own sake. Half of what a competitor ships gets\nquietly removed within two years, and you cannot tell which half from the\noutside.\n\n## Where to look\n\n### Klue, first\n\nKlue is where the competitive intelligence lives: battlecards, win and loss\nnotes, pricing, positioning, and what sales is hearing in deals right now.\n\nStart there rather than with a competitor's website, because Klue carries the\npart a website never will, which is what happened when someone tried to sell\nagainst them.\n\n**Ask it for:** the battlecard on the competitor, anything recent on the area\nyou are looking at, and the win and loss notes that mention it. Win and loss\nnotes are the most useful thing in the building for this and the least read.\n\n> If the Klue connector is not authorised in your session, say so and ask for it\n> rather than working around it with web searches. A competitor's marketing site\n> and Klue tell you different things, and the marketing site is the one that is\n> trying to persuade you.\n\n### The weekly competitor feed\n\n> **https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/feed/**\n\nOur own DesignOps site runs a competitor feed that refreshes every Monday, built\nfrom the competitors' own changelogs and blogs plus a filtered news sweep. It\ncurrently covers **Awin, CJ Affiliate, Rakuten Advertising, Partnerize,\nShareASale, Pepperjam and Refersion**.\n\nUse it for *what shipped lately*, which is the question Klue answers least well.\nYou can filter it by features, news, or both, and by week.\n\n**It is signed in, restricted to @impact.com Google accounts**, so an agent\ncannot fetch it on your behalf. Open it yourself and paste in what matters, or\ndrive it in a browser you are signed into. Do not substitute a web search and\npresent it as the feed. If you could not get in, say so in the write-up.\n\nIf a competitor you care about is not on that list, that is a decision for\nwhoever runs the feed, not something to quietly work around.\n\n### G2, and our own Frame\n\nG2 carries what customers say about the others, which is closer to use than any\nfeature list. We already hold 158 brand reviews there.\n\nAnd check [`user-context`](../user-context/) before you start. Sometimes we\nalready know what our users do about this, which turns a feature comparison\ninto a much sharper question.\n\n## How to do it\n\n### 1. Start from a job, not a feature list\n\n> Not \"compare reporting across platforms.\"\n> Instead: \"a brand manager needs to work out which partners are worth keeping.\n> How does each platform let them do that, and where does ours make it harder?\"\n\nA feature-by-feature grid tells you who has more boxes ticked. A job tells you\nwho makes the work easier, which is the only question with an answer worth\nacting on.\n\nTake the job from the Frame if we have one. Our unmet needs are already written\nthis way.\n\n### 2. Follow it the whole way through\n\nCompare the journey, not the screen. Most of the difference between platforms\nis in the handoffs: what you have to remember between steps, how many places\nyou go, what happens when something fails.\n\nFor each platform, for that job:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Can you do it at all?** | Fully, partly, with a workaround, or not |\n| **How much work is it** | Steps, screens, places you have to go |\n| **What they assume** | Who they think is doing this, and how often |\n| **Anything genuinely different** | An approach nobody else took |\n\nNote where you could not look. Most of these platforms do not let you in\nwithout an account, so a lot of this is documentation, screenshots and what\nKlue holds. **Say which it was.** \"From their help docs\" and \"I used it\" are\nvery different claims, and a reader deserves to know which one they are getting.\n\n### 3. Sort what you find into four piles\n\n| | Means | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Everyone has it** | Table stakes. Missing it is a reason we lose deals | Check against win and loss notes in Klue. If it shows up there, it is real |\n| **Someone solved it better** | A genuinely nicer answer to a job our users have | Worth understanding. Not worth copying until a user says the problem is real |\n| **Nobody does it** | Either an opening, or something everyone tried and dropped | Treat as an opening only if our evidence says the need exists |\n| **We do it better** | Worth knowing, and worth telling sales | Hand to Klue rather than burying it in a research doc |\n\nThe third pile is where people get excited and where most bad roadmap items\ncome from. An empty space in a market usually got that way for a reason.\n\n### 4. Say what it means for us, honestly\n\nThe only section anyone needs to read, and the one that takes the judgement.\n\nFor each finding, answer: **do our users have this problem?**\n\n- **Yes, and we have evidence** → link the claim. Now it is a real gap, and\n  the competitor is just confirmation\n- **Yes, probably, but nothing written down** → this is a research question.\n  Route it through [`research-intake`](../research-intake/), with the\n  competitive finding as context rather than as the reason\n- **No idea** → say that. It is the honest answer most of the time\n- **No, our evidence says otherwise** → the most valuable line in the document.\n  Write it down, because somebody will raise this feature again in six months\n\n## The write-up\n\nKeep it short. Nobody reads a long competitor document twice.\n\n```markdown\n# [The job], across the platforms\n[date] · Klue [✅ / not reachable] · Feed [✅ / signed out] · G2 [✅] · Frame [✅]\n\n## The short version\n[Three or four lines. What the others do, and the one thing that actually\nmatters for us. Not a list.]\n\n## The job\n[Whose job, what they are trying to get done, and which of our claims or\nunmet needs it maps to]\n\n## How each one handles it\n| Platform | Can you do it | How much work | Their angle | How I know |\n\n## What it means for us\n**Table stakes we're missing:** … [with the Klue win/loss note, if there is one]\n**Someone's solved this better:** … [and whether our users have the problem]\n**Nobody does this:** … [and whether that is an opening or a warning]\n**We're ahead here:** … [send to Klue]\n\n## What our own evidence says\n[For each finding: do our users actually have this problem? Claim ids and\nconfidence, or \"nothing written down\", honestly]\n\n## What this can't tell you\n[Which platforms you could not get into. What came from docs rather than use.\nHow old the feed entries were. Everything here is UNVALIDATED until a user\nsays the problem is real.]\n```\n\n## Keeping it from going stale\n\nA competitive analysis is true of the week it was written. It does not get\nre-verified like a claim does, because the thing it describes keeps moving.\n\n**Put a date on it and let it expire.** Six months old is a historical\ndocument, not a current one. The Monday feed is the thing that stays current;\nthis is a snapshot taken for a decision.\n\nIf a finding here turns into something we act on, it gets recorded as a claim\nthrough [`user-context`](../user-context/) `record` with the user evidence\nattached, not the competitor evidence. \"Partnerize does this\" is not a claim\nabout our users and never becomes one.\n\n## What this is not\n\n**Not a reason to build something.** It is context for a decision somebody else\nmakes on evidence about our own users.\n\n**Not a feature parity checklist.** Chasing parity means your roadmap is set by\nwhoever ships fastest, and it is how products end up with everything and no\npoint of view.\n\n**Not a review of their design quality.** If you want to know whether an\ninterface works, [`heuristic-evaluation`](../heuristic-evaluation/) does that,\nand it works just as well on somebody else's product as on ours.\n",
"creator-affiliate-ux-persona": "---\nname: creator-affiliate-ux-persona\ndescription: >-\n  Applies impact.com Partner / creator-affiliate UX persona lenses when framing\n  problems or reviewing specs. Use in Define write-spec, Ideate critique, or\n  Partner-side experience questions.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  audience: partner\n---\n\n# Creator-affiliate UX persona (Define)\n\nLens for **Partner** side: creators, affiliates, publishers — not Brand operators.\n\n## Sources (prefer live)\n\n1. [Partner Personas](https://impact.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ux/pages/1400766526/Partner+Personas) (Novice / Intermediate / Expert)\n2. Creator Affiliate product narrative / primers in Confluence (search Atlassian)\n3. Research Insight Monitor Partner themes (Link & Content Tools, proposals, finance)\n4. Fetch live pages via Atlassian Rovo MCP (`getConfluencePage` / `search`) yourself — do not invent quotes or maturity levels, and do not ask the designer to paste the page\n\n## How to apply\n\n1. Name Partner segment (creator vs classic affiliate/publisher) and maturity if known.\n2. Map JTBD: discover brands, generate links, proposals/campaigns, payouts, content tools.\n3. Ask: does this problem block Partner earning/activation, or only Brand ops?\n4. Return 3–5 implications for the one-pager.\n\n## Output\n\n```markdown\n# Partner / creator-affiliate lens — [topic]\nSegment: …\nJTBD affected: …\nVerified pain (links): …\nImplications for framing: …\nGaps / do not assume: …\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Collapsing all Partners into “creators”\n- Speaking for Brand operators without `brand-operator-ux-persona`\n",
"data-analyze": "---\nname: data-analyze\ndescription: >-\n  Runs ad-hoc PostHog and Pendo analysis for impact.com UX Empathize questions\n  (funnels, drop-offs, trends, polls). Use when designers ask quantitative\n  product questions in Cursor, Claude, or any MCP-enabled harness. Alias: data:analyze.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-alias: data:analyze\n---\n\n# Data analyze (Empathize)\n\nAd-hoc quantitative analysis. Skill id is `data-analyze`.\n\n## Integrity\n\n- Query live tools; do not invent series.\n- State window, filters (especially filter-test-accounts), and math definition.\n- Metrics are **Verified**; “why” text is **AI interpretation** until qual agrees.\n- Severity heuristic (Research Insight Monitor): HIGH drop-off >30%, MEDIUM 15–30% — say so explicitly.\n- **Quality gate:** If step-from count is tiny (e.g. <30 persons) or drop is 100% with ~0 completing, label **low sample / monitor only** — do not treat as a confirmed problem.\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md) and [references/query-playbook.md](references/query-playbook.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- “What’s the drop-off between X and Y?”\n- Validate or challenge a Research Insight Monitor funnel claim with live PostHog\n- Compare MLW / UPS / Login / PublisherPro volumes before a human gate\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Clarify the question** — Metric, segment, window, comparison.\n2. **Schema first (PostHog)** — `read-data-schema` for events; do not assume names.\n3. **Choose tool** — `query-funnel` / `query-trends` / Pendo usage or polls.\n4. **Execute minimal query** — Only needed series.\n5. **Report** using the template; flag low-N and instrumentation risks.\n6. **Bridge** — Qual next → `research-synthesis` or `user-research`.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Analysis — [question]\n\n## Answer (Verified)\n- Result: …\n- Definition: …\n- Window / filters: …\n- Link / tool: …\n\n## Caveats\n- Sample size / instrumentation: …\n- Compared to monitor report: agree | disagree | report may be stale\n\n## AI interpretation\n- …\n\n## Action for designer\n- [ ] Ready for human gate discussion (volume + clear drop)\n- [ ] Monitor only (low N or instrumentation suspect)\n- [ ] Next: `research-synthesis` | `user-research`\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use data-analyze: what's the drop-off from MLW slideout open → link create API → copy button in the last 90 days?`\n2. `Use data-analyze: validate PublisherPro manage-links open_linkgen_slideout → affiliate_link_generated. Is the monitor's 100% drop real?`\n3. `Use data-analyze: how many partners open UPS details vs open send-proposal modal?`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Treating 100% drop with <30 starters as a product crisis\n- Comparing PostHog persons to Pendo accounts without a conversion note\n- Answering “why” from quant alone as fact\n",
"design-system": "---\nname: design-system\ndescription: >-\n  Points Define/Ideate/Prototype work at impact.com UX styleguide and component\n  library constraints. Use when writing specs that mention UI, checking\n  tokens/components, before on-brand Ideate mocks, or Prototype handoff.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# Design system (Define / Ideate)\n\nLightweight routing skill — does not invent a parallel system.\n\n## Canonical sources\n\n| Resource | Link |\n|---|---|\n| Styleguide (Figma) | https://www.figma.com/file/n3L9jQqoKCgM7Jiems9bQH/Styleguide |\n| Component library | https://impact.atlassian.net/l/c/yEv0GqPN |\n| UX hub | https://impact.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ux/overview |\n\n## When to use\n\n- One-pager mentions UI patterns, density, navigation, or components\n- Spec should call out reuse vs new pattern\n- Before on-brand Ideate mocks (Figma Make / Cursor) or Prototype scaffolder handoff\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Open/search styleguide + component library (Figma MCP / Confluence) for existing patterns.\n2. In the one-pager **Scope** or **Design deliverables**, note: reuse X / gap Y.\n3. Do not invent tokens or components; flag gaps for design system owners.\n\n## Output snippet for write-spec\n\n```markdown\n## Design system notes\n- Reuse: …\n- Gaps / new patterns (needs DS review): …\n- Figma / library links: …\n```\n",
"design-thinking-ux": "---\nname: design-thinking-ux\ndescription: >-\n  Master orchestrator for the impact.com UX Design Thinking pipeline. Walks a\n  designer Empathize → Define → Ideate → Prototype → Test: asks intake\n  questions, invokes the phase skills (research, write-spec, tickets, ideate,\n  prototype shells, test-plan), and stops at each human gate. Use when the user\n  says Design Thinking UX, design-thinking-ux, start the pipeline, take me\n  through design thinking, walk me through Empathize to Test, or wants a guided\n  run from research through usability testing.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: pipeline\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Design Thinking Pipeline\n---\n\n# Design Thinking UX (master)\n\nOn-demand **pipeline orchestrator**. First skill to load when a designer wants\nthe full run. This skill does **not** replace phase skills — it asks questions,\n**reads and runs** the skill for the current step, then invites the next phase.\n\nDoes **not** skip human gates. Designer confirms “problem worth solving,”\npublishing, chosen direction, and recruiting.\n\nRouting tables: [references/phases.md](references/phases.md).\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Orchestrate, don’t rewrite.** When this skill says invoke `skill-name`,\n   Read `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (fallback `.cursor/skills/<name>/`)\n   plus any references that step needs, then execute that skill’s workflow in\n   **this chat**. Do not ask the designer to type `use skill-name`.\n2. **One child skill at a time** unless that skill itself calls another\n   (e.g. `test-plan` → `ux-critique` + `accessibility-review`).\n3. **Honor the child’s stop.** Jira / Confluence / Outset drafts only\n   when that skill allows it — after designer confirm. Never launch Outset\n   recruitment or Maze unless the designer asks.\n4. **Label Verified vs AI interpretation.** Never invent metrics or quotes.\n5. **Join anywhere.** If artifacts already exist (research, one-pager, epic,\n   mocks, prototype), start at that phase. Do not force Empathize from scratch.\n6. **Do not auto-advance.** After a phase gate, update the board, **entice**\n   the next phase, and wait. “Keep going” still stops at the next gate.\n7. **Prototype shells only.** In Prototype, invoke `ux-prototyping` in\n   **this** harness (Cursor, Claude, Figma Make, Copilot). Brand or Partner\n   shell only — live preview here (localhost, Make, or Claude). After READY,\n   `design-system` may map mocks. Never scaffold Vite/Next/HTML from scratch.\n   Never send the designer to another tool to finish Prototype.\n8. **Atlassian Rovo MCP.** Fetch and publish Jira/Confluence with the\n   Atlassian Rovo connector yourself (`getJiraIssue`, `search`,\n   `createJiraIssue`, …). Do not wait for the designer to say it is on,\n   and do not ask them to paste a ticket or run `/mcp` until a tool call\n   fails. See [references/atlassian-rovo.md](references/atlassian-rovo.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use Design Thinking UX” / `design-thinking-ux` / “start the pipeline”\n- Designer wants a guided Empathize → Test run\n- Unclear which phase skill to start with\n\nIf they name a **single** phase skill (`write-spec`, `ideate`, `ux-prototyping`,\n`test-plan`), run that skill — do not wrap it in this orchestrator unless they\nalso asked for the full pipeline.\n\n## Session start (always)\n\n0. If sibling skills are missing (`write-spec`, `ideate`, `ux-prototyping`,\n   `test-plan`), invoke `ux-install-all-skills` first, then continue.\n1. Show the **pipeline board** (template below). Fill what you already know.\n2. **Intake** — batch these (do not drip one-by-one):\n\n   - What are you working on? (flow, product area, hunch, epic, ticket)\n   - Audience: **Brand** / **Partner** / **Shared**?\n   - Where are you in the pipeline? (start at Empathize, or jump)\n   - What do you already have? Research, one-pager/PRD, epic, mocks, prototype, test results — paste or link\n   - If Empathize: do you have research, or should I pull / synthesize / plan a study?\n\n   If they named a Jira key, **fetch it now** via Atlassian Rovo MCP\n   (`getJiraIssue`) before asking them to paste title/description. Fill\n   the board from the issue. See [references/atlassian-rovo.md](references/atlassian-rovo.md).\n\n3. Locate the phase using [references/phases.md](references/phases.md).\n4. Confirm the landing phase in one sentence, then run that phase.\n\nResume mid-chat from the last open gate + artifacts — don’t re-ask intake.\n\n## Phase loop\n\nFor the current phase:\n\n1. Ask only the **phase questions** still unanswered (see below).\n2. **Invoke** the child skill(s) from [references/phases.md](references/phases.md).\n3. Return the child’s output.\n4. Update the pipeline board.\n5. **Gate** — if the child says send back a phase, do that; don’t entice forward.\n6. **Entice** — when the gate is passed, offer the next phase in one short\n   prompt (what you’ll invoke, what they must confirm). Wait for yes.\n\n### Empathize\n\nAsk:\n\n- Existing research? (PostHog, Pendo, Dovetail, Outset, Freshdesk, pack, paste)\n- If yes: links / paste — synthesize; don’t redo fieldwork unless gaps remain\n- If no: Frame check (`user-context` / `ux-research`)? pull live quant\n  (`data-analyze`)? triangulate (`research-synthesis`)? affinity-map one\n  corpus (`synthesize-research`)? plan a study (`user-research` /\n  `research-planner`)?\n- What decision must this unlock?\n\n**Gate:** Designer confirms **problem worth solving** (or explicit override /\nmonitor-only). Then entice Define: one-pager + UX epic on their confirm.\n\n### Define\n\nAsk (if missing): one-pager vs PM-facing PRD depth; Confluence parent; Jira\nproject (`UX` default).\n\nInvoke `write-spec` (personas + `anti-sycophancy` + `design-system` per that\nskill). Draft first; create Confluence/Jira only after confirm.\n\n**Gate:** Designer happy with framing, then **PM chat** before Ideate. Entice\nIdeate after they say the PM chat happened (or they explicitly skip).\n\n### Ideate\n\nAsk: fidelity (sketches / wires / hi-fi); surface; how many directions\n(default 3–5); DS-bound vs exploratory.\n\nInvoke `ideate` → designer iterates → `ux-critique` → on chosen direction\n`prototype-handoff`.\n\n**Gate:** Designer names the direction; critique has no open Critical (or\naccepted residual risk). Entice Prototype: Brand vs Partner shell.\n\n### Prototype\n\n**Exclusive shells.** Invoke `ux-prototyping` first — it stays in this\nharness, then runs Brand or Partner only. See\n[references/phases.md](references/phases.md). Route from intake Audience\n(do not re-ask if already Brand or Partner):\n\n- Brand → `ux-prototyping` → Brand shell\n- Partner / creator-affiliate → `ux-prototyping` → Partner shell\n- Shared → ask which shell first; the other can run later\n\nShell harnesses: `ensure-harness.sh` + `bootstrap.sh` (sandbox off). Figma\nMake and Claude chat: live preview in that harness (do **not** bounce to\nCursor). Do not ask the designer to install Node or run `npm install`.\n\nAfter READY, ask if they have a GitHub account (publish to **their** user,\nnot the template account). Then `design-system` if mapping mocks to UI.\n\n**Gate:** Designer has a runnable preview (localhost, Make, or Claude) of\nthe Brand or Partner shell. Entice Test: pre-flight + Outset/Maze draft.\n\n### Test\n\nAsk: prototype URL; Outset vs Maze vs recommend; sessions already done?\n\n- No sessions → invoke `test-plan` (it runs pre-flight).\n- Sessions done → `synthesize-research` then `findings-publish`.\n\n**Gate:** Designer reviews the study before recruiting; reviews loopback\nbefore Confluence/Jira. Pipeline **ends** at published loopback (or a\nready-to-publish test plan if they stop before sessions). Offer a new\nEmpathize cycle only if findings reframe the problem.\n\n## Pipeline board (keep updated)\n\n```markdown\n# Design Thinking UX — [title]\nAudience: Brand | Partner | Shared\nPhase: Empathize | Define | Ideate | Prototype | Test\nStatus: INTAKE | IN PHASE | GATE | READY FOR NEXT\n\n## Board\n| Phase | Status | Artifacts | Gate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Empathize | todo \\| doing \\| done \\| skipped | … | problem worth solving? |\n| Define | todo \\| doing \\| done \\| skipped | … | Designer + PM? |\n| Ideate | todo \\| doing \\| done \\| skipped | … | critique + chosen direction? |\n| Prototype | todo \\| doing \\| done \\| skipped | … | runnable URL? |\n| Test | todo \\| doing \\| done | … | study reviewed / loopback? |\n\n## Invoked this session\n- `skill-name` — why\n\n## Next\n- Offer: [next phase / child skill]\n- Waiting on designer: [confirm / links / PM chat / sessions]\n```\n\nShow the board at start, after each child skill, and at each gate.\n\n## Entice lines (adapt, don’t copy blindly)\n\n- Empathize → Define: “You confirmed this is worth solving. Define next — I’ll draft the one-pager and, if you confirm, create the Confluence page and UX epic. Ready?”\n- Define → Ideate: “Framing is signed off. After your PM chat, I can start Ideate (brief + directions). Say the word.”\n- Ideate → Prototype: “Direction is chosen and critique is clear. I can bootstrap the Brand or Partner prototype shell. Which surface?”\n- Prototype → Test: “Prototype is up. I can run a11y + coverage pre-flight and draft an Outset or Maze study. If you confirm, I’ll create an Outset draft via MCP — not a live recruit. Go?”\n- Test complete: “Loopback is the end of this run. Findings can start a new Empathize cycle if the problem shifted.”\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use Design Thinking UX — I'm starting from scratch on Partner UPS Step 2. I don't have research yet.`  \n   → Intake → Empathize skills → gate → offer Define (`write-spec` + epic).\n2. `Use design-thinking-ux. We already have a one-pager and UX-1234. Jump to Ideate.`  \n   → Skip Empathize/Define; invoke `ideate`.\n3. `Use Design Thinking UX. Prototype is at localhost:5173 — take me through Test.`  \n   → Invoke `test-plan` (pre-flight + study draft). On confirm, create an Outset draft via MCP. Stop before recruit.\n\n## Check the orchestrator\n\nFrom repo root: `python3 .agents/skills/design-thinking-ux/scripts/validate.py`\n\nConfirms every child `SKILL.md` loads, markdown links resolve, and the UPS Step 2 Empathize → Test invoke sequence still matches this skill.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Reimplementing a child skill instead of reading its `SKILL.md`\n- Asking the designer to manually invoke the next skill\n- Auto-creating Jira/Confluence without confirm, or launching Outset/Maze recruitment\n- Asking the designer to paste a Jira issue, run `/mcp`, or turn on Atlassian Rovo before you call the MCP tools yourself\n- Jumping to mocks before Empathize gate + Define sign-off (unless they joined mid-pipeline)\n- Running Empathize → Test in one turn with no gates\n- Treating synthetic pre-tests as session findings\n- Scaffolding a from-scratch Vite/Next app instead of `ux-prototyping`\n  (Brand/Partner shell in **this** harness: Cursor, Claude, or Figma Make)\n- Sending a Make or Claude designer to Cursor to finish Prototype\n",
"findings-publish": "---\nname: findings-publish\ndescription: >-\n  Takes a finished study and turns it into the things people actually read. It\n  pulls together what Outset, Pendo and PostHog hold, writes a Dovetail report\n  with a TL;DR, key insights and deep dives, drafts a short Slack post, puts a\n  page wherever you point it in Confluence, and suggests Jira tickets worth\n  raising. Use when someone says write this up, publish the findings, share what\n  we learned, or loop this back to the squad.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  supersedes: test-loopback, dovetail-report\n---\n\n# Findings publish\n\nA study has finished. This turns it into four things: a report in Dovetail, a\nshort post for Slack, a Confluence page where you want it, and a list of tickets\nworth raising.\n\nIt drafts all four and creates nothing until you say so.\n\n> Replaces `test-loopback` in the DesignOps pack and the old `dovetail-report`\n> skill. Retire both rather than running them alongside this.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## How to write\n\nLead with what people said and how they felt about it. The numbers come after,\nto show how common something was, not to open with.\n\nPlain words. Short sentences. If a sentence needs a second read, cut it in half.\n\nSay what you do not know. A report that admits its gaps gets trusted. One that\npapers over them gets quoted once and then quietly doubted.\n\nNever invent a quote, a number or a participant. If you cannot find something,\nsay you could not find it.\n\n## What to gather\n\n| Where | What you are after |\n|---|---|\n| **Outset** | The interviews. Transcripts, topline, the things people actually said |\n| **Dovetail** | Earlier work on this. Highlights someone already marked |\n| **Pendo** | In-product responses. Bigger samples, thinner answers |\n| **PostHog** | Whether the behaviour matches what people told you |\n| **The Frame** | What we already believed, so you can say whether it held |\n\nStart with the interviews. The quant tells you how often, never why, and a\nreport built from numbers first reads like a dashboard with sentences.\n\n**Say what you could not get to.** If Pendo was empty or PostHog had no event\nfor this, that belongs in the report, not left out quietly.\n\n## 1. The Dovetail report\n\nThree layers, in this order.\n\n### TL;DR\n\nFive or six lines. What we asked, what we found, what it means for the decision.\nSomeone should be able to read only this and not be misled.\n\n### Key insights\n\nThree to five. Any more and nothing stands out.\n\nEach one opens with the finding in plain words, then a quote that shows it, then\nhow common it was and what contradicts it.\n\n```\n**Partners give up on the assets page rather than ask for help.**\n\n> \"I just gave up and emailed my contact. Took two days but at least\n>  I knew it'd get done.\" P7\n\nSix of eleven did something similar. Two found it first time and were\nsurprised anyone struggled, both of them daily users.\n```\n\nThe disconfirming line is not optional. If you looked and found nothing against\nit, say that instead.\n\n### Deep dives\n\nOne section per insight for people who want the detail. Transcript quotes with\ntimestamps, the behavioural read if there is one, what you are still unsure\nabout.\n\nThis is where numbers belong. Keep them out of the top two layers unless the\nnumber is the finding.\n\nWrite it with `create_doc` into the project the evidence sits in, after you have\nshown the draft.\n\n## 2. The Slack post\n\nShort, business casual, no jargon. Under 150 words.\n\n```\nWe spent two weeks with partners on the assets page.\n\nShort version: most people can't find it, and they don't ask. They work\naround it or email their contact instead.\n\nA few things stood out:\n• Six of eleven gave up before finding what they came for\n• The two who managed it were daily users who'd learned where things live\n• Nobody we spoke to had used search\n\nWorth saying: small group, eleven people, all partners. We didn't talk to\nanyone on the brand side.\n\nFull write-up: [link]\n```\n\nSay the sample size and who you did not speak to. That one line is the\ndifference between a post people trust and one that gets over-read.\n\nNothing at low confidence goes in here. In a channel it gets screenshotted\nwithout the caveat.\n\n## 3. The Confluence page\n\n**Ask where it goes.** Do not guess at a space. If the person has not said,\nask, and offer what `getConfluenceSpaces` returns.\n\nThe page carries the report plus the bits Dovetail does not hold well: who\ndecided what, what happens next, and which beliefs this changed.\n\n## 4. Jira tickets\n\nSuggest them. Do not raise them without a yes.\n\nMost findings are not tickets. A finding becomes one when someone has decided to\ndo something about it.\n\n| Finding | Ticket? |\n|---|---|\n| Blocks people, and the owner agreed to fix it | Yes |\n| We cannot measure something we need to measure | Yes, to whoever owns that surface |\n| Real, but nobody has decided anything | No. Leave it in the report |\n| Thin evidence | No. Suggest more research instead |\n| Two readings disagree | No. Settle it first |\n\nA backlog full of tickets nobody asked for teaches a squad to ignore research.\n\nEach suggestion says what would change, who agreed, and what the evidence was.\n\n## Before you create anything\n\nShow all four drafts together. Say which findings you deliberately left out of\nthe Slack post and which you chose not to ticket. That second list is usually\nthe one worth arguing about.\n\n## After\n\nClose the original request, link everything together, and record what you\nlearned in the Frame through [`user-context`](../user-context/).\n\nIf the study was testing something we already believed, say plainly whether it\nheld up, got narrower, or turned out wrong. If anything shipped, hand it to\n[`adoption-check`](../adoption-check/) so someone looks again in a month.\n",
"frame-maintenance": "---\nname: frame-maintenance\ndescription: >-\n  Staleness check and rebuild plan for the Frame. Re-reads every claim against\n  Dovetail and PostHog, says what to retire, what to refresh, and what to\n  research next, ranked by what a decision currently rests on. Run it quarterly,\n  or when someone asks whether the Frame can still be trusted. Use when the user\n  says frame maintenance, staleness check, what should we retire, is the Frame\n  stale, what should we research next, or rebuild the Frame.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  cadence: quarterly\n  frame: https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/frame/\n  frame_claude: https://claude.ai/artifact/FHc7oAPoyohgxo87VdxCGw\n---\n\n# Frame maintenance\n\nA Frame nobody maintains **degrades silently while still looking\nauthoritative** — which is worse than having none, because decisions keep being\nmade against it. This is the ritual that stops that.\n\nRun it quarterly. It takes an hour or two, most of it reading.\n\n> **The Frame:** https://claude.ai/artifact/FHc7oAPoyohgxo87VdxCGw\n> Collections `claims` and `needs`, read and written with `ArtifactData`.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Re-read the source, don't re-read the claim.** A claim that still sounds\n   right is not evidence that it is. Open what it links to.\n2. **Retirement is a feature.** A Frame that only grows is one nobody trusts.\n   \"This used to be true\" is itself worth recording.\n3. **Never retire quietly.** Every retirement says what changed and when.\n4. **Rank by exposure, not by age.** A stale claim nobody cites is housekeeping.\n   A stale claim a roadmap rests on is urgent.\n5. **Pull, don't ask.** Dovetail and PostHog are connected. Read them rather\n   than asking the researcher what they remember.\n\n## The four questions\n\nFrom Constantine Papas, *AI-Powered UX Research* — asked in this order:\n\n1. What did we learn this cycle that **updates** what we believed?\n2. What did we find that we still **cannot explain**?\n3. What would we **do differently**?\n4. What used to be true and **isn't any more**?\n\nEverything below is a way of answering them with evidence.\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Pull the Frame\n- [ ] Age every claim\n- [ ] Re-read what PostHog measures\n- [ ] Re-read what Dovetail holds\n- [ ] Decide: keep / refresh / retire / contest\n- [ ] Write the verdicts back\n- [ ] Rank what to research next\n- [ ] Publish the maintenance note\n```\n\n### 1 — Pull the Frame\n\n```\nArtifactData list  claims   (limit 100)\nArtifactData list  needs    (limit 100)\n```\n\nUse `out_dir` and read the files rather than paging through output.\n\n### 2 — Age every claim\n\nMechanical, no sources needed. Sort by `verified` and bucket:\n\n| Age | Default verdict |\n|---|---|\n| Under 3 months | Keep |\n| 3–6 months | Keep; flag if it describes a surface that shipped since |\n| 6–12 months | **Refresh** — drop one confidence level unless re-verified |\n| Over 12 months | **Retire or re-verify.** No middle option |\n\nThen override by **exposure**: any claim cited by a `needs` row, a one-pager or\nan epic jumps the queue regardless of age. Check `needs[].claims` for which\nclaims are load-bearing.\n\n### 3 — Re-read what PostHog measures\n\nEvery claim with a `posthog` link is re-runnable. Re-run it.\n\n```\nexec  learn -s \"<topic>\"              (once per session, first)\nexec  call insight-query {\"insightId\": \"<short_id>\"}\nexec  call dashboard-insights-run {\"id\": <id>}\nexec  call cohorts-retrieve {\"id\": <id>}\n```\n\nCompare to the claim's stated number. Three outcomes:\n\n| Then | Do |\n|---|---|\n| Within noise | Update `verified` to today. Cheapest maintenance there is |\n| Materially moved | Rewrite the claim with the new figure; record the old one in `caveat` |\n| Query broken or event gone | **Instrumentation gap.** The claim is unmeasurable now — say so rather than keeping a number nobody can reproduce |\n\n**A number that cannot be re-run is not a claim, it is a memory.**\n\n### 4 — Re-read what Dovetail holds\n\nQual decays differently: findings do not move, their *relevance* does.\n\n```\nsearch_workspace          → what is new in this domain since last review\nlist_docs {project_id}    → new synthesis in the projects behind the claims\nget_doc_content           → read the ones that touch a live claim\n```\n\nAsk of each domain:\n\n- Has anything **new** been published that agrees, disagrees, or supersedes?\n- Has the **surface changed** since the research? A claim about a screen dies\n  the day that screen ships\n- Did a new study **corroborate** a single-source claim? That is a promotion —\n  see [references/verdicts.md](references/verdicts.md)\n\n### 5 — Decide\n\nFour verdicts, full criteria in\n[references/verdicts.md](references/verdicts.md):\n\n| | Means |\n|---|---|\n| **Keep** | Re-verified. Update `verified`, nothing else changes |\n| **Refresh** | Still directionally true, evidence is ageing. Drop a confidence level and name what would restore it |\n| **Retire** | No longer true, or describes something that no longer exists |\n| **Contest** | New evidence disagrees. Keep both, mark the domain contested, and it stops auto-closing requests |\n\n### 6 — Write the verdicts back\n\nUse one `ArtifactData` `batch`. Pin every write with the `version` you read.\n\nRetirement is a field, not a delete:\n\n```json\n{ \"retired\": \"2026-10-13\",\n  \"retired_why\": \"The assets page was rebuilt in August; this described the old IA.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"GAP\" }\n```\n\n**Keep the row.** A retired claim is the record that something used to be true,\nand it stops the same finding being rediscovered and re-believed next year.\n\n### 6b — Check the prompts still hold\n\nThe Frame's starter prompts describe what the claims say. When a claim is\nrewritten, recalibrated or retired, **a prompt built on it goes quietly stale** —\nand it arrives with the page's authority behind it, which makes it worse than no\nprompt at all.\n\n```\nArtifactData list prompts\n```\n\nFor each: does it still describe something the claims support? Rewrite what has\ndrifted, and keep it **human-first** — the question a designer would ask, with\nthe figure as backing rather than as the opening.\n\n### 7 — Rank what to research next\n\nThe output that makes this worth doing. Rank by **exposure × weakness**, not by\ncuriosity:\n\n| Rank | Pattern |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | A `blocking` need whose only evidence is LOW or retired |\n| 2 | A domain the coverage matrix shows blind where a decision is live |\n| 3 | A contested claim that keeps blocking requests |\n| 4 | A single-source claim a roadmap rests on — one corroborating source makes it HIGH |\n| 5 | Everything else |\n\nRank 4 is usually the cheapest win: promotion needs one new source, not a study.\n\n### 8 — Publish the note\n\nShort, and it goes to the team, not in a drawer. Template:\n[references/note.md](references/note.md).\n\n## What to watch\n\nThree numbers, quarter on quarter:\n\n| | Healthy |\n|---|---|\n| Claims re-verified vs retired | Both non-zero. All-keep means nobody looked |\n| Share of claims over 6 months | Falling, or at least flat |\n| Requests closed as already-answered | Rising |\n\n**All-keep is the failure mode**, not all-retire. A review that retires nothing\nis a review that did not happen.\n\n## References\n\n- [references/verdicts.md](references/verdicts.md) — the four verdicts, promotion and retirement criteria\n- [references/note.md](references/note.md) — the maintenance note template\n",
"heuristic-evaluation": "---\nname: heuristic-evaluation\ndescription: >-\n  Expert review of a screen or flow against Nielsen's ten heuristics, with a\n  severity rating on every issue and a recommendation attached. Share a Figma\n  screenshot, a frame link, a staging URL or a written description. Use when you\n  need problems found now and cannot wait to recruit anyone.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: prototype\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  accepts: figma-screenshot, figma-link, url, written-flow\n  output: markdown-file\n---\n\n# Heuristic evaluation\n\nWalk an interface against ten well worn usability rules, write down what breaks,\nrate how badly, and say what to do about it.\n\nIt is the fastest way to find the obvious problems. It needs nobody's calendar.\n\n**It is an expert opinion, not a finding.** Nobody used this. Never write \"users\nstruggled with\" about anything this produced. The honest phrasing is \"this looks\nlike it would trip people up, and here is why\".\n\nThe label goes in the file, under the title, because the file outlives the\nconversation.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## Where it sits\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **This** | Does the interface break known usability rules? |\n| [`persona-check`](../persona-check/) | Where does one specific person get stuck doing a task? |\n| `ux-critique` | Does the design do what the one-pager asked for? |\n| `accessibility-review` | Contrast, focus order, keyboard, screen readers |\n\nThis one and `persona-check` pair well. Run this first for the rule breaking,\nthen `persona-check` for the task breaking. They catch different things: this\nfinds the inconsistent button, the persona finds the moment someone gives up.\n\n## How to write it\n\nPlain words. \"There's no way back from this screen\" rather than \"the flow lacks\na reversible exit affordance\".\n\nSay what someone would feel, not just what is wrong. An unlabelled error that\nloses the form is not a consistency issue, it is the moment someone stops\ntrusting the product.\n\nKeep it short. Eight real problems beat thirty padded ones, and a long list\nmeans a designer fixes the three easiest.\n\n## Back it with a quote wherever you can\n\nThis is the part that makes the difference here.\n\nBefore you write the list, pull what we already know about this area from\n[`user-context`](../user-context/) and the Frame.\n\nAn issue that lines up with something a real person said is worth ten issues\nthat only an expert noticed. Put the quote right under the issue:\n\n> **Nothing confirms the link saved.** (Visibility of system status, severity 3)\n> A partner already told us this: \"I clicked save like four times because\n> nothing happened. Then I had four of them.\" [p07, Dovetail]\n\nMark the ones with nothing behind them as what they are. They may still be real.\nThey are just softer, and the reader deserves to know which is which.\n\n## What you can give it\n\n**A Figma screenshot.** Fine for one screen or state.\n\n**A Figma frame link.** Better, because the real labels come through and labels\nare where half of these problems live. If the connector is not authorised, say\nso and ask for a screenshot rather than guessing at the copy.\n\n**A staging or production URL.** Best, because you can try the error states,\nwhich is where most severity 3 and 4 issues hide. Do not sign in with anyone's\nreal credentials, and do not submit anything live.\n\n**A written description.** Works. You will miss anything that is only visible in\nthe layout.\n\nIf you were handed one screen and the issues are really about the flow, say so.\nA single screen cannot tell you whether someone gets through.\n\n## Running it\n\n### 1. Say what you are reviewing and what you are not\n\nName the screens and the flows. Name what you could not reach: states behind a\npaywall, error paths you could not trigger, anything you only saw as a static\nimage.\n\nThat list goes in the file. It is how a reader knows the difference between\n\"clean\" and \"not looked at\".\n\n### 2. Walk it three times\n\n**As someone new.** First time here. No idea what the words mean. This pass\nfinds the jargon, the missing starting point and the unexplained default.\n\n**As someone who does this every day.** Fourth time this week. This pass finds\nthe missing shortcut, the confirmation that got boring, the five clicks that\nshould be one.\n\n**Through the actual tasks.** The real jobs people come here for. This pass\nfinds the broken handoffs between screens.\n\nThree passes because each one makes different things visible. A new person never\nnotices the missing bulk action, and an expert stopped reading the empty state\nmonths ago.\n\n### 3. The ten heuristics\n\nUse them as a checklist on each pass, not a structure for the report.\n\n| | Ask |\n|---|---|\n| **Visibility of system status** | Do you know what just happened, and what is happening now? |\n| **Speaks your language** | Are these our words or the database's words? |\n| **A way out** | Can you undo, go back, or leave without losing work? |\n| **Consistent** | Does the same thing look and behave the same everywhere? |\n| **Stops mistakes** | Is it easy to do the wrong thing? |\n| **Shows, not remembers** | Do you have to carry something in your head from the last screen? |\n| **Fast for regulars** | Is there a quicker path once you know the way? |\n| **Nothing spare** | Is anything on screen competing with what matters? |\n| **Good errors** | Does it say what went wrong, in plain words, and how to fix it? |\n| **Help when stuck** | Is there help where you get stuck, rather than in a manual? |\n\nSeveral heuristics usually fit one issue. Pick the one that explains it best and\nmove on. Arguing about which heuristic something belongs to is wasted time.\n\n### 4. Write each issue the same way\n\n**What breaks.** In plain words, from the person's side.\n\n**Where.** The screen, and the exact element or label.\n\n**Which rule.** One heuristic.\n\n**How bad.** The severity, with a reason.\n\n**What we already know.** The quote or claim, if there is one, or \"nothing\nbehind this yet\".\n\n**What to do.** A concrete suggestion, not \"improve clarity\". If you genuinely\ndo not know the fix, say so. A named problem with an honest \"not sure what the\nright answer is\" is more useful than an invented recommendation.\n\n### 5. Severity\n\n| | Means | Reality |\n|---|---|---|\n| **4** | Catastrophe. Fix before this ships | Data loss, money wrong, a dead end with no way out |\n| **3** | Major. Important to fix | People will fail the task or route around it |\n| **2** | Minor. Fix when you are nearby | Slows people down, they still get there |\n| **1** | Cosmetic | Fix if there is time |\n| **0** | Not actually a problem | You talked yourself out of it. Drop it from the list |\n\nRate it on three things together: how often people hit it, how much it hurts\nwhen they do, and whether it keeps hurting or they learn their way past it once.\nSomething annoying that never goes away can outrank something painful that\npeople solve on their first try.\n\n**Be strict with 4s.** If everything is a catastrophe, nothing is. A review with\nnine 4s gets ignored, and deserves to be.\n\n## The file\n\nWrite it to `docs/heuristic-reviews/<date>-<surface>.md`.\n\n```markdown\n# Heuristic review: [surface]\n**Expert review, not research. Nobody used this.**\n[date] · [what you were given] · [who ran it]\n\n## What we looked at\n[screens and flows]\n**Not covered:** [states you could not reach, and why]\n\n## The short version\n[Three or four lines. The pattern, not a list. What is the one thing that would\nmake the most difference.]\n\n## Issues\n### [n]. [What breaks, in plain words] · severity [0-4]\n**Where:** …\n**Rule:** …\n**What we already know:** [quote + claim id, or \"nothing behind this yet\"]\n**Suggestion:** …\n\n## What this can't tell you\n[One evaluator, not three to five. Screens not reached. Which issues have real\nevidence behind them and which are judgement. What would need a real session.]\n```\n\nSort by severity, worst first.\n\n## Handing it over\n\nThe markdown opens anywhere and pastes into a Figma comment or a doc as is. To\nsend it to a Figma agent, point the agent at the file and the frame together so\neach issue lands on the screen it came from.\n\nIf someone asks for just the issues without the caveats, give them the issues\nand the one label line. It is a sentence, and it is the difference between an\nopinion and a claim about users.\n\n## Being straight about the limits\n\nSay these in the file, every time. They are not boilerplate, they change what\nthe reader should do with it.\n\n**One evaluator finds a fraction of what is there.** The research on this is\nclear: three to five people reviewing separately, then comparing, find\nsubstantially more than one person does. One pass is a start, not a sweep.\n\n**None of this is validated.** Every issue is a hypothesis. Some will turn out\nnot to bother anyone. A couple of real sessions will reorder this list.\n\n**It cannot tell you what people want.** It only tells you where the interface\nbreaks its own rules. Plenty of usable products fail because nobody needed them.\n\nIf you have a real usability session coming up, hand them this list first. The\nbest use of a heuristic review is deciding what to watch for.\n\n## Two things never to put in\n\n**A number about users.** No percentages, no \"most people\". There is no sample\nhere.\n\n**A ship or no-ship verdict.** It says what is broken and how badly. The call is\nthe team's.\n",
"heuristic-review": "---\nname: heuristic-review\ndescription: >-\n  Expert usability pass over a screen, flow, prototype or Figma frame — the ten\n  classic heuristics plus the marketplace ones impact.com keeps breaking:\n  two-sided consequence, money legibility, role truth, scale asymmetry. Each\n  finding names the heuristic, the likely user cost, a severity, and the\n  cheapest fix. Expert judgement, never evidence. Use when someone says run a\n  heuristic review, expert review, usability review, what's wrong with this\n  screen, give this a once-over before I show it, or review this flow.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: ideate\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  output: expert-judgement\n---\n\n# Heuristic review\n\nOne pass over a design against a fixed list, so the obvious problems are found\nby a checklist rather than by a participant in session three.\n\nMinutes, not days. It needs no recruiting, no connectors and no prototype — a\nscreenshot, a Figma frame, a URL or a description is enough.\n\n## The rule everything else depends on\n\n**Everything this skill produces is Expert judgement.** Never evidence.\n\n> Never write \"users struggle with\" about something a heuristic review found.\n\nA violation predicts a problem. It does not measure one, and the size of the\nproblem is unknown until someone watches a real person hit it. Label the\noutput, keep the label on it when it travels into a doc, and when a review and\na study disagree, the study is right.\n\n## What it is not\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **`heuristic-review`** | Does this design break known usability principles? |\n| **`ux-critique`** | Does it cover the one-pager, the epic and the tickets? |\n| **`persona-check`** | Where does *this named person* get stuck walking through it? |\n| **`accessibility-review`** | WCAG blockers, focus order, contrast, keyboard paths |\n| **`user-context` critique** | What the evidence predicts about this design |\n\nThe four pre-test skills answer different questions and none substitutes for\nanother. A design can pass every heuristic and still solve the wrong problem —\nthat is `ux-critique`'s job, not this one's.\n\n## Integrity\n\n**Lead with the insight, not the evidence.** What they get back is short: the\nfindings that change something, ranked. Not a numbered sweep of all fifteen\nheuristics — nobody reads the clean ones.\n\n**Offer the depth, don't deliver it.** Close with one follow-up they can run —\n*\"want the full pass including what's already fine?\"* — not a menu.\n\n1. **Name the heuristic.** A finding without one is an opinion. Use the list in\n   [references/heuristics.md](references/heuristics.md) and quote its name.\n2. **Say what the user does.** Not \"this is confusing\" — *\"they will hit Save\n   expecting a draft and publish to live partners.\"* No predicted behaviour, no\n   finding.\n3. **Rank by consequence, not by count.** Three findings that cost money beat\n   twelve that cost a second. Severity rules below.\n4. **Cheapest fix that clears the violation**, not the redesign you would\n   prefer. If the fix is a redesign, say that plainly and say why.\n5. **Check both sides.** Every impact.com screen sits in a two-sided\n   marketplace. A brand-side change that quietly costs partners is a finding\n   even when the brand screen is immaculate.\n6. **Separate what you can see from what you are assuming.** A static frame\n   hides states. Say which findings depend on behaviour you could not observe.\n7. **Do not invent the product.** If you cannot tell what a control does, that\n   is itself a finding under *Recognition over recall* — do not guess and\n   review the guess.\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Get the artifact and the context\n- [ ] Pass against the list\n- [ ] Severity and fixes\n- [ ] Offer the evidence cross-check\n```\n\n1. **Take the artifact.** URL, Figma frame, screenshot, or a description.\n   Ask for only what is missing, once: who the screen is for (brand or\n   partner), and the one task it exists to support. Without the task there is\n   no *Match with the real world* and no severity — say so and ask.\n\n2. **Pass against [references/heuristics.md](references/heuristics.md).** Ten\n   classic, five marketplace. Work the list, not your first impression.\n\n3. **Write the findings** in the format in\n   [references/report.md](references/report.md). Ranked, severity-rated, each\n   with the heuristic, the predicted user cost, and the fix.\n\n4. **Offer the cross-check.** Some findings will already be answered —\n   confirmed or contradicted — by what we know. Offer one line:\n   *\"three of these touch claims in the Frame — want me to check them against\n   `user-context`?\"* Do not run it unasked.\n\n5. **Hand on.** If the design is heading for sessions, the next skills are\n   `accessibility-review` then `persona-check`, and `test-plan` turns the\n   unresolved findings into tasks worth watching someone attempt.\n\n## Severity\n\n| | Means | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Blocker** | The task cannot be completed, or completing it causes loss — money, data, a live change they did not intend | Publish with no confirmation on a live partner list |\n| **Major** | The task completes but most people take a wrong turn first, or a sizeable group gives up | Primary action below the fold on the densest screen |\n| **Minor** | Friction. People get there, slower or more annoyed | Filter state lost on back navigation |\n| **Cosmetic** | Inconsistency with no behavioural cost | Two button styles for the same action class |\n\nSeverity is **frequency × cost × recoverability**, judged for the role the\nscreen is for. A rare action that moves money outranks a daily one that costs\na click. If you cannot judge frequency, say so and rate on cost alone.\n\n## Output\n\nShort. Blockers and Majors in the reply, Minors and Cosmetics listed as\none-liners, Cosmetics collapsed into a single line if there are more than\nthree. Full template:\n[references/report.md](references/report.md).\n\nEvery finding carries the **Expert judgement (AI)** label when it travels into\nConfluence, a ticket or a readout.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Walking all fifteen heuristics out loud, including the ones with no findings\n- \"This is confusing\" with no predicted behaviour behind it\n- Severity by how strongly you feel about it\n- Reviewing one side of the marketplace and calling the design reviewed\n- Redesigning instead of reviewing — the fix clears the violation, nothing more\n- Writing \"users will\" instead of \"a partner at this step likely\"\n- Letting a finding reach a doc without the Expert judgement label\n",
"ideate": "---\nname: ideate\ndescription: >-\n  Starts Ideate from framing (one-pager/PRD preferred; epic, tickets, Granola,\n  or notes OK): prompts for fidelity/tool, emits an ideation brief, and seeds\n  wireframe or mock directions. Use for Ideate-stage divergence, solution\n  brainstorming, wireframes, mock variations, or Solution Ideator handoffs.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: ideate\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Solution Ideator\n---\n\n# Ideate (diverge)\n\nOn-demand Ideate skill. Implements the FigJam **Solution Ideator**:\nframing in → ideation brief + tool routing → starter concepts / wires / mock prompts.\nDoes **not** pick a final direction — designer iterates, then runs `ux-critique` before\n`prototype-handoff`.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Prefer a **one-pager or detailed PRD**; accept epic, tickets, Granola/meeting notes, Slack, or chat paste. If framing is thin, build a **Framing snapshot**, label gaps, warn — do not block.\n2. Label **Verified** vs **AI interpretation**. Never invent metrics/quotes from framing sources.\n3. Prompt the designer for **fidelity**, **surface**, and **# of directions** before generating visuals.\n4. Dual marketplace: Brand and/or Partner lenses via personas when audience is known.\n5. Stop short of Prototype pack — that is `prototype-handoff` after `ux-critique`.\n\nSee [references/brief.md](references/brief.md), [references/routing.md](references/routing.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Designer (+ PM chat) ready to explore solutions\n- “Ideate / brainstorm / wireframe / mock variations” from a spec or notes\n- Need a brief before FigJam, Figma Make, Cursor, Claude, or Google Stitch\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\nPull before diverging — do not rely on chat memory alone:\n\n| Source | How | Preference |\n|---|---|---|\n| One-pager / PRD | Confluence (space `ux`) or pasted markdown | Preferred |\n| UX Epic + tickets | Atlassian / Jira project `UX` | Preferred with one-pager |\n| Meeting / Granola notes | Pasted notes or linked doc | Acceptable |\n| Prior chats | `SearchConversations` for topic / epic | Fallback |\n| UX DesignOps FigJam | Figma `get_figjam` on board `slhZ9uzMTmaekjmbEosyzb` (Ideate column) | Pipeline context |\n| Personas / DS | `brand-operator-ux-persona`, `creator-affiliate-ux-persona`, `design-system` | When UI-bound |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Intake** — Collect whatever framing exists. Prefer one-pager/PRD; if only notes, snapshot problem / audience / JTBD / constraints / open questions and list gaps.\n2. **Prompt designer** — Ask (batch): fidelity (sketches / wires / hi-fi); surface (FigJam / Claude / Cursor / Figma Make / Figma agent / Stitch); how many directions (default 3–5); DS-bound vs exploratory.\n3. **Ideation brief** — Jobs, constraints, non-goals, success criteria snippets, evidence anchors. See [references/brief.md](references/brief.md).\n4. **Route** — Recommend surface per [references/routing.md](references/routing.md). Styleguide-aware → Figma Make or Cursor + `design-system`; wires/thinking → Claude or FigJam; visual variations → Figma agent / Stitch after brief exists.\n5. **Diverge** — Emit 3–5 concept directions (text + optional lo-fi wires or tool-ready prompts). Label AI mocks as exploration.\n6. **Stop** — Point designer to iterate in chosen tool, then `ux-critique` on shortlist. Do not declare a chosen solution or run Prototype handoff.\n\n## Output template (chat)\n\n```markdown\n# Ideation brief — [title]\nStatus: DIVERGING · Audience: Brand | Partner | Shared\nSources: [one-pager / PRD / epic / tickets / notes / chat ids]\nFraming quality: Strong (one-pager/PRD) | Partial | Thin (notes only — gaps listed)\n\n## Framing snapshot\n- Problem / opportunity:\n- JTBD:\n- Constraints / non-goals:\n- Gaps in framing:\n\n## Designer choices\n- Fidelity:\n- Surface:\n- # directions:\n- DS-bound: yes | no | mixed\n\n## Directions (AI exploration)\n### D1 — [name]\n- Concept:\n- Addresses (from framing):\n- Risk:\n\n### D2 — …\n…\n\n## Next\n- Iterate in [surface]\n- When shortlisted: run `ux-critique`\n- When happy with mocks: run `prototype-handoff`\n```\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use ideate on UX-1234 — pull Confluence one-pager + epic. I want 4 wireframe directions in Claude first.`\n2. `Use ideate from these Granola notes (no one-pager yet). Snapshot framing, warn on gaps, then 3 FigJam concepts.`\n3. `Use ideate: we have a PRD — recommend tool for on-brand mock variations, then seed Figma Make prompts.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Generating hi-fi mocks before asking fidelity/surface\n- Treating notes-only intake as a signed PRD without labeling gaps\n- Declaring a winning direction or skipping `ux-critique`\n- Jumping to Prototype scaffolder inside Ideate\n",
"measurable-check": "---\nname: measurable-check\ndescription: >-\n  Answers one question before anyone designs a study: can we measure this at\n  all? Checks whether the events, properties and surfaces a question depends on\n  actually exist in PostHog, and says plainly when they do not. Use when someone\n  says can we measure this, is this instrumented, do we track that, before we\n  run a study can you check the data exists, or why does this number say zero.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  answers: can-we-measure-it\n---\n\n# Measurable check\n\nOne question, asked early: **can we measure this at all?**\n\nIt takes five minutes and it is the cheapest step in the pipeline. The\nalternative is finding out after fifteen people have been recruited around a\nquestion the data cannot see.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## Why it earns its own skill\n\n`research-builder` checks instrumentation while building a dashboard, and\n`research-intake` routes to *instrument first* when a request turns on a missing\nevent. Both find the problem **mid-flow**, once someone is already invested.\n\nThis is the standalone front door: ask it before designing anything, about a\nquestion that does not yet have a study attached.\n\n## The rule this exists for\n\n**An uninstrumented event returns zero, and a zero reads exactly like nobody\ndoing the thing.**\n\nThat sentence is the entire justification. A funnel step with no event behind it\nlooks like total abandonment. A property that was never set looks like every\naccount having the same value. Both look confident, both look plausible, and both\nare wrong. Neither one tells you it is wrong.\n\n## The rules that keep this honest\n\n1. **Check the schema, never go off the name.** Teams name events differently,\n   and a name can look official when it is nothing of the sort.\n2. **No data is not the same as zero.** Say which of the two you are looking at,\n   every time.\n3. **Check the property, not just the event.** An event that fires without the\n   breakdown you need is tracked and still no use to you.\n4. **Report the gap, do not design around it.** Shrinking a question until the\n   data we happen to have can answer it is how a study ends up answering\n   something nobody asked.\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] State what would have to be true\n- [ ] Check the events exist\n- [ ] Check the properties exist\n- [ ] Check there is enough history\n- [ ] Verdict\n```\n\n### 1. State what would have to be true\n\nTurn the question into the events that would answer it. Write them down before\nsearching, or you will find something adjacent and accept it.\n\n> *\"Do partners who use bulk link generation stay longer?\"*\n> needs: a bulk-generation event · something on it that identifies the partner ·\n> sign-in or activity events across a window long enough to see people stay.\n\nThree things, any of which can be missing independently.\n\n### 2. Check the events\n\n```\nexec  learn -s \"<topic>\"                                     once, first, alone\nexec  call read-data-schema {\"query\":{\"kind\":\"events\"}}\n```\n\nMatch on the real name. A near-match is not a match: `link_generated` and\n`PPX:publisherpro_affiliate_link_generated` may be different surfaces, different\neras, or one may have replaced the other and stopped firing.\n\n### 3. Check the properties\n\n```\nexec  call read-data-schema {\"query\":{\"kind\":\"event_properties\",\"event_name\":\"<event>\"}}\nexec  call read-data-schema {\"query\":{\"kind\":\"event_property_values\",\"event_name\":\"<e>\",\"property_name\":\"<p>\"}}\n```\n\nThe breakdown is usually where this fails. An event fires for everyone but\ncarries nothing that separates partners from brands, or one tier from another.\nIt can tell you *how many* and never *which*.\n\nCheck the **values**, not just that the property exists. A property present on\n3% of events is not a breakdown, it is a trap.\n\n### 4. Check there is enough history\n\nAn event instrumented three weeks ago cannot answer a question about last\nquarter. Find when it started firing and compare that to the window the question\nneeds.\n\nWatch for a **rename**: a clean cliff where one event stops and a similar one\nstarts is one behaviour split across two names, and every trend through that\ndate is wrong.\n\n### 5. The answer\n\nFour, and only one of them is \"yes\":\n\n| | Means | Next |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Measurable** | Events, properties and history all present | Hand to [`research-builder`](../research-builder/) to build the dashboard |\n| **Partly** | We can honestly answer a smaller version | Say exactly which part and let them decide. Do not shrink it for them |\n| **Not yet** | The event or property does not exist | Instrumentation ticket to the squad that owns the surface, then a small study once there is a fortnight of data |\n| **Not countable** | The question is about how people feel: why, whether they trust it, what they expected | Not a tracking gap. Go and ask people |\n\n**That last one matters most.** Not every question is a counting question.\nTreating a how-do-they-feel question as a tracking gap sends a squad off to\nbuild instrumentation that will never answer it.\n\n## Output\n\n```markdown\n# Can we measure this? [question]\nChecked [date] · PostHog project [id]\n\n## What would have to be true\n1. [event] · [found / missing / renamed on <date>]\n2. [property on that event] · [found / missing / thin: n% of events]\n3. [history] · [first seen <date>, question needs <window>]\n\n## The answer\n**[Measurable | Partly | Not yet | Not countable]**\n[one line]\n\n## If not yet\nEvent: [name] · Property: [name] · Surface: [where] · Owner: [squad]\nEarliest a study could run: [date + a fortnight]\n\n## What this does not tell you\n[the how-do-they-feel half of the question, if there is one. No amount of\ntracking will reach it]\n```\n\n## Record it\n\nA **Not yet** is worth recording. Write it to the Frame via\n[`user-context`](../user-context/) `record` with `confidence: GAP`, so the next\nperson asking the same question finds the answer instead of rediscovering it.\n\nInclude the event name and the owning squad. A gap that names its fix is a\nticket; one that does not is a complaint.\n",
"panel-planner": "---\nname: panel-planner\ndescription: >-\n  Works out who to talk to for a study, using a Rally CSV export. Checks whether\n  the right people are in the panel at all, suggests who to approach based on\n  what the export tells you about them, and flags anyone we have been leaning on\n  too often. Use when someone says who should we talk to, can we recruit this,\n  check the panel, or have we spoken to these people before.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  input: rally-csv\n---\n\n# Panel planner\n\nThree questions, in order:\n\n1. Do we have the right people at all?\n2. Who specifically should we approach?\n3. Who have we been asking too often?\n\nRun it before anyone writes a screener. A screener built for people who are not\nin the panel wastes the design and the slot.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## How to write it up\n\nPlain words. The person reading this is deciding whether a study can happen, not\nreading a report.\n\nSay what the export does not tell you. CSV columns vary, and a column that is\nmissing is not a column you can guess at.\n\nNever widen the audience quietly to make the numbers work. The study then\nanswers a question nobody asked, and nobody notices until the readout.\n\n## The export\n\nRead the header rather than assuming. What this needs, and what it is usually\ncalled:\n\n| Needs | Often called |\n|---|---|\n| Someone's id | `id`, `person_id`, `email` |\n| Partner or brand | `type`, `account_type`, `role` |\n| Size or tier | `partner_tier`, `programs`, `monthly_earnings` |\n| Region | `country`, `region` |\n| When we last spoke to them | `last_study`, `last_contacted` |\n| How often they have taken part | `studies`, `participation_count` |\n\n**If the last contacted column is missing, say so and stop after question two.**\nFatigue cannot be guessed at, and a confident \"these people are fine to ask\"\nthat nobody checked is worse than not answering.\n\nCount people, not rows. Exports have duplicates, and a panel that looks like 400\nis often nearer 260.\n\n## 1. Do we have the right people?\n\nFilter down one thing at a time and write the count after each step.\n\n```\nEveryone in the panel          1,240\n  partners only                1,240\n  Scaling Up tier                418\n  EMEA                            96\n  active in the last 30 days      31   <- this is what's limiting you\n```\n\nThat ladder is the useful output, not the number at the bottom. It shows which\nfilter is doing the damage, which is the one worth negotiating.\n\nCompare the pool against the target in\n[`research-planner`](../research-planner/references/rigor.md):\n\n| Pool against target | What to say |\n|---|---|\n| Five times or more | Comfortable. Recruit normally |\n| Three to five times | Workable. Expect to chase, allow a longer window |\n| Under three times | Tight. Flag it before the screener gets written |\n| Under the target | We cannot recruit this group. Options below |\n\nYou need the multiple because most people do not reply. A panel the same size as\nthe target recruits nobody.\n\nWhen it is short, lay out the choices and let the requester pick. Widen one\nfilter, and say what that costs. Use a different method, since a Pendo guide\nreaches people the panel does not. Or wait and recruit into the panel first.\n\n## 2. Who should we approach?\n\nThis is the part people usually do by hand, and the export already knows most of\nit.\n\nSort the eligible pool by what makes someone worth talking to for this study:\n\n**Match the behaviour, not the label.** If the study is about link generation,\nsomeone who generates links weekly tells you more than someone whose tier says\nthey should.\n\n**Spread across the thing most likely to change the answer.** Usually tenure,\nsize, or how heavily they use the feature. Pick two or three and spread across\nthem deliberately.\n\n**Include people it went badly for.** Lapsed accounts and low earners are harder\nto reach and usually say the most useful things. A panel of happy regulars\nproduces a comfortable study.\n\n**Flag anyone unusual.** The partner with 200 programmes, the brand with none.\nWorth one session each, never worth treating as typical.\n\nHand back a shortlist with a reason beside each name, not just a filtered list.\nThe reason is what lets someone swap one out sensibly.\n\n## 3. Who are we over-asking?\n\n| Last spoke to them | Treat as |\n|---|---|\n| Under 30 days | Leave them out. Too soon |\n| 30 to 90 days | Fine, unless they are more than a third of the pool |\n| Over 90 days | Fine |\n| Three or more studies in a year | Flag as a regular |\n\nThe regulars are the real problem and you cannot see them without checking. The\nsame handful says yes to everything, gets good at being interviewed, learns the\nproduct and the team, and gives you articulate, forgiving research that reads\nlike strong signal.\n\nIf regulars are more than a third of who is left, say so. It belongs in the\nreadout's limitations whether or not anyone changes the recruitment.\n\n## What to hand back\n\n```markdown\n# Panel plan: [study]\n[file], [n] rows, [n] actual people · [date]\n\n## Can we run it?\n[The filter ladder]\nTarget [n], pool [n]. [Comfortable / Workable / Tight / Not with this group]\nWhat's limiting it: [which filter]\n\n## Who to approach\n| Person | Why them |\nSpread across: [what you varied]\nWorth one session: [the unusual ones]\n\n## Who to leave out this time\nSpoke to them recently: [n]\nRegulars: [n], which is [n]% of the pool\n\n## Who this won't reach\n[The group the panel does not contain. This goes in the readout.]\n\n## What the export didn't tell me\n[Missing columns, and what that means you cannot answer.]\n```\n\nSend the shortlist to Rally for recruitment, and send the \"who this won't reach\"\nline to [`research-planner`](../research-planner/). It belongs in the write-up\nfrom the start, not added defensively at the end.\n",
"partner-prototype": "---\nname: partner-prototype\ndescription: >-\n  Auto-sets up the impact.com Partner UI prototype shell: clones\n  PartnerUI-shell, installs Node if missing plus npm/Vite deps, starts Vite.\n  Use when the user says partner-prototype, Partner UI shell, or Partner\n  prototype in Cursor / Claude Code / Copilot. In Figma Make or Claude chat,\n  use ux-prototyping (those harnesses run a live preview without this script).\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: prototype\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Prototype Scaffolder\n---\n\n# Partner prototype (shell bootstrap)\n\n**One invocation → running Partner UI shell.** Do not ask the designer to clone,\ninstall Node, or run `npm install`. The bootstrap script does that.\n\nThis skill **only** bootstraps the shell. Building mocks into screens is a\nfollow-on step in the same Cursor chat after READY.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Run the bootstrap script** — do not improvise `mkdir` / `git clone` / `npm`\n   steps by hand unless the script is missing.\n2. Template is **Vite + React** (Figma Make–origin bundle).\n3. In Figma Make or Claude chat (no shell), do **not** run this script —\n   invoke `ux-prototyping` instead (live preview in that harness).\n4. After READY, move the Cursor workspace to the project path when possible.\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use `partner-prototype`” / `/partner-prototype`\n- Designer ready to prototype Partner-side after mocks / `prototype-handoff`\n- Need the real Partner chrome locally\n\n## Hard requirements (agent must check)\n\n| Need | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Shell / terminal | Script runs locally (Cursor or Claude Code) |\n| `git` | Clone the public template |\n| Network | GitHub + npm registry (+ Node download if missing) |\n\n**Node 18+ and npm are auto-provisioned** when missing (local copy under\n`~/Documents/impact-prototypes/.toolchain`, no sudo). Only `git` is a\nhard machine prerequisite (Xcode CLT on macOS).\n\nIf git is missing on a **shell** harness → stop with a short git/Xcode note.\nIf this is Figma Make or Claude chat → invoke `ux-prototyping` (Path B / C).\nDo not hand the designer a git/npm checklist.\n\n## Workflow (Cursor / Claude Code)\n\n1. **Locate script** —  \n   `.agents/skills/partner-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh`  \n   (or `.cursor/skills/partner-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh` via symlink).\n2. **Run it with the sandbox off.** In Cursor Shell, pass\n   `required_permissions: [\"all\"]` (network + unsandboxed npm). Default\n   sandbox **cannot** `npm install` or start Vite — that is why other\n   designers see “packages missing.”\n\n   ```bash\n   bash \"/absolute/path/to/partner-prototype/scripts/bootstrap.sh\"\n   ```\n\n   Optional flags: `--no-dev`, `--reclone`, `--parent ~/other/folder`.\n3. **Parse READY** — look for `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY shell=partner path=… url=…`.\n4. **Cursor only** — call `move_agent_to_root` with that `path`.\n5. **Confirm** — folder path + open the printed URL (Partner default `http://localhost:5173/`).\n6. **Stop** — shell is ready. Wait for mock links before editing UI.\n   Use `design-system` for Styleguide V2 constraints.\n\nDefault install location:\n\n`~/Documents/impact-prototypes/partner-ui`\n\nOverride with `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_HOME` or `--parent`.\n\n## Harness matrix\n\nSee [references/harnesses.md](references/harnesses.md).\n\n| Harness | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| **Cursor** | Run script → `move_agent_to_root` → confirm URL. |\n| **Claude Code / Copilot** | Run script → open the path → confirm URL. |\n| **Figma Make / Claude chat** | Do not run this script. Use `ux-prototyping` (preview in that harness). |\n\n## Template facts (do not invent)\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Repo | https://github.com/lesley-spec/PartnerUI-shell.git (public) |\n| Stack | Vite · React · `npm run dev` |\n| Source design | https://www.figma.com/design/jsQedgqU0QkgXQEsMFEyOE/Partner-UI-%E2%80%94-impact.com--Copy- |\n| Layout | `src/app/` (routes, layout, TopNav), `src/styles/`, `index.html` |\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use partner-prototype — set up the Partner shell for me.`\n2. `Use partner-prototype; then build these Partner mocks into the shell: [Figma link].`\n3. `Partner prototype broken — partner-prototype --reclone.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Manual git/npm recipes for the designer when the script exists\n- Asking the designer to install Node, Homebrew, or `npm install` themselves\n- Claiming this bootstrap ran inside Figma Make or Claude chat (use `ux-prototyping`)\n- Editing UI before READY\n- Asking where to put the folder when the default is fine\n- Scaffolding a new Vite app instead of this Partner shell\n\n## Success criteria\n\n- [ ] Script exited 0\n- [ ] `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY` printed with path + url\n- [ ] Designer sees Partner UI at the URL\n- [ ] (Cursor) Workspace root is the prototype folder\n",
"pendo-guide-strategy": "---\nname: pendo-guide-strategy\ndescription: >-\n  The strategic layer before anyone opens Pendo. Sets audience temperature,\n  writes benefit-first copy, collapses the flow to a single step, and defines the\n  three conversion goals that say whether a guide actually recruited anyone.\n  Built for research recruitment and panel building; the levers work for any\n  in-product guide. Use when someone says build a Pendo guide, our guide\n  engagement is low, recruit partners into a research panel, should this be Pendo\n  or email, or why did this guide underperform.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  builds: pendo-guide|research-panel\n---\n\n# Pendo guide strategy\n\nGuides fail because they get built first and thought about second. The common\nresult is **1–5% engagement**: shown to everyone, three steps long, no stated\nbenefit, no conversion goal, and no way to tell afterwards whether anyone joined\nanything.\n\nThis is the layer **before** the Pendo UI.\n\n## Integrity\n\n**Lead with the insight, not the evidence.** What the person gets back is\nshort: the finding, what it changes, and the next thing to do. A few lines, not\na report — they came for a decision, not a download.\n\n**Offer the depth, don't deliver it.** Close with one specific follow-up they\ncan run — \"want the evidence trail behind finding 2?\" — not a menu of five. The\ndetail stays available; it does not arrive unasked.\n\n**A number earns its place by changing a decision.** Everything else goes in the\nartifact (the claim, the card, the one-pager) where someone can find it when\nthey need it.\n\n1. **A guide is customer-facing.** It never goes to live traffic without a named\n   human signing off. The skill drafts; a person ships.\n2. **Guide engagement is not recruitment.** A 20% click rate with nobody in the\n   panel is a failure. Measure through to participation or do not claim success.\n3. **No decision, no guide.** If you cannot name the product decision the\n   research informs, you are fishing. Stop here.\n4. **One ask per person.** Frequency-cap and exclude prior viewers, or you train\n   people to dismiss the next guide unread.\n5. **Benchmarks are not promises.** The numbers below are planning targets from\n   guide-type norms, not measured impact.com results. Record your own.\n\n## The three levers\n\n```\nengagement  =  audience temperature  ×  message clarity  ×  friction\n```\n\nCold audience + vague message + multi-step → **~2%**\nWarm audience + clear benefit + single step → **15–25%**\n\nMost teams only ever pull the third one. The first is the biggest.\n\n## 1 — Audience temperature\n\n| Temperature | Who | Expected | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Cold** — everyone | No prior in-app engagement | 1–5% | Urgent, universal only: security, critical bugs |\n| **Warm** — engaged | Has interacted with other guides or releases | 15–35% | Research recruitment, feature adoption |\n| **Super-warm** — behavioural segment | Matches specific recent behaviour | 25–50% | High-value recruitment, strategic pushes |\n\n**Target on behaviour, not self-description** — \"viewed a release guide twice in\n90 days\" beats \"says they are an active user\". Three segment recipes, written as\nPendo rules: [references/targeting.md](references/targeting.md).\n\n> If you are about to launch to everyone and it is not an emergency, that single\n> decision is costing you roughly a 10× difference in response.\n\n## 2 — Copy architecture\n\nLead with the benefit, not the ask.\n\n| Component | Job | Weak | Strong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Subtitle | Benefit first | \"Help us out\" | \"Shape what we prioritise next\" |\n| Title | What I will do | \"Participate in research\" | \"Join our user research panel\" |\n| Description | Why me, why now, what is involved | \"Understand how you use the platform\" | \"First look at what we're building · 2–10 min studies · informs the roadmap\" |\n| Button | Assumptive and specific | \"Learn more\" | \"Join the panel\" |\n| Decline | Keeps them warm | \"Not interested\" | \"Remind me later\" |\n\nFull component guidance and the three-variant test framework (exclusivity /\ncommunity / problem-solving): [references/copy.md](references/copy.md).\n\n## 3 — Friction: single-step or multi\n\n**Single-step** — header, title, description, one CTA to an external form.\nUse when you are asking for a **decision or a one-time action**. Join, sign up,\nbook. Expect 15–25% on a warm audience.\n\n**Multi-step** — use only when you are **teaching a process**: onboarding,\nfeature walkthroughs. For recruitment it is a graveyard; typical drop is 70–80%\nby step three.\n\nMove the form and the scheduling *outside* the guide. The guide's only job is\nthe yes.\n\n## 4 — The decision this informs\n\nAnswer all three before building. If any is blank, do not build.\n\n1. **What product decision depends on this research?** Roadmap prioritisation,\n   feature validation, adoption strategy, retention intervention.\n2. **What would change if we got the feedback?** No answer means no study.\n3. **Who has to see it to make that decision?** If no named person says yes, the\n   audience is wrong.\n\nRouting — whether this is MICRO, SPRINT or DEEP, and whether it should run at\nall — belongs to `research-intake`, not here.\n\n## 5 — Conversion goals\n\nSet three in Pendo. Not one.\n\n| Goal | Transition | Tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Clicked the button | awareness → intent | Is the messaging working |\n| 2 Completed the signup form | intent → commitment | Is the friction acceptable |\n| 3 Attended the first study | commitment → follow-through | Does the panel actually exist |\n\n**Goal 3 is the real one.** High 1 and low 3 means you built an audience of\nclickers, and the fix is downstream of the guide — form, scheduling, follow-up.\n\n## Benchmarks\n\n| Guide type | Typical | Target | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Research recruitment | 1–5% cold · 15–25% warm | 15%+ | Warm targeting is the whole game |\n| Feature release | 5–15% | 10%+ | Context-triggered |\n| Urgent alert | 20–50% | 30%+ | The one case for cold |\n| Survey / feedback | 5–10% | 8%+ | See `research-builder` for question shape |\n| Onboarding, multi-step | 40–60% | 50%+ | Mandatory flow |\n\n## Before launch\n\n- [ ] Audience is warm — segmented on behaviour, prior viewers excluded\n- [ ] Subtitle leads with the benefit\n- [ ] The description names the decision the feedback informs\n- [ ] Single step, one screen\n- [ ] Button is assumptive; decline is \"remind me later\"\n- [ ] Three conversion goals set, through to participation\n- [ ] Success metric written down before launch, not after\n- [ ] Run length set — 2–4 weeks saturates a warm segment\n- [ ] **A human has signed off.** This is customer-facing\n\nA ready-to-fill guide spec, the four failure patterns with their fixes, and the\nFAQ: [references/template.md](references/template.md).\n\n## Where this sits\n\n| Skill | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `research-intake` | Decides whether the study runs at all, and how big |\n| **this skill** | How to recruit for it in product |\n| `research-builder` | Builds the guide — and the in-guide **survey** protocol, which is a different instrument: [its pendo.md](../research-builder/references/pendo.md) is two or three questions asked in place; this skill is recruitment out to a panel |\n| `research-planner` | Designs the study the panel then takes part in |\n| `adoption-check` | Whether the decision the research informed actually worked |\n",
"persona-check": "---\nname: persona-check\ndescription: >-\n  Walks a design, flow or piece of copy through a persona as a task — where they\n  hesitate, what they look for and cannot find, what they do instead — before it\n  reaches real participants. A synthetic pre-test, not a verdict. Everything it\n  produces is a hypothesis to test, never a finding. Use when someone says run\n  this past a persona, synthetic user check, pre-test this prototype, how would\n  a Scaling Up creator handle this, or check this before we recruit.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: prototype\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  output: persona-voice\n---\n\n# Persona check\n\nTake a design and walk one persona through it as a **task**: where they stop,\nwhat they look for, what they do when they cannot find it.\n\nThis is a **pre-test**. It finds the obvious breakages before a real participant\nspends a session on them, and it costs minutes. It does not tell you what users\nthink, and nothing it produces is evidence.\n\n## The rule everything else depends on\n\n**Everything this skill produces is Persona voice.** Never evidence.\n\n> Never write \"users said\" about something a persona simulation said.\n\nA persona check generates **hypotheses worth testing**. It cannot confirm one.\nIf a check and a study disagree, the study is right. Label the output, keep the\nlabel on it when it travels into a doc, and if someone quotes a persona check in\na readout as a finding, that is the failure this rule exists to prevent.\n\n## What it is not\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **`user-context` `critique`** | What the evidence *predicts* about this design. Claims, confidence, gaps |\n| **`persona-check`** | Where this *person* gets stuck walking through it |\n| **`ux-critique`** | Does the design cover the one-pager and the epic |\n| **`accessibility-review`** | WCAG blockers, focus, contrast, keyboard |\n\nRun the evidence one when you need to defend a decision. Run this one when you\nneed to find the breakages before a session. They answer different questions and\nthe second cannot do the first's job.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Ground it or do not run it.** Pull the persona from\n   [`user-personas`](../user-personas/) and the relevant claims from\n   [`user-context`](../user-context/). An ungrounded persona is a confident\n   stranger inventing a user.\n2. **Refuse outside the research.** When a question falls outside what the\n   persona set covers, say so and log it as a gap. A refusal is a finding about\n   our coverage, not a failure of the check.\n3. **One persona at a time**, named. \"Users would struggle\" means nobody did.\n4. **Tasks, not opinions.** Never \"would they like this?\" — always \"what do they\n   do on this screen, and what happens when it is not there?\"\n5. **Rank by consequence**, not by how many things you found. Ten observations\n   with no ordering is a list, not a review.\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Pick the persona, and say why that one\n- [ ] Pull what we know about them here\n- [ ] Set the task and the entry state\n- [ ] Walk it, step by step\n- [ ] Rank what broke\n- [ ] Hand the top few to the real study\n```\n\n### 1 — Pick the persona\n\nOne, named, from the reference six. Say why: *who does this design have to\nsatisfy first, and who would it fail most expensively?*\n\nThose are often different people. If they are, run the second one too — the\ndesign usually survives its intended audience.\n\n### 2 — Pull what we know\n\nFrom `user-context`: the claims for this persona and this domain, with their\nconfidence. From `user-personas`: their JTBD, workflow and the stated gaps.\n\n**What is thin here matters more than what is solid.** A check grounded in LOW\nclaims produces LOW hypotheses, and should say so up front.\n\n### 3 — Set the task\n\nA real one, in their words, with the state they arrive in:\n\n> *\"You have just published a video. You want the affiliate link for the product\n> you mentioned. You are on your phone.\"*\n\nEntry state carries most of the weight — logged out, mid-task, interrupted, on a\nphone, first time, fourth time this week. The same screen is a different design\ndepending on how someone reaches it.\n\n### 4 — Walk it\n\nStep by step, in their voice, per\n[references/walkthrough.md](references/walkthrough.md). At each step:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Looking for** | What they expect to see |\n| **See** | What is actually there |\n| **Do** | Where they click, or stall |\n| **Carry** | What question they take to the next step unanswered |\n\nThat last one is where real abandonment comes from — not one bad screen, but\nthree screens that each left something unresolved.\n\n### 5 — Rank what broke\n\nFour levels, in [references/walkthrough.md](references/walkthrough.md#ranking):\n**stops them · costs them · annoys them · noticed**.\n\nStops-them items go to the study. Noticed items go in a list and no further.\n\n### 6 — Hand it on\n\nThe top two or three become **things to watch for** in the real sessions, not\nfindings in a report. Give them to\n[`research-planner`](../research-planner/) as probes, so the study tests the\nhypotheses instead of rediscovering them.\n\n## Output\n\n```markdown\n# Persona check — [design]\nPersona: [name, tier] · Task: [one line] · Entry: [state]\n**Persona voice — hypotheses to test, not findings**\n\n## Grounding\n| Claim | Confidence | n |\n|---|---|---|\n_Thin where: [what we do not know about this persona here]_\n\n## Walkthrough\n| Step | Looking for | See | Do | Carry |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## What broke\n| | What | Why it matters to them |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Stops** | | |\n| **Costs** | | |\n| **Annoys** | | |\n\n## Watch for in the real sessions\n1. …\n\n## Outside what we know\n[questions the persona set cannot answer — log as gaps]\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [references/walkthrough.md](references/walkthrough.md) — running the walk, and the ranking\n",
"prototype-handoff": "---\nname: prototype-handoff\ndescription: >-\n  Packages a chosen Ideate direction into a Prototype handoff pack after\n  ux-critique: decision, mock links, coverage report, open questions, DS notes,\n  and epic/one-pager links. Use when the designer is happy with mocks and ready\n  to leave Ideate for Prototype.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: ideate\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Solution Ideator\n---\n\n# Prototype handoff (Ideate → Prototype)\n\nFormal exit from Ideate. Runs **after** the designer is happy with mocks and\n**after** `ux-critique` (or re-runs critique if missing / stale).\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Require a recent **`ux-critique`** with no open Critical gaps, or documented designer acceptance of residual risk.\n2. Do not invent a “chosen” direction — designer must name it.\n3. Label Verified links vs AI summary of the pack.\n4. Do **not** auto-start Prototype scaffolder; stop after the pack for designer confirm.\n\nSee [references/pack.md](references/pack.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Designer: “happy with these mocks — hand off to Prototype”\n- Need a durable pack for Prototype Scaffolder / eng / PM\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\n| Source | How |\n|---|---|\n| Chosen mocks | Figma / FigJam / Make links from designer |\n| Last `ux-critique` | Chat output or re-run if missing |\n| Framing | One-pager/PRD, epic, tickets |\n| DS notes | `design-system` if UI-bound |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Gate** — Confirm designer-named direction + `ux-critique` status.\n2. **Assemble pack** — Decision, rejected alternatives (1 line each), annotated mock links, coverage summary, open questions / deferred scope, DS reuse/gaps, framing links.\n3. **Designer review** — Show pack; adjust on feedback.\n4. **Stop** — On confirm, Ideate is done. Next: the pipeline invokes\n   `ux-prototyping` **in this harness** (Cursor, Claude, or Figma Make). Do\n   not auto-start that here unless asked. Do not point them at a from-scratch\n   Vite app.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Prototype handoff — [title]\nStatus: READY FOR PROTOTYPE (pending designer confirm) | DRAFT\nAudience: Brand | Partner | Shared\n\n## Decision\n- Chosen direction:\n- Why (1–2 lines, tied to JTBD / success criteria):\n- Rejected alternatives:\n  - D#: … — rejected because …\n\n## Artifacts\n- Primary mocks / Figma:\n- Supporting FigJam / wires:\n- Annotations / flow notes:\n\n## Coverage (from ux-critique)\n- Critique date / link:\n- Critical open: none | [list + residual risk accepted: yes/no]\n- Major deferred:\n\n## Design system\n- Reuse:\n- Gaps (needs DS review):\n\n## Framing links\n- One-pager / PRD:\n- Epic / tickets:\n- Research (if any):\n\n## Open questions / out of scope for Prototype\n- …\n\n## Suggested Prototype next step\n- [ ] `ux-prototyping` in this harness (Brand or Partner live preview)\n- [ ] Designer confirmed — Ideate complete\n```\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use prototype-handoff — we picked D3. Last ux-critique is in this chat; mocks: [Figma link].`\n2. `Use prototype-handoff after ux-critique; I accept the one Major gap on empty-state copy.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Handing off without `ux-critique`\n- Multiple “chosen” directions with no primary\n- Auto-building a prototype inside this skill\n- Dropping epic / one-pager links from the pack\n",
"research-builder": "---\nname: research-builder\ndescription: >-\n  Builds the instruments a study needs that are not the Outset study itself: a\n  Pendo in-product guide, a human interview guide, the PostHog dashboard that\n  will measure the outcome, and the audience — who to test with, drawn from real\n  feature usage rather than a guess. Use when someone says build the dashboard,\n  write the interview guide, set up a Pendo survey, who should we test with, find\n  me partners who use this feature, or build the measurement before we run.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  builds: pendo-guide|interview-guide|posthog-dashboard|audience\n---\n\n# Research builder\n\nFour instruments. **`research-planner` designs the study and builds the Outset\none** — this builds the rest, and the one that matters most is the dashboard,\nbecause it gets built *before* the study runs.\n\n| Build | When |\n|---|---|\n| **PostHog dashboard** | Always, if the question touches behaviour. First |\n| **Audience** | Always. Who, from real usage |\n| **Pendo guide** | In-product reach, large n, people you cannot recruit |\n| **Interview guide** | Human sessions — SPRINT follow-ups, DEEP fieldwork |\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Measurement first.** Build the dashboard before recruiting. If the event\n   does not exist you have found that out for free, instead of after fifteen\n   sessions.\n2. **An uninstrumented event returns zero**, which reads identically to nobody\n   doing the thing. Never report a zero you have not confirmed is real.\n3. **Target on behaviour, not on self-description.** \"People who used this\n   feature twice last month\" beats \"people who say they use it\".\n4. **Draft, don't ship.** Guides and dashboards are drafts until a human has\n   read them. Never publish a Pendo guide to live traffic without explicit\n   sign-off — it is customer-facing.\n5. **Name who you excluded.** A sample is defined as much by who is missing.\n\n## 1 — The PostHog dashboard\n\n**Do this first, before any recruiting.** Full protocol:\n[references/dashboard.md](references/dashboard.md).\n\n1. **Name the measure** the study is meant to move, in events: what fires, on\n   what surface, by whom.\n2. **Check the schema** — `read-data-schema` — before building anything. This is\n   the step that pays for the whole skill.\n3. **Build it** — the funnel or trend, plus the breakdown that will matter, plus\n   a baseline window *before* the change.\n4. **Record the baseline figure** as a claim in the Frame, dated, so the\n   after-reading has something honest to compare against.\n\n> If the decisive event is not instrumented, **stop and raise an instrumentation\n> ticket.** No study design recovers data that was never recorded, and a study\n> run anyway will produce a confident answer to a question it cannot see.\n\n## 2 — The audience\n\nWho to test with, drawn from what people actually do. Full recipes:\n[references/audience.md](references/audience.md).\n\nFive axes, used together:\n\n| Axis | From |\n|---|---|\n| **Side** | Partner or brand |\n| **Persona** | [`user-personas`](../user-personas/) — never invented here |\n| **Size** | Partner tier, brand partner-count band, DAU tier |\n| **Feature usage** | PostHog: has done X, n times, in the last N days |\n| **Recency** | Active, lapsed, never — three different studies |\n\n**Build the cohort in PostHog, hand it to Rally for recruitment.** Usage is the\naxis teams skip and it is the one that decides whether the sessions are worth\nwatching: a study about a feature, recruited from people who have never opened\nit, measures first impressions and nothing else. Sometimes that is the question\n— say so if it is.\n\nThen check it against **minimum viable diversity**\n([`research-planner`](../research-planner/references/rigor.md)): eight people who\nall look the same give you one story told eight times, and it will feel like\nconsensus.\n\n## 3 — The Pendo guide\n\nIn-product, large n, reaches people you could never recruit — and interrupts\nsomeone mid-task, which is the cost. Full guidance:\n[references/pendo.md](references/pendo.md).\n\n- **Two or three questions.** This is an interruption, not a study\n- Target by segment and page, never site-wide\n- Ask about **the thing they are doing right now** — that is the whole advantage\n- Set a response cap and an end date before it goes live\n- Expect single-digit response rates and a satisfaction skew: the annoyed answer\n  and the delighted answer, with the middle missing\n\n**Draft only.** A Pendo guide is customer-facing; it ships when a human says so.\n\n## 4 — The interview guide\n\nFor SPRINT follow-ups and DEEP fieldwork. Full structure:\n[references/interview.md](references/interview.md).\n\nOpens with warm-up, moves to **artefacts and tasks before opinions**, and ends\nwith the one question worth asking everyone. Probes are written down so two\ninterviewers get comparable data.\n\nThe rules in [`research-planner`](../research-planner/references/prompts.md)\napply here hardest: test the thing not the idea, no hypotheticals without a\nstimulus, no yes/no comprehension questions. A human moderator can follow a\nthread an AI cannot — that is the point of the format, and it is wasted on a\nscript that only collects agreement.\n\n## Handoff\n\n| Built | Goes to |\n|---|---|\n| Dashboard + baseline claim | The Frame, via `user-context` `record` |\n| Audience cohort | Rally, for recruitment |\n| Pendo guide | A human, for sign-off, then Pendo |\n| Interview guide | `research-planner`, for the 3-participant pass |\n\nNothing here launches anything. Building is not running.\n\n## References\n\n- [references/dashboard.md](references/dashboard.md) — measurement before the study\n- [references/audience.md](references/audience.md) — targeting on real usage\n- [references/pendo.md](references/pendo.md) — in-product guides\n- [references/interview.md](references/interview.md) — human session guides\n",
"research-intake": "---\nname: research-intake\ndescription: >-\n  Research request front door for impact.com UX. Four questions, two checks,\n  one route: is there a decision, do we already know the answer, and if not —\n  MICRO (48 hours), SPRINT (1–2 weeks), or DEEP (4+ weeks), run by research or\n  by the team that asked. Use when someone files or asks for research, or says\n  research request, research intake, triage this, route this study, scope this\n  research, is this micro sprint or deep, or asks UX Research to look into\n  something.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  position: entry\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  modes: micro|sprint|deep\n---\n\n# Research intake\n\nThe front door for research requests. **Four questions, two checks, one route.**\n\nThis is deliberately light. It is a v1 meant to be used and then corrected — if\na step here feels like paperwork, cut it and say what you cut. Weight gets added\nlater, from evidence that something was missed, not up front in case it is.\n\nModes come from Constantine Papas, *AI-Powered UX Research*, ch. 4 — as does\nthe Frame, which is his term for what the organisation believes, how confident\nit is, and where the gaps are. The question of who *runs* the work comes from\nLozé's bronze/silver/gold model, kept as a separate question here because effort\nand ownership come apart more often than one label allows.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Check before you plan.** A request that is already answered does not need\n   a method.\n2. **No decision, no study.** Research with nothing waiting on it becomes a\n   document nobody reads.\n3. **Speed never lowers the mode.** Say the conflict out loud; let the requester\n   choose. Shrinking method to fit a deadline produces confident fiction.\n4. **Draft the ticket, don't create it.** Show it first.\n5. **\"Already answered\" is a win.** Log it. It is the cheapest research we do.\n\n## Who runs intake\n\n**One named person at a time, checking once a day.** Default: Nihal Somers. If\nthey cannot, they hand it to someone **by name** — never back to the queue.\n\nDaily is not ceremony, it is what makes the 48-hour MICRO promise true. Triage\ntwice a week and a request filed on Tuesday gets routed Friday, so the clock is\nblown before anyone starts. Most days the check takes a minute.\n\nThis section exists because a process nobody owns is exactly the thing Check 1\nrejects: work with no one waiting on it, which becomes a document nobody reads.\nA triage step that runs when someone remembers is not a triage step.\n\n## The four questions\n\nAsk these, batched, in whatever words fit the conversation.\n\n| | Ask | Gives you |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **What do you want to know?** | The question |\n| 2 | **What decision does it inform, and who makes it?** | Check 1 |\n| 3 | **When do you need it, and how long does the answer need to hold?** | The constraint |\n| 4 | **What happens if we get this wrong?** | The routing axis |\n\nThat is the whole form. Do not add fields — ambiguity and domain are yours to\njudge, not theirs to fill in, and every extra question costs you a request that\nnever gets filed.\n\nQuestion 3 is two halves on purpose. *When you need it* is a deadline. *How long\nit must hold* is shelf life. A copy test decided Friday and stale next quarter is\nfine; a two-week study whose answer expires in one is not.\n\nQuestion 4 asks about **consequence, not cost**. Ask \"how much rides on it\" and\nyou get an answer about budget or headcount — what it costs to find out, which\ntells you nothing about how bad a wrong answer would be. A cheap decision can be\nexpensive to get wrong. Listen for whether it is reversible.\n\nIf they named a Jira key, fetch it (`getJiraIssue`) before asking them to paste\nanything.\n\n## Check 1 — Is there a decision?\n\nOne question, asked of the requester:\n\n> **If the answer comes back the opposite of what you expect, what changes?**\n\nA real answer means there is a decision and someone owns it. No answer means one\nof two things, and both are conversations rather than rejections:\n\n| What you're hearing | Say |\n|---|---|\n| The call is already made | Offer to pressure-test it instead, or to look at it after they ship |\n| It's a design choice, not a question about users | Reframe it once — \"should we add a confirmation screen?\" becomes \"do partners believe their submission worked without one?\" |\n\nReply template: [references/templates.md](references/templates.md).\n\n## Check 2 — Do we already know?\n\nLook before you plan. Proportionate to the ask — a 48-hour question gets a\n15-minute look, not a full audit.\n\n| Where | For |\n|---|---|\n| **The Frame** — [claims + needs](https://claude.ai/artifact/FHc7oAPoyohgxo87VdxCGw) | Do we already believe something about this? **Always first.** |\n| **Dovetail** | Have we interviewed on this? |\n| **Outset** | Has a study already run — or is one running right now? |\n| **PostHog** | Only if the question is behavioural |\n\n**Read the Frame before touching a connector.** It is one `ArtifactData` query,\ninstant, already graded, and it records what was unanswerable last time — the\nsingle most useful thing to know before planning a study. Filter `claims` by\ndomain and segment; check `needs` for whether this is already a named gap.\nSkipping it is how you spend five API calls rediscovering something a colleague\nwrote down.\n\nA **contested** claim does not close a request, whatever its confidence. Route it\nand say which two readings disagree.\n\n**A partial check is provisional, and provisional does not get routed on.** If a\nsource is unavailable, say which half you ran, give the verdict as provisional,\nand stop. Do not pick a mode.\n\nThis is not caution for its own sake. One source will hand you a plausible,\nconfident answer — and the source you skipped is disproportionately the one that\ndisagrees, because a second source only changes the verdict when it says\nsomething different. A half-run check is not a weaker version of the full one;\nit is a different answer that looks identical.\n\n**Budget about five calls and a few minutes** for the full check: briefs, a\nDovetail search, then Outset search → project → reports → topline. It is not a\nglance. Saying so is what stops it being quietly skipped.\n\nInvoke `user-context` in `ask` mode when you want the full version — it sweeps\nevery source properly, with counter-evidence, and records what it finds back\ninto the Frame.\n\nThree verdicts, not five grades:\n\n| Verdict | Means | Do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Solid** | Clear evidence, right segment, still current | **Close.** Send the link. |\n| **Thin** | Right area, wrong segment, or ageing | Route — but the scope just got smaller |\n| **Nothing** | No coverage of this area at all | Route in full. If the *area* is unknown, that is a DEEP signal. |\n\n**Solid has a bar, because Solid closes tickets.** It means you could put the\nfinding in front of the decision owner and defend it:\n\n1. It covers **the segment they asked about** — not an adjacent one\n2. It **states how many people** it is based on\n3. **Nothing has changed since** that would undermine it — no relevant ship, no\n   pricing change, and not simply old\n\nMiss any of the three and it is **Thin**. Thin is not a failure; it still cuts\nthe scope, often by a lot.\n\n**The bar scales with consequence.** If question 4 said the decision is big or\nhard to undo, one source is not enough — Solid then needs two that agree and\nwere gathered independently. A reversible decision can rest on one good study;\nan irreversible one should not.\n\nA study already in flight routes to **wait**, not to a second study.\n\n## Step 3 — Route\n\n### First — can it be answered at all?\n\nBefore picking a mode, check the question does not turn on something nobody\nmeasures. If the decisive evidence would come from an event that is not\ninstrumented, a funnel that does not exist, or a field nobody fills in, **no\namount of research fixes that.**\n\nThe route is **instrument first, then a MICRO** — an instrumentation ticket, and\na small study once the data exists. Say it plainly: the question is good, the\nmeasurement is missing, and running a study now would produce opinion dressed as\nevidence.\n\nThis is a real outcome, not a failure to route. It is also the one the three\nmodes cannot express, because all of them assume research is the thing standing\nin the way.\n\n### Then — what shape\n\n| | MICRO | SPRINT | DEEP |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Turnaround** | 48 hours | 1–2 weeks | 4+ weeks |\n| **Ambiguity** | We understand the problem | Mostly | We don't |\n| **Consequence** | Small or reversible | Material | Big or hard to undo |\n| **Shape** | One question | 1 central + 2–4 sub | Open-ended |\n| **Method** | Persona chat, data query, one Outset study | Outset study + 2–5 human follow-ups | Contextual inquiry, 15–20 participants |\n| **The Frame** | Uses what we know | Fills gaps in it | **Builds it** |\n\n**Score ambiguity and consequence separately; the higher one wins.** Not both\ntogether — a clear problem with a big consequence is still DEEP.\n\n**Ambiguity wins outright.** If we cannot state the problem, it is DEEP whatever\nelse is true. A 48-hour study against a question nobody can phrase buys\nconfidence, not knowledge.\n\n**Stuck between MICRO and SPRINT?** Ask whether one Outset study answers it\nalone. Yes → MICRO. Needs human follow-up to interpret → SPRINT.\n\n### Who runs it\n\n> **Can the person who asked run this, with support?**\n\n| | Who | Research does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Team-led** | The designer, PM, or CX person who asked | Agrees the method, looks at the guide, reads the analysis |\n| **Research-led** | Us | All of it |\n\nMost MICRO and a good share of SPRINT can be team-led. DEEP is research-led —\nfoundational work is where the Frame comes from.\n\nThis question is the one that stops the queue becoming a bottleneck. Ask it\nevery time, even when the answer is obvious.\n\n### Timing\n\nThe deadline is a **constraint, not an input**. It never lowers the mode. When\nthe mode needs longer than they have, offer three things and let them pick:\n\n1. Move the date.\n2. Narrow the question until a faster mode honestly answers it.\n3. Take a quick read now, labelled as directional and not decision-grade.\n\nOption 3 is legitimate. Running option 3 while calling it the full study is not.\n\n## Step 4 — Ticket and handoff\n\nDraft the ticket ([references/templates.md](references/templates.md)), show it,\ncreate it on confirm.\n\n**Where it goes:** Jira project `UX`, [board 679](https://impact.atlassian.net/jira/software/projects/UX/boards/679).\nType **Research** or **Analytics** — the *method* picks it, not the mode.\nAssignee **Nihal Somers** unless someone else is taking it.\n\n| Mode | Next |\n|---|---|\n| **Instrument first** | Instrumentation ticket to the squad; a MICRO once the data lands |\n| MICRO | `user-context`, `data-analyze`, or one Outset study |\n| SPRINT | `user-research` — the study plus the follow-up calls |\n| DEEP | `user-research` — contextual inquiry, then recruit |\n\nTeam-led work hands to the requester with the three support points agreed.\nIntake recommends; the research lead decides.\n\n## Re-routing\n\nRouting once at intake is a guess made at the moment of least information. Three\nsignals say re-route, and any of them is enough:\n\n- The same pattern keeps turning up across studies or teams\n- We can see *what* people do but not *why*\n- People read the findings differently and argue about what they mean\n\nThe usual case: a usability test reveals people don't understand what the thing\nis for, and a design question turns strategic. That is a success, not a scope\nfailure — say so, and re-route it.\n\n## References\n\n- [references/routing.md](references/routing.md) — the three modes in full, and\n  two bugs in the original routing spec that this version fixes\n- [references/templates.md](references/templates.md) — reply templates and the\n  ticket template\n\n## What to watch\n\nOne number, and it costs nothing to collect: **how often you close a request as\nalready answered.** If it rises, the Frame is compounding and intake is paying\nfor itself. If it stays at zero, either nothing is being reused or Check 2 is\nbeing skipped — both worth knowing.\n\nEverything else — cycle time, rejection mix, capacity — wait until there is a\nreason to want it.\n",
"research-planner": "---\nname: research-planner\ndescription: >-\n  Turns a routed research request into a study that is actually worth running.\n  Applies question hygiene, sets go/no-go thresholds, designs prompts that test\n  the thing rather than the idea, picks a sample and its diversity, builds the\n  study, runs the 3-participant rigor pass before the rest launches, and updates\n  the UX ticket as it goes. Use after research-intake routes a request, or when\n  someone says plan a study, design the research, write the discussion guide,\n  set up the Outset study, or draft a screener.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  picks-up-from: research-intake\n---\n\n# Research planner\n\nTakes a **routed** request and makes it a study worth running.\n\nIntake decided *whether* and *what shape*. This decides *how* — and the gap\nbetween those is where most research quietly fails, by testing the idea of a\nthing instead of the thing, or by discovering at participant 12 that the question\nwas wrong at participant 1.\n\n> Question hygiene, prompt design, the 3-participant pass and sample guidance\n> follow Constantine Papas, *AI-Powered UX Research*, chs. 7–8 and the Outliers\n> Booking keynote.\n\n## Where it sits\n\n```\nresearch-intake ──routed──→ research-planner ──findings──→ user-context (record)\n     creates ticket             updates ticket              writes the claim\n```\n\n**Intake owns the ticket's triage fields; this skill owns the plan fields and the\ntransitions.** Never rewrite intake's sections — the record of *why this was\nrouted* has to survive the study.\n\n| Writes | |\n|---|---|\n| Intake | Question · Decision · What we already know · Route · Who runs it |\n| **Planner** | Method · sub-questions · sample · probes · thresholds · pass result |\n\nOnly **routed** requests arrive here. Closed, reframed, instrument-first and\nprovisional outcomes end at intake.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Draft, don't decide.** This skill writes the first version of everything —\n   design, screener, guide, probes. A human finalises, sets the boundaries, and\n   owns what goes to participants.\n2. **Thresholds before fieldwork.** \"If 70%+ say X we build\" is written *before*\n   anyone is recruited. Deciding what counts after you've seen the data is how\n   research confirms whatever was already wanted.\n3. **The 3-participant pass is not optional.** No mode skips it. It is the\n   difference between fixing a broken study at participant 3 and discovering it\n   at participant 15.\n4. **Test the thing, not the idea of the thing.**\n5. **Never launch recruitment without the research lead saying so.**\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Pick up the ticket\n- [ ] Question hygiene — sharpen until it is answerable\n- [ ] Thresholds — what finding triggers what decision\n- [ ] Sample — size and diversity\n- [ ] Design — prompts, probes, screener, guide\n- [ ] Human review — boundaries set, leading questions removed\n- [ ] Build it\n- [ ] 3-participant pass\n- [ ] Launch the rest\n- [ ] Readout → hand to user-context\n```\n\n### 1 — Pick up the ticket\n\nRead the intake ticket (`getJiraIssue`). You need the scoped question, the mode,\nthe decision and its owner, the date, and **what the Frame already covered** —\nthat last one narrows scope and stops you re-asking a settled question.\n\n### 2 — Question hygiene\n\nFive things must be true before a question reaches a participant. Full checklist\nand the anti-patterns: [references/hygiene.md](references/hygiene.md).\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **The question** | Specific, not \"do users like this?\" |\n| **The decision** | Named. No decision, no research |\n| **The decision owner** | A person who has agreed to wait for the answer |\n| **The outcomes** | What finding triggers what decision — **before** fieldwork |\n| **The boundaries** | What's in scope, and explicitly what isn't |\n\nIntake checked that a decision exists. This checks the question can actually\nanswer it.\n\n> **Anti-pattern:** \"we're doing research to understand X.\"\n> **Pattern:** \"we're doing research because we need to decide whether to build X\n> by 1 September.\"\n\n### 3 — Sample\n\nConcrete numbers, not \"enough interviews\":\n\n| Mode | Target | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MICRO** | 8–15 | Min 5 if patterns are obvious by 3; diminishing returns past 15 |\n| **SPRINT** | 15–20 Outset, **5** human follow-ups | Follow-ups on days 7–10 |\n| **DEEP** | 12–15 | Min 8 if homogeneous; 5–7 per segment if multi-segment |\n\n**Minimum viable diversity**, not representativeness. You need *variation* to\ncatch edge cases and common paths — eight identical admins give you one story\ntold eight times. See [references/rigor.md](references/rigor.md).\n\n### 4 — Design\n\nWhere most studies are won or lost. Principles with worked\nanti-pattern/pattern pairs: [references/prompts.md](references/prompts.md).\n\n- **Test the thing, not the idea.** Not \"would you use bulk import?\" but \"here's\n  the screen and a CSV — what would you do?\"\n- **No hypothetical futures** without a prototype in front of them. People are\n  bad at predicting their own behaviour and generous about yours.\n- **Probes specific enough for consistent AI follow-up.** \"Ask about their\n  workflow\" produces a different conversation per participant and uncomparable\n  data. Tighter in MICRO, where there is no second chance; looser in SPRINT,\n  where human follow-ups adjust on the fly.\n- **No yes/no questions.** \"Do you understand this?\" invites people to avoid\n  admitting failure. Ask them to *do* or *recall* something instead.\n\nDraft the screener, the guide and the probes. Then stop — a human reviews for\nleading questions, scope creep and domain nuance before anything is built.\n\n### 5 — Build it\n\nCreate the study, sections, screener and quotas. Configure recruitment. **Do not\nlaunch.** Validate, show the research lead what will go out, and wait.\n\n**For an Outset study**, follow [references/outset.md](references/outset.md):\nunder five minutes, 4–8 questions, mostly closed-ended, gated hard. That file\nalso carries the call it is easy to get wrong — whether this study is measuring\n**how much** (quant, closed-ended) or **what people do** (behavioural, tasks).\nA quant study cannot show you a workaround; a behavioural one cannot give you a\npercentage. Picking wrong is how a study produces a number nobody can act on.\n\nDemographics come from the screener and Rally, never from questions that eat the\nrespondent's five minutes.\n\n### 6 — The 3-participant pass\n\n**Review the first three end-to-end before the rest runs.** Per mode:\n[references/rigor.md](references/rigor.md).\n\nRead the raw transcripts, not the AI summary. Then decide: **proceed, fix, or\npivot.** Record which, and why, on the ticket.\n\nThis is the single cheapest piece of rigor available. It is not gatekeeping — it\nis staying curious long enough to notice the study is asking the wrong thing.\n\n### 7 — Launch the rest\n\nOnly after the pass, and only on the research lead's go.\n\n### 8 — Readout\n\nFour questions, in order — including the one most readouts omit. Structure:\n[references/readout.md](references/readout.md).\n\n1. What did you ask?\n2. What did you find?\n3. What does it mean for the decision?\n4. **What can't it tell you?**\n\nState findings as **observations, not recommendations.** \"6/8 admins mentioned\nbulk import; current workaround is one-by-one creation\" lets the reader decide.\n\"We should build bulk import\" is your opinion wearing research's clothes.\n\nThen hand the claims to `user-context` in `record` mode. **A study that doesn't\nwrite back doesn't compound** — and transition the ticket closed.\n\n## References\n\n- [references/hygiene.md](references/hygiene.md) — the five checks, thresholds\n- [references/prompts.md](references/prompts.md) — prompt design, with pairs\n- [references/outset.md](references/outset.md) — building the Outset study: shape, question types, gating, thresholds\n- [references/rigor.md](references/rigor.md) — sample sizes, diversity, the pass\n- [references/readout.md](references/readout.md) — readout structure and tone\n",
"research-synthesis": "---\nname: research-synthesis\ndescription: >-\n  Triangulates impact.com UX signals across PostHog, Pendo, Dovetail, Outset,\n  and Freshdesk into confidence-scored themes. Use for Empathize cross-source\n  synthesis, Research Insight Monitor follow-ups, or when 2+ sources may agree.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# Research synthesis — cross-source (Empathize)\n\nUse for **multi-source** triangulation. For one Dovetail project / interview set only, use `synthesize-research`.\n\n## Integrity\n\n- Cite every Verified claim with source + link or file path.\n- High confidence requires **≥2 independent sources** with **usable evidence** (not empty titles).\n- **Reject weak matches:** empty Dovetail `body`/`preview`, NPS comments that only share a keyword (e.g. “link”) but are about payments/signup, or Outset studies that are only a welcome script.\n- Separate Brand vs Partner themes.\n- Designer owns “problem worth solving.”\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md) and [references/sources.md](references/sources.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Monitor flagged an issue as `triangulated: true` — verify that claim\n- Need one themed brief before the human gate\n- After `data-analyze` finds a real drop and you need qual corroboration\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Scope** — Topic, audience, window.\n2. **Collect** — Live MCP and/or `research-agent/docs/research/latest.json`.\n3. **Normalize** — One row per signal; drop empty/irrelevant matches.\n4. **Cluster** — impact taxonomy themes when they fit.\n5. **Triangulate** — `high` / `medium` / `low` with honest source counts.\n6. **Publish draft** — Gate-ready synthesis (not stakeholder-final).\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Cross-source synthesis — [topic]\nWindow: [dates] · Audience: [Brand|Partner|Shared|All]\n\n## Executive take (AI interpretation)\n[3–5 sentences]\n\n## Themes\n\n### [Theme] — confidence: high|medium|low\n- **Problem statement (draft):** …\n- **Verified evidence:**\n  - PostHog: … (N=, link)\n  - Pendo: “…” (only if on-topic)\n  - Dovetail: “…” (non-empty quote + link)\n  - Outset: … (study name + what it actually tested)\n- **Discarded matches (not evidence):** … (empty DT / off-topic NPS)\n- **Open questions:** …\n\n## Ready for human gate?\n- High-confidence themes:\n- Need fieldwork (`user-research`):\n- Suggested next: `user-research` | `data-analyze` | Define handoff\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use research-synthesis on UPS Step 2 (details view → send proposal). Is triangulation real or keyword noise?`\n2. `Use research-synthesis for Partner Link & Content Tools using the latest research pack + live PostHog if needed.`\n3. `Use research-synthesis: Login email→password drop — what evidence survives quality filters?`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Counting empty Dovetail notes toward high confidence\n- Mixing Brand and Partner pain into one “users say” blob\n- Promoting monitor `triangulated: true` without re-checking evidence quality\n",
"study-summary": "---\nname: study-summary\ndescription: >-\n  Takes a pile of raw material, transcripts, PDFs, CSV exports, survey dumps,\n  PostHog numbers, and works out what we currently know about a topic. Leads\n  with what people said. Honest about what the material cannot tell you. Use\n  when someone says here are the files, what do we know, make sense of this, or\n  summarise where we are on this.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  input: raw-material\n---\n\n# Study summary\n\nSomeone hands you a folder. Transcripts, a CSV, a PDF deck from last year, a\nPostHog export. The question is always the same: what do we actually know here?\n\nThis reads all of it and says so, in plain words, with the gaps left visible.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## How to write\n\nOpen with what people said and how they felt. Quote them. The counts come after,\nto show how widespread something was.\n\nKeep it short. A summary that runs longer than the attention someone has for it\nhas failed, however thorough it is.\n\nSay what the material cannot tell you. That is not a disclaimer at the end, it\nis part of the answer.\n\nNever fill a gap with a plausible sentence. If three files disagree, say they\ndisagree.\n\n## What you can take in\n\n| Type | Read it for |\n|---|---|\n| Interview transcripts | What people said, in their words. The best material you will get |\n| Survey or CSV exports | How common something is. Check the open text, not just the counts |\n| PDF decks and docs | What someone already concluded, and when |\n| PostHog | Whether behaviour matches what people told you |\n| Support tickets | What breaks often enough that people complain |\n\nAsk for what is missing before you start. If there are no transcripts, say the\nsummary will be thin on why, because nothing in the rest of the pile answers it.\n\n## Working through it\n\n### 1. Sort by age and type\n\nList what you have, with dates. Anything over a year old gets read as history,\nnot as current state, and you should say so when you quote it.\n\nA deck from last year that disagrees with interviews from last month is not a\ncontradiction. It is a change, and that is worth more than either file alone.\n\n### 2. Read the words first\n\nTranscripts and open text before numbers. Note what people say, where their tone\nshifts, what they do instead when something does not work, and anything that\nsurprised you.\n\nPull quotes as you go, with who said it and roughly when. A summary without\nverbatim reads like someone else's opinion.\n\n### 3. Then the numbers\n\nUse quant to say how common, never to say why. Check that an event or question\nmeans what you think before you lean on it.\n\nIf a number cannot be checked, leave it out or mark it clearly as unverified.\n\n### 4. Find what disagrees\n\nDeliberately look for the file that cuts against the emerging story. If you did\nnot find one, say where you looked.\n\nTwo sources agreeing is worth more than one source with a big sample. Say which\nyou have.\n\n### 5. Write it\n\n```markdown\n# What we know about [topic]\nBased on [what you read] · [dates] · written [date]\n\n## The short version\n[Four or five lines. What people experience and how they feel about it.]\n\n## What people told us\n**[Finding in plain words]**\n> \"[quote]\" [who, when]\n[How common. What cuts against it.]\n\n## What the behaviour shows\n[Only where it adds something. Skip this if there is nothing solid.]\n\n## What we don't know\n[The honest list. Who we didn't hear from, what the material can't answer,\nwhere two sources disagree and nobody has settled it.]\n\n## How much to trust this\n[One or two lines. Thin, mixed, or solid, and why.]\n```\n\nThe \"what we don't know\" section is the one that makes the rest usable. Without\nit a reader assumes you covered everything.\n\n## Being honest about thin material\n\nSay it plainly at the top when it applies.\n\n- \"This is six interviews from one segment. It tells you about them and nothing\n  about brands.\"\n- \"The only recent material here is a survey. We have numbers and almost no\n  explanation.\"\n- \"Two of these files disagree and nobody has reconciled them. I have kept both.\"\n\nThat is more useful than a confident summary that falls apart when someone\nchecks a source.\n\n## When you are done\n\nOffer to record what you found through [`user-context`](../user-context/) so the\nnext person asking finds it, and point anything genuinely unanswered at\n[`research-intake`](../research-intake/).\n\nIf the pile turned out to answer the question already, say so clearly. That is a\ngood outcome and it saves a study.\n",
"synthesize-research": "---\nname: synthesize-research\ndescription: >-\n  Affinity-maps a single research corpus (one Dovetail project, interview set,\n  or study export) into themes and insight cards. Use for Empathize single-source\n  synthesis — not cross-tool triangulation (use research-synthesis for that).\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# Synthesize research — single corpus (Empathize)\n\nAffinity / thematic synthesis for **one** dataset. For PostHog + Pendo + Dovetail together, use `research-synthesis`.\n\n## Integrity\n\n- Quotes must be Verified from the corpus with IDs/links when available.\n- Skip empty notes; do not invent quote text from titles alone.\n- Note N and selection bias; max confidence = **medium** for a single corpus.\n- Mark narrative as **AI interpretation**.\n- Human gate still applies.\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md) and [references/themes.md](references/themes.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- One Dovetail project / highlight set\n- One Outset study transcript or notes dump\n- Designer pastes interview notes and wants affinity themes\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Ingest** — Load one corpus; record N.\n2. **Open code** — Atomic observations (blocker, workaround, desire…).\n3. **Affinity** — 4–8 themes; prefer impact taxonomy labels when they fit.\n4. **Insight cards** — observation + quotes + frequency + audience + implication.\n5. **Handoff** — If quant needed → `data-analyze`; if multi-source → `research-synthesis`.\n\n## Insight card template\n\n```markdown\n### Insight: [short name]\n- **Audience:** Brand | Partner | Shared\n- **Observation:** …\n- **Evidence (Verified):**\n  - “…” — id/link\n- **Frequency in corpus:** n / N\n- **Implication for product:** … (AI interpretation)\n- **Confidence:** low | medium\n- **Action for designer:** …\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use synthesize-research on this Dovetail highlight about commission details findability (paste quote + URL). Affinity for Partner proposals.`\n2. `Use synthesize-research on the Brand Marketplace Discovery Outset study summary — themes only, no PostHog.`\n3. `Use synthesize-research on these Partner contextual-inquiry notes I pasted — blockers vs desires.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Inflating one quote into a roadmap item\n- Cross-walking to PostHog inside this skill\n- Treating study welcome scripts as findings\n",
"talk-to-users": "---\nname: talk-to-users\ndescription: >-\n  The Ideate-stage way in to the people you are designing for. Frames what you\n  actually want to know, then points you at the right place. The persona set to\n  talk to, the Frame for what the evidence says, or a real study when neither\n  will do. It generates no personas of its own. Use when someone says I want to\n  talk to users, what would a partner think, run this past someone, or who am I\n  designing this for.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: ideate\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  generates: nothing\n---\n\n# Talk to users\n\nIn Ideate you want a user in the room. This gets you to one, or tells you\nstraight that we do not have one for this question.\n\n**It generates nothing.** There is one persona voice at impact.com, in\n[`user-personas`](../user-personas/), and one body of evidence, in\n[`user-context`](../user-context/). A second place to produce a user-shaped\nanswer would become the place people go when they want a friendlier one.\n\n**Answer short.** Lead with what changes, not with everything you found. A few\nlines, then one specific follow-up they can ask for — the detail stays\navailable, it just doesn't arrive unasked. A number earns its place by changing\na decision; the rest belongs in the artifact, where someone can go and find it.\n\n## Four ways in, and they answer different questions\n\n| You want | Go to | You get |\n|---|---|---|\n| To hear how someone talks about this | [`user-personas`](../user-personas/), the Ask panel | Persona voice, grounded in the research behind that card |\n| To know what the evidence says | [`user-context`](../user-context/) `ask` | Claims with confidence, n, and the gaps named |\n| To walk a design through someone | [`persona-check`](../persona-check/) | Where a persona gets stuck, as a task walkthrough |\n| To actually ask real people | [`research-intake`](../research-intake/) | A route, and often the answer is that we already know |\n\nMost Ideate questions want the second. People ask for the first because it feels\ncloser to a user, and the evidence is usually both closer and stronger.\n\n## The question to ask first\n\n> **What would you do differently depending on the answer?**\n\nIn Ideate the honest answer is often \"nothing yet, I am just exploring\". That is\nfine. It only changes where you go. Exploring wants the persona voice and the\nclaims, not a study. Save `research-intake` for when the answer would change\nsomething.\n\n## Routing\n\n**\"What would a Scaling Up creator think of this?\"**\n→ `user-personas`. Their cards answer from the evidence behind them and decline\nwhen a question falls outside it.\n\n**\"Do partners actually struggle with X?\"**\n→ `user-context` `ask`. This is an evidence question wearing a persona's\nclothes, and the Frame answers it better.\n\n**\"Here's a flow. Where does it break?\"**\n→ `persona-check` for where someone gets stuck, `user-context` `critique` for\nwhat the evidence predicts. Both, if it matters.\n\n**\"We've never researched this group.\"**\n→ `research-intake`. Say plainly that the persona set does not cover them, and\nthat anything a simulation says about them would be invention.\n\n## When a persona card refuses\n\n`user-personas` declines questions outside its research. **That is the system\nworking.** It means you have found an area where we have a persona and no\nevidence.\n\nDo not route around it by asking a different persona, or by rephrasing until\nsomething answers. Record it as a gap in [`user-context`](../user-context/).\nA refusal nobody writes down gets rediscovered next quarter.\n\n## What you may not do with what comes back\n\nPersona voice is **never evidence**. It generates hypotheses worth testing.\n\n- Never write *\"users said\"* about something a persona card said\n- Never put persona voice in a one-pager as a finding\n- Never let it settle an argument. If two people disagree about users, that is a\n  research question, and a made up voice will take a side with total confidence\n\nWhat it is genuinely good for: finding the question you should be asking, before\nyou spend a session finding it the expensive way.\n\n## If you want a persona we do not have\n\nSay so, and treat it as a gap rather than a gap to fill with a generated card.\n\nThere is no affiliate persona, for instance, because the screener left them out.\nThat gap is written down rather than hidden. A generated stand-in would be quoted\nwithin a week and would not survive contact with a real affiliate.\n\nThe route is [`research-intake`](../research-intake/): a new persona is DEEP\nresearch, because a persona built from nothing is the most expensive kind of\nwrong.\n",
"test-loopback": "---\nname: test-loopback\ndescription: >-\n  Publishes Verified usability-test findings to Confluence and Jira as Empathize\n  input after sessions complete. Use after Outset/Maze when the designer wants\n  findings looped back to research context — not for synthetic pre-test output.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Test Plan Generator\n---\n\n# Test loopback (Test → Empathize)\n\nCloses the design-thinking loop: **real session findings** → durable\nresearch artifacts for the next Empathize cycle. Runs **after** sessions —\nnever on AI-drafted study plans or synthetic pre-tests alone.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Require **completed sessions** (Outset/Maze export, Dovetail notes, or designer paste).\n2. Run `synthesize-research` or brief `user-research` on the corpus **before** publish.\n3. Label every finding **Verified** (quote/metric from session) vs **AI interpretation**.\n4. Do **not** promote AI synthesis to the research pack without designer confirm.\n5. **Only publish after designer confirms** the loopback summary.\n6. **Atlassian Rovo MCP** — fetch the framing epic with `getJiraIssue` / `search` yourself. Do not ask the designer to paste the ticket or run `/mcp` until a tool call fails.\n\nSee [references/publish.md](references/publish.md), [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md), and [references/atlassian-rovo.md](references/atlassian-rovo.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Outset/Maze study finished; need findings in Confluence/Jira\n- “Loop test results back to Empathize” / “publish usability findings”\n- PM/stakeholder readout prep from **Verified** session data\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\n| Source | How |\n|---|---|\n| Session corpus | Outset/Maze export, Dovetail project, pasted notes/transcripts |\n| Framing | Original one-pager, epic (`getJiraIssue` if they named a key), `test-plan` draft link |\n| Prototype | URL tested + decision under review |\n| Prior synthesis | `synthesize-research` output if already run |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Gate** — Confirm sessions are complete (not just a study draft). If only synthetic pre-tests exist, stop → not loopback-ready.\n2. **Synthesize** — Run `synthesize-research` on the session corpus (single study set). Or `user-research` for a gate brief if N is tiny.\n3. **Draft loopback pack** — Themes, Verified quotes (anonymized), task outcomes, recommendation vs kill/iterate/pivot.\n4. **Link framing** — Tie findings to original success criteria from one-pager/epic; note confirm / contradict / inconclusive.\n5. **Designer review** — Show pack; strip or relabel anything not Verified.\n6. **Publish** (on confirm only) — Confluence findings page + Jira comment or linked issue update. See [references/publish.md](references/publish.md).\n7. **Stop** — Point designer to Empathize skills (`research-synthesis`, `data-analyze`) if quant follow-up needed.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Test loopback — [title]\nStatus: DRAFT (pending designer confirm) | PUBLISHED\nStudy: Outset | Maze | mixed\nAudience: Brand | Partner | Shared\n\n## Decision this informs\n[Ship | iterate prototype | reframe problem | monitor]\n\n## Verified findings (from sessions)\n| Theme | Evidence (Verified) | vs success criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| … | quote / metric | confirm \\| contradict \\| inconclusive |\n\n## AI interpretation (review before publish)\n- …\n\n## Task outcomes\n| Task | Completion | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| … | … | … |\n\n## Recommendation\n- …\n\n## Links\n- Test plan / prototype:\n- One-pager / epic:\n- Confluence (after publish):\n- Jira (after publish):\n\n## Human gate\n- [ ] Designer confirmed Verified labels\n- [ ] Ready to publish to Confluence/Jira\n- [ ] Stakeholder summary approved (if sharing externally)\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use test-loopback on this Outset export — link to UX-1234 epic and publish draft to Confluence ux space.`\n2. `Use test-loopback after synthesize-research on Maze results — do not publish until I confirm.`\n3. `Use test-loopback: sessions done, here's Dovetail link — loop to Empathize, no stakeholder email yet.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Publishing synthetic `accessibility-review` or pre-test critique as session findings\n- Looping back without tying to original success criteria\n- Auto-updating UX Roadmap or creating duplicate epics\n- Asking the designer to paste a Jira issue or enable Atlassian Rovo before calling the MCP tools\n- Pasting participant PII into Confluence/Jira\n",
"test-plan": "---\nname: test-plan\ndescription: >-\n  Drafts Outset or Maze usability studies from a prototype plus one-pager success\n  criteria; orchestrates synthetic pre-tests before recruiting. Use in Test stage\n  after Prototype is ready, or when the designer asks for a test plan, study script,\n  or session guide (Test Plan Generator).\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: test\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Test Plan Generator\n---\n\n# Test plan (Test)\n\nOn-demand Test skill. Implements the FigJam **Test Plan Generator**:\nproblem + prototype → drafted study in **Outset** or **Maze**, after synthetic\npre-tests. Does **not** auto-publish or recruit — designer reviews first.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Require a **prototype link** (Figma, localhost shell, or Maze-ready URL).\n2. Anchor tasks and success criteria to **Verified** framing (one-pager / PRD / epic).\n3. Run synthetic pre-flight before drafting the study (see Workflow step 2).\n4. Label **Verified** vs **AI interpretation** in every output.\n5. **Stop at the human gate** — do not publish to Outset/Maze or share with stakeholders without designer confirm.\n6. After real sessions, loop findings to Empathize via `synthesize-research` — never treat this skill's draft as verified research.\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Prototype is ready; need an Outset or Maze study draft\n- “Test this prototype” / “draft usability study” / “session guide”\n- Pre-flight before scheduling participants\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\n| Source | How |\n|---|---|\n| Prototype | Figma / localhost / handoff pack URL from designer |\n| Framing | One-pager / PRD, epic, tickets, prototype-handoff pack |\n| Pre-tests | Recent `ux-critique`, `accessibility-review` (re-run if missing) |\n| Audience | Brand / Partner / Shared — drives persona skills + recruitment |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Gate** — Confirm prototype URL + framing sources. If no prototype, stop → back to Prototype.\n2. **Synthetic pre-flight** — Before drafting the study:\n   - `ux-critique` on prototype vs framing (pre-test mode — flag Critical blockers)\n   - `accessibility-review` on prototype URL (flag blockers before Maze)\n   - `brand-operator-ux-persona` / `creator-affiliate-ux-persona` when audience is Brand, Partner, or Shared\n   - Summarize blockers; if Critical a11y/coverage gaps, recommend fix before recruiting unless designer accepts risk.\n3. **Method pick** — Recommend Outset vs Maze (see [references/study-template.md](references/study-template.md)).\n4. **Draft study** — Goals, screener, tasks, success metrics, facilitator script, recruitment notes, PII handling.\n5. **Designer review** — Show full pack; adjust on feedback.\n6. **Stop** — On confirm, create an Outset **draft** study (do not publish, do not launch recruitment). Next after real sessions: `test-loopback` (or `synthesize-research` then `test-loopback`).\n\n## Outset connection\n\n| Path | What it can do |\n|---|---|\n| `OUTSET_API_KEY` (v1 REST) | List/read studies. **Cannot** create guides. |\n| Outset MCP `https://api.outset.ai/mcp/` (OAuth `studies:write`) | Create/edit draft studies, sections, questions. |\n\nWhen the designer asks to publish the test to Outset:\n\n1. Use MCP `create_project` / `create_study` / `create_section` / `create_question`.\n2. Label the study **PIPELINE TEST** if this is a pipeline demonstration.\n3. Leave the study **draft** (`validate_study` is OK). Do **not** call `publish_study`, `configure_recruitment`, or anything under the `studies:launch` scope unless the designer explicitly asks to recruit.\n4. Link the Outset URL on the Confluence plan and UX Research ticket.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Test plan — [title]\nStatus: DRAFT (pending designer review) | READY TO PUBLISH\nAudience: Brand | Partner | Shared\nMethod: Outset | Maze | hybrid\n\n## Prototype under test\n- URL:\n- Framing links (one-pager / epic):\n\n## Pre-flight summary (synthetic)\n| Check | Status | Blockers |\n|---|---|---|\n| ux-critique | pass \\| defer \\| fail | … |\n| accessibility-review | pass \\| defer \\| fail | … |\n| Persona task realism | pass \\| notes | … |\n\n## Study goals (from Verified success criteria)\n1. …\n\n## Research questions\n- …\n\n## Participants\n- Profile / screener:\n- Target N: (heuristic — state if provisional)\n- Recruitment notes:\n\n## Tasks (think-aloud)\n1. **Task:** …\n   - Success: …\n   - Probe if stuck: …\n\n## Success metrics\n- Task completion / time-on-task / SUS or custom:\n- What would confirm or kill the direction:\n\n## Facilitator script (Outset) / Maze setup notes\n- Intro:\n- Consent / recording:\n- Wrap-up questions:\n\n## Out of scope for this round\n- …\n\n## Human gate\n- [ ] Designer reviewed AI draft\n- [ ] Blockers accepted or resolved\n- [ ] Ready to publish to Outset/Maze\n\n## After sessions (loop-back)\n- `test-loopback` — publish Verified findings to Confluence/Jira (after designer confirm)\n- Or run `synthesize-research` first, then `test-loopback`\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use test-plan for [prototype link] from the one-pager success criteria — Outset, Partner audience.`\n2. `Use test-plan after prototype-handoff; run pre-flight then draft Maze study.`\n3. `Use test-plan on localhost:5173 shell — epic UX-1234; flag anything that blocks recruiting.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Publishing to Outset/Maze without designer review\n- Tasks not tied to Verified success criteria\n- Skipping pre-flight when prototype changed since last critique\n- Treating AI-drafted study results as Empathize evidence\n- Recruiting when Critical a11y blockers are open (unless risk accepted in writing)\n",
"user-context": "---\nname: user-context\ndescription: >-\n  What impact.com knows about its users, and how confident it is. Ask a question\n  about any user, surface, workflow, cohort or hypothesis and it sweeps Dovetail,\n  PostHog (including session replay), Outset, Pendo and support tickets, grades\n  what it finds, and names the gaps — then pressure-tests a design or a roadmap\n  call against that evidence. Also records new findings back into the Frame. Use\n  for \"what do we know about X\", \"has this been researched\", \"is this hypothesis\n  supported\", \"where are our blindspots\", \"what does the evidence say about this\n  mock\", \"which of these should we build first\", or to log a finding.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  modes: ask|critique|decide|record|review\n  requires-connectors: dovetail, posthog\n---\n\n# User context — the Frame\n\nThe Frame is **what we currently believe about our users, how confident we are in\neach belief, and where the gaps are.** Not what we have studied — what we know.\n\nThat distinction is the whole design. A research repository stores past projects.\nThe Frame stores *claims*: deduplicated, dated, graded, and queryable. Narrative\nis thrown away; the facts are kept.\n\nThis skill reads the Frame, and writes to it.\n\n> Constantine Papas, *AI-Powered UX Research* — the Frame. Claims-pool structure\n> after Kelly's model (Outliers Insights keynote).\n\n## Not fenced by personas\n\nAsk about **anything**: a persona, a surface, a workflow, a cohort, a support\npattern, a hypothesis. Persona is one way to slice the Frame, not the door into\nit. \"What do we know about payout failures\" is a valid question with no persona\nattached, and the answer should not begin by making you pick one.\n\n**Personas live in [`user-personas`](../user-personas/).** This skill references\nthat set and never restates it. If you need who-they-are, go there; if you need\nwhat-we-know, you are in the right place.\n\n## Modes\n\n| | Mode | For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | `ask` | What do we know about X? Sweep, grade, name the gaps |\n| **2** | `critique` | What does the evidence predict about this mock, flow or copy? |\n| **3** | `decide` | Which of these should we build, and what does the evidence support? |\n| **4** | `record` | Write a new finding into the Frame |\n| **5** | `review` | Quarterly maintenance — what changed, what's stale, what's next |\n\nModes 1–3 **read** the Frame. Modes 4–5 **maintain** it. Always name the mode you\nare in.\n\n**Talking to a persona is not here.** That lives in\n[`user-personas`](../user-personas/), whose cards answer from the evidence behind\nthem and decline when a question falls outside it. One persona voice, one owner,\none update path — a second one would become the place people go when they want a\nfriendlier answer.\n\n## Where to start\n\nPeople rarely arrive with a well-formed question. **Route what they said to a\nmode, sharpen it, and say the sharper version back** before running anything.\n\n| They say | Mode | Ask back |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"What do we know about X?\" | `ask` | Which slice — a segment, a surface, a workflow? And what decision does this unlock? |\n| \"Has anyone researched this?\" | `ask` | What would count as an answer? A near-match narrows the study rather than ending it |\n| \"Is it true that…?\" | `ask` | Restate as a falsifiable claim, then write what would kill it before searching |\n| \"Here's a mock / flow / bit of copy\" | `critique` | What is it meant to do, for whom? Critique needs the job, not just the pixels |\n| \"Should we build A or B?\" | `decide` | What decision, by when, and what happens if we get it wrong? |\n| \"Which of these matters most?\" | `decide` | Ranked for whom — which segment is this for? |\n| \"We just learned X\" | `record` | One falsifiable sentence, its n, its source, and what it does *not* cover |\n| \"Can the Frame still be trusted?\" | `review` | That is [`frame-maintenance`](../frame-maintenance/) — hand it over |\n\n**Three questions that are not for this skill**, and should be handed on rather\nthan answered:\n\n- *\"What would persona X say?\"* → [`user-personas`](../user-personas/)\n- *\"Should we research this at all?\"* → [`research-intake`](../research-intake/)\n- *\"Did it work after we shipped?\"* → [`adoption-check`](../adoption-check/)\n\n### Openers worth offering\n\nWhen someone has no question yet, offer these rather than a blank prompt. Each\none lands somewhere real, and the answer teaches what the Frame can do:\n\n- **What is contested?** The claims two readings disagree on — usually figures\n  still circulating in decks\n- **Where are we blind?** The empty coverage cells, and which has a live decision\n  behind it\n- **Which blocking needs rest on the weakest evidence?** High severity, low\n  evidence: a research job, not a design job\n- **What can we actually decide on today?** What HIGH confidence supports, and\n  what is still blocked\n- **What do we know about [domain]?** The densest domain is the easiest place to\n  see the Frame working\n\nThe Frame page carries the same openers as buttons, generated from its current\nstate, so the two stay in step.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Never fabricate.** If the sweep found nothing, say **\"data not available.\"**\n   A plausible answer with no source behind it is the one failure that looks\n   identical to success.\n2. **Label every line** — **Verified** (source + link + N), **Inferred**\n   (reasoning from verified evidence), or **Persona voice** (what a persona\n   simulation said). Persona voice is never evidence; it generates hypotheses.\n3. **Confidence travels with the claim.** Never a bare statement — always\n   `[claim] + [confidence] + [n] + [evidence link]`.\n4. **Bound your language to your sample.** At n=8 you cannot say \"90% of users.\"\n   You can say \"most\" or \"roughly half.\" Precision you did not measure is a lie\n   with a decimal point.\n5. **Answer from evidence, not from a voice.** Persona questions go to\n   `user-personas`. This skill says what the claims support.\n6. **Carry the disconfirming evidence.** Every finding states what contradicts\n   it, or states that you looked and found nothing.\n7. **Separate frequency from importance.** Eight of eight mentioning something is\n   not the same as it blocking anyone's work.\n\nFull rules, confidence definitions and AI safeguards:\n[references/evidence.md](references/evidence.md).\n\n## Sources\n\n| Source | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| **Dovetail** | What have people told us? Interviews, highlights, past projects |\n| **PostHog** | What do people actually do? Events, funnels, retention |\n| **PostHog session replay** | What does it *look like* when they do it? |\n| **Outset** | What studies have run — or are running right now? |\n| **Pendo** | Guides, polls, in-product surveys |\n| **Support tickets** | What is actually breaking, in volume |\n\nSession replay and support tickets are the two that catch what interviews miss:\nreplay shows the gap between what people say they do and what they do, and\nticket clusters show friction nobody thought to report in a study.\n\nPreflight, sweep order and query recipes: [references/sources.md](references/sources.md).\n\n## Mode 1 — ask\n\nThe core loop. Full protocol: [references/audit.md](references/audit.md).\n\n1. **Make it falsifiable.** Restate as a claim with a subject, a behaviour and a\n   direction. Write what would confirm it and what would kill it *before*\n   searching. No killer means it is not a hypothesis yet.\n2. **Scope it.** Which slice of the Frame — segment, surface, workflow, cohort?\n   Name what is explicitly *out* of scope.\n3. **Check the claims pool first.** Existing claims are free. See\n   [references/frame.md](references/frame.md).\n4. **Sweep** what the pool does not cover, in the order in\n   [references/sources.md](references/sources.md).\n5. **Hunt counter-evidence.** Deliberately look for the disconfirming case.\n   \"None found\" is reportable — but you must have looked.\n6. **Grade and gap.** Every claim HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW / UNVALIDATED. Every gap\n   gets a type and the cheapest way to close it.\n\n### Output\n\n```markdown\n# Context — [topic]\nMode: ask · Date: [YYYY-MM-DD] · Scope: [segment / surface / cohort]\nSources: Dovetail [✅] · PostHog [✅] · Replay [✅] · Outset [✅] · Pendo [—] · Support [✅]\n\n## Question (falsifiable)\n**Claim:** [subject] + [behaviour] + [direction]\n**Would confirm:** … · **Would kill:** …\n\n## Verdict\n[Supported | Partly | Not supported | Unknown — cannot answer yet]\nBest confidence: [HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW|UNVALIDATED]\n\n## What we know\n| Claim | Confidence | n | Source | Link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Frequency vs importance\n| Finding | Mentioned by | Actually blocking |\n|---|---|---|\n\n## What contradicts it\n- … (or: \"searched [scopes], none found\")\n\n## Gaps\n| Gap | Type | Why it matters | Cheapest close |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Settled — do not re-research\n- … (with links)\n\n## Who this affects\n[from user-personas, when a persona slice applies — otherwise say \"not\npersona-scoped\"]\n```\n\n**Decision thresholds.** Say out loud what the confidence supports. Building a\nfeature needs HIGH plus corroboration; deprioritising can run on MEDIUM; a\nstrategic pivot needs HIGH and a sustained pattern. See\n[references/evidence.md](references/evidence.md#what-confidence-justifies-what-decision).\n\n## Modes 2–3 — critique, decide\n\nBoth answer **from the claims**, never from a simulated voice. Run `ask` first, or\nre-read a dated brief — neither mode is useful ungrounded.\n\n**`critique`** takes a mock, flow or copy and says what the evidence predicts:\nwhere it collides with observed behaviour, which assumption it rests on and how\nwell that assumption is evidenced, and what it does not address at all. This is\nthe thing a persona simulation cannot do, because the claims pool is here and the\nsimulation only has a character.\n\n**`decide`** weighs options against what the evidence actually supports, using\nthe thresholds in [references/evidence.md](references/evidence.md#what-confidence-justifies-what-decision)\n— and names what is still blocked rather than ranking everything regardless.\n\n### When a persona card refuses\n\n`user-personas` declines questions that fall outside its research. **That refusal\nis a finding**: an area where we have a persona but no evidence.\n\nCapture it as a gap and, if it recurs, a 🔴 cell on the coverage matrix. The\npersona set already treats a refusal as a research gap rather than a failure;\nnothing currently writes it down, and a gap nobody records gets rediscovered next\nquarter.\n\n## Mode 4 — record\n\nNew finding, new claim. **Research that does not write back does not compound.**\n\nWrite with the `ArtifactData` tool against the Frame:\n\n> **https://claude.ai/artifact/FHc7oAPoyohgxo87VdxCGw**\n\n- One **claim** per finding, into `claims`, with its denominator and source\n- A **need** into `needs` when the finding names something people lack, linked to\n  the claim ids that evidence it\n- Use `batch` for more than a couple of writes\n- The page holds no data, so **writing a claim is not republishing the page**\n\nAlso refresh `verified` when a sweep confirms an existing claim still holds —\nsomeone checked it today and it held, and not recording that ages a good claim\ninto a staleness flag for nothing. Pin every write to an existing document with\nthe `version` you last read.\n\nField shapes, dedup and contradiction handling:\n[references/frame.md](references/frame.md).\n\n## Mode 5 — review\n\nQuarterly, and it is load-bearing — skip it and the Frame degrades silently while\nstill looking authoritative.\n\n- What did we learn this cycle that **updates** what we believed?\n- What did we find that we still **cannot explain**?\n- What would we **do differently**?\n- Coverage matrix — where are the **blindspots**?\n- What used to be true and **isn't any more**? Retire it.\n\nRitual, coverage matrix and decay tracking:\n[references/frame.md](references/frame.md#maintenance).\n\n## References\n\n- [references/frame.md](references/frame.md) — the Frame: claims pool, coverage\n  matrix, belief map, maintenance ritual\n- [references/evidence.md](references/evidence.md) — confidence standard,\n  decision thresholds, AI safeguards, evidence governance\n- [references/sources.md](references/sources.md) — the six sources, preflight,\n  sweep order, query recipes\n- [references/audit.md](references/audit.md) — the `ask` protocol in full\n- [`user-personas`](../user-personas/) — who our users are. Not duplicated here.\n",
"user-personas": "---\nname: user-personas\ndescription: >-\n  Points work at the impact.com user persona set — three partner and\n  three brand personas with jobs-to-be-done, workflow maps and per-claim\n  confidence. Use in Empathize to check what is already known, in Define to\n  frame a problem against a named persona, and in Ideate or Test to decide who\n  a concept must satisfy first.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  audience: both\n---\n\n# User personas (Empathize / Define)\n\nRouting skill for the reference persona set. Does not invent personas — it points at\nthe maintained set and says how to use and refresh it.\n\n## Where the set lives\n\n| Resource | Link |\n|---|---|\n| DesignOps personas page | https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/personas/ |\n| Frame (claims) | https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/frame/ |\n| Markdown copy (in this skill) | [references/personas.md](references/personas.md) |\n| Claude artifact (Ask + live connectors) | https://claude.ai/artifact/FaBjbFu7hiM5LYBfALemAQ |\n\nThe DesignOps pages are the day-to-day view. The Claude artifact is private (Ask / live PostHog) — ask UX Research for access.\n\n## The set\n\nMaturity is the spine. Role sits inside each tier as a variant.\n\n| | Persona | Who | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | **Nia Bennett** | New creator, 1–3 programmes, under $500/mo | 64 / 100 |\n| P2 | **Danny Alvarez** | Working creator, 5–10 brands, $500–$5k/mo | 81 / 100 |\n| P3 | **Priya Shah** | Network operator, 15+ programmes, $5k+/mo | 58 / 100 |\n| B1 | **Sophie Carter** | Founder-operator, wearing a million hats | 62 / 100 |\n| B2 | **Maya Thompson** | Programme manager, 30–200 partners | 76 / 100 |\n| B3 | **James Whitfield** | Partnerships lead, 200+ partners | 58 / 100 |\n\nBrand personas are **roles, not company sizes** — the ladder is how much of the job is\nyours: done alongside other work, done as the work, or owned but delegated.\n\n## The organising idea\n\n**For a partner, impact.com is a waypoint. For a brand, it is a workspace.**\nPartners need speed — every second here is overhead on work happening elsewhere.\nBrands need depth and control — this is where the programme is built, run and paid.\nMost partner friction is speed being taxed; most brand friction is depth or control\nbeing withheld.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Empathize: before commissioning research, check whether the question is already answered\n- Define: name the persona a one-pager is for, and the one it is explicitly not for\n- Ideate: decide which persona a concept must satisfy first\n- Test: pick who to recruit, and pressure-test a concept against the Ask panel\n\n## Tie-breaks\n\n- **Partners** → favour Danny, unless the change removes a capability Priya depends on.\n  A simplification is an inconvenience to one and a permanent loss to the other.\n- **Brands** → favour Maya. Mid-market is both the heaviest user and the group most\n  likely to leave.\n- **Nia** wins only on signup, profile and first-link surfaces.\n- **Creator vs affiliate** → unresolved. Escalate rather than guess (see Gaps).\n\n## Confidence model\n\nEach persona is scored 0–100 on four things, stated openly so it can be argued with:\nhow many people were spoken to at that tier, how many independent sources agree,\nwhether behavioural data corroborates the qualitative finding, and how severe the\nknown gaps are. It is a judgement, not a statistical measure.\n\n**Never cite a persona claim without its denominator.** Several figures that circulated\nas segment-wide rates were six or four people.\n\n## Evidence base\n\n| Source | Coverage |\n|---|---|\n| Outset — Partner Exp | 105 interviews, 13 studies |\n| Outset — Mobile App Exp | 119 interviews |\n| Outset — Brand Exp | 43 interviews, 7 studies |\n| Outset — Contextual Inquiry 2026/27 | 12 sessions |\n| Dovetail — Brand Value Stream | 37 Gong calls, founding research Sept 2026 |\n| G2 | 158 brand reviews, Jan 2025 – Jul 2026 |\n| PostHog | partner and brand cohorts, 90- and 180-day pulls |\n\nQuantitative charts in the artifact are wired live to PostHog. Qualitative claims are\ndated snapshots — re-check them against Outset before quoting in a decision document.\n\n## Known gaps — do not paper over these\n\n1. **No affiliate persona exists.** The Partner Exp screener excluded deal, coupon,\n   review, email and network partners, and no partner-type field exists in product data.\n   Every partner quote is a creator's. These types carry the highest median commissions.\n   Highest-value study available.\n2. **Nobody who signed up and never activated has been interviewed.** Every screener\n   requires active use. That group is the largest by an order of magnitude.\n3. **No brand in its first 90 days has been interviewed.** Sophie is built from reviews\n   and telemetry only.\n4. **English-only recruitment**, though just under half of partner activity is outside\n   North America.\n5. **Application submission is uninstrumented**, so time-to-decision cannot be measured\n   on either side.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Open the artifact. Scan the comparison matrix; click through to the persona in question.\n2. Check the confidence score and the named gaps before leaning on a claim.\n3. Use the Ask panel to pressure-test a concept. It answers only from the evidence behind\n   that card and will decline when a question falls outside it — treat a refusal as a\n   research gap, not a failure.\n4. Export the persona card (PNG or SVG) into the spec, deck or Figma file.\n5. Name the persona and the tie-break in the one-pager.\n\n## Output snippet for write-spec\n\n```markdown\n## Persona\n- Primary: … (confidence …/100)\n- Explicitly not for: …\n- Tie-break if it conflicts with …: …\n- Unmet need this addresses: …\n- Evidence gap to flag: …\n```\n\n## Refreshing the set\n\n- Behavioural charts refresh themselves from PostHog on load.\n- Qualitative findings are snapshots. Re-read the Outset toplines when a new report runs.\n- Re-score confidence when a gap closes, and say in the artifact what moved and why.\n- Related lenses: `creator-affiliate-ux-persona` (partner), `brand-operator-ux-persona`\n  (brand), `research-synthesis` (cross-source triangulation).\n\n## Feedback\n\nSlack the UX Research team.\n",
"user-research": "---\nname: user-research\ndescription: >-\n  Plans and deep-dives impact.com UX research before confirming a problem worth\n  solving. Use when designing studies, interviewing, probing a Research Insight\n  Monitor finding, Outset/Maze prep, Empathize-stage research, or Test loop-back\n  after sessions.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: empathize\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# User research (Empathize)\n\nOn-demand Empathize skill. Complements the scheduled **Research Insight Monitor**; does not replace the human gate.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Prefer verified sources over model recall.\n2. Label **Verified** vs **AI interpretation**.\n3. Never invent quotes, metrics, or participant counts.\n4. Stop at the gate — recommend; designer decides.\n5. If quant N is tiny or qual matches are empty/off-topic, recommend **fieldwork or monitor**, not “confirm problem.”\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Deep-dive one monitor finding before Define\n- Turn a synthesis into a study plan (Outset / Maze)\n- Map evidence gaps blocking the human gate\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Frame** — Who, JTBD, decision unlocked.\n2. **Inventory** — Report + MCP; note Brand / Partner / Shared.\n3. **Gaps** — Prefer `research-synthesis` / `data-analyze` before new fieldwork if sources may already agree.\n4. **Method** — PostHog / Pendo / Dovetail / Outset / Maze as appropriate.\n5. **Plan** — Goals, tasks, success criteria, N heuristic, PII note.\n6. **Gate brief** — Template below with a clear recommendation.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# Research brief — [topic]\n\n## Decision this unlocks\n[One sentence]\n\n## What we already know (Verified)\n- …\n\n## AI interpretation\n- …\n\n## Gaps blocking “problem worth solving”\n- …\n\n## Proposed method\n- Method / audience / tasks / success criteria:\n\n## Recommendation for human gate\n- [ ] Enough evidence to confirm problem\n- [ ] Need fieldwork first\n- [ ] Deprioritize / monitor only\nRationale (cite Verified only):\n\n## Action for designer (next 24h)\n1. …\n2. …\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use user-research on MLW: 32% drop slideout→create and 46% overall to copy. Do we have enough to confirm a problem, or design an Outset study?`\n2. `Use user-research on PublisherPro link gen (14 open → 0 generated). Gate recommendation + study plan if needed.`\n3. `Use user-research for UPS details→send proposal (large Partner volume). What must we still ask before Define?`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Jumping to Ideate solutions\n- Confirming a problem on low-N or empty triangulation\n- Shipping AI summaries past the human gate\n",
"ux-critique": "---\nname: ux-critique\ndescription: >-\n  Checks Ideate mocks and concepts against one-pager, PRD, epic, tickets, and\n  notes — coverage matrix plus solution-quality critique. Use mid-Ideate or\n  before prototype-handoff or Test pre-flight when the designer asks to critique,\n  pressure-test concepts, or confirm framing issues are covered (UX Critique agent).\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: ideate\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: UX Critique\n---\n\n# UX critique (Ideate)\n\nCoverage + concept critique for solution exploration. Distinct from\n`anti-sycophancy` (which pressure-tests **problem framing** in Define).\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Critique against **stated framing sources** (one-pager / PRD / epic / tickets / notes). Do not invent requirements.\n2. Label **Verified** source items vs **AI interpretation** of gaps.\n3. Dual marketplace: call `brand-operator-ux-persona` / `creator-affiliate-ux-persona` when audience is Brand, Partner, or Shared.\n4. Call `design-system` when mocks claim on-brand / component reuse.\n5. **Required before** `prototype-handoff`: no open Critical gaps, or designer explicitly accepts residual risk.\n\nSee [references/coverage.md](references/coverage.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Mid-Ideate: kill weak directions early\n- Before Prototype: designer thinks mocks are ready\n- Test pre-flight: prototype vs framing before Outset/Maze (`test-plan` step 2)\n- “Did we cover the PRD / epic / tickets / notes?”\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\n| Source | How |\n|---|---|\n| Framing pack | One-pager/PRD, epic, tickets, notes used in `ideate` |\n| Current concepts / mocks | Pasted links (Figma/FigJam), screenshots, or chat wires |\n| Personas / DS | Persona skills + `design-system` as needed |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Inventory framing items** — Extract material claims: problems, requirements, success criteria, ticket ACs, noted constraints. Prefer one-pager/PRD; include tickets and notes.\n2. **Map to mocks** — For each item: addressed / partial / missing; cite where (frame, flow, annotation).\n3. **Quality pass** — Off-problem polish? One-sided marketplace? Feature-as-goal sneaking back? DS invent vs reuse?\n4. **Severity** — Critical (blocks handoff) / Major / Minor.\n5. **Gate** — Ready for `prototype-handoff`, revise mocks, or back to `ideate` for more divergence.\n\n## Output template\n\n```markdown\n# UX critique — [title]\nSources: [one-pager / PRD / epic / tickets / notes]\nArtifacts reviewed: [Figma / FigJam / chat wires]\n\n## Coverage matrix\n| Framing item | Status | Where in mocks | Gap / risk | Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| … | addressed \\| partial \\| missing | … | … | Critical \\| Major \\| Minor |\n\n## Keep\n- …\n\n## Challenge\n- …\n\n## Marketplace / DS\n- Brand lens:\n- Partner lens:\n- Design system: reuse … / gaps …\n\n## Gate recommendation\n- [ ] Ready for `prototype-handoff` (no open Critical, or residual risk accepted)\n- [ ] Revise mocks — then re-run `ux-critique`\n- [ ] Back to `ideate` (need more directions)\n```\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use ux-critique on these three Figma directions vs the UX-1234 one-pager + epic tickets.`\n2. `Use ux-critique before handoff — here's the PRD and mock links. Flag anything Critical.`\n3. `Use ux-critique mid-Ideate on D2 vs Granola notes — is this off-problem?`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Softening Critical gaps to be “helpful”\n- Approving handoff with uncovered ticket ACs unmarked\n- Confusing with `anti-sycophancy` (re-litigating whether the problem exists)\n- Inventing PRD requirements not in sources\n",
"ux-install-all-skills": "---\nname: ux-install-all-skills\ndescription: >-\n  Installs all 37 impact.com UX Design Thinking + UX Research pipeline skills\n  (Empathize through Test, plus Frame research skills) into the current project\n  and globally for Cursor and Claude Code. Use when the user says\n  ux-install-all-skills, install all skills, install the design thinking\n  skills, set up the UX pipeline, get started with Design Thinking UX, or a\n  designer needs the full skill pack before using the framework.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: pipeline\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n---\n\n# UX install all skills (bootstrap)\n\n**One invocation → all 37 pipeline skills installed.** Do not ask the\ndesigner to curl, pick a harness, or copy folders. The script does that.\n\nThis skill does **not** start Empathize → Test. After READY, offer\n`design-thinking-ux` or `ux-research` and wait.\n\nCatalog: [references/catalog.md](references/catalog.md).\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Run the install script** — do not improvise `mkdir` / `cp` / `curl`\n   of individual skills unless the script is missing.\n2. **Install the full pack.** Never install a subset “to keep it simple.”\n   The orchestrator needs every child skill.\n3. **Do not start the pipeline** unless they asked for a guided run in the\n   same message. Install, confirm READY, entice `design-thinking-ux`.\n4. **Overwrite is OK.** Re-running updates skills. Do not ask “replace?”\n5. **No-shell harnesses** (Figma Make, Claude chat): give the zip / skills\n   page. Do not pretend a local install ran.\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use `ux-install-all-skills`” / `/ux-install-all-skills`\n- “Install the Design Thinking skills” / “set up the UX pipeline”\n- First time a designer opens a project without the pack\n- `design-thinking-ux` (or a phase skill) is missing its siblings\n\n## What gets installed\n\n**37 pipeline skills** (masters + Empathize/Research → Test, including\nNihal’s Frame pack). This installer is copied too so they can re-run later.\nNames: [references/catalog.md](references/catalog.md).\n\n**Where (defaults — do not ask):**\n\n| Location | Path |\n|---|---|\n| Canonical | `<project>/.agents/skills/<name>/` |\n| Cursor | `<project>/.cursor/skills/<name>/` (symlink) |\n| Claude Code | `<project>/.claude/skills/<name>/` (symlink) |\n| Cursor global | `~/.cursor/skills/<name>/` |\n| Claude global | `~/.claude/skills/<name>/` |\n\nGlobal copies mean the pack works in **any** later project, not only this\nfolder.\n\n`--project-only` if they said “just this repo.” `--target ~/path` if they\nnamed another folder. Otherwise cwd.\n\n## Workflow (Cursor / Claude Code / Copilot)\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Locate or bootstrap the script\n- [ ] Run install-all.sh (sandbox off)\n- [ ] Parse READY\n- [ ] Tell them they’re set + offer design-thinking-ux\n```\n\n1. **If the skill is not in the catalog** (Claude.ai, or a blank Cursor/Claude Code\n   project) — do **not** wait for `use ux-install-all-skills`. Run the published\n   installer:\n\n   ```bash\n   curl -fsSL https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/install-all.sh | bash\n   ```\n\n   Claude.ai in the browser cannot run this. Tell the designer to paste the\n   same curl in **Cursor or Claude Code**.\n2. **If the script is already on disk** —\n   `.agents/skills/ux-install-all-skills/scripts/install-all.sh`  \n   (or `.cursor/skills/…` / `.claude/skills/…`).\n3. **If the script is missing** (blank project), bootstrap with the curl above.\n   If that URL 404s, use the harness installer:\n\n   ```bash\n   curl -fsSL https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/install.sh | bash -s -- cursor .\n   ```\n\n   Replace `cursor` with `claude` or `agents` when you know the harness.\n4. **Run with the sandbox off.** In Cursor Shell, pass\n   `required_permissions: [\"all\"]` (network + write to `~/.cursor` /\n   `~/.claude`).\n\n   ```bash\n   bash \"/absolute/path/to/ux-install-all-skills/scripts/install-all.sh\"\n   ```\n\n   Optional: `--target /path/to/project` · `--project-only` ·\n   `--global-only`.\n5. **Parse READY** — look for\n   `IMPACT_SKILLS_READY pipeline=37 …`.\n6. **Confirm** — show how many skills landed, where, and this next line:\n\n   > All 37 UX Design Thinking and Research skills are installed. Start a guided run with\n   > **use design-thinking-ux** — I’ll walk Empathize → Test and stop at\n   > each human gate.\n\n7. **Stop.** Wait for yes (or a named phase skill).\n\n## Path — Figma Make / Claude chat (no shell)\n\n1. Point them at the [skills page](https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/)\n   and [designops-skills.zip](https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/designops-skills.zip).\n2. If they later open Cursor or Claude Code, they can re-run this skill\n   there for a local + global install.\n3. Do not block the current chat on a local install.\n\n## Check the installer\n\nFrom repo root:\n`python3 .agents/skills/ux-install-all-skills/scripts/validate.py`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Asking the designer to copy skill folders by hand\n- Installing only `design-thinking-ux` and hoping the rest appear\n- Starting Empathize intake in the same turn as install (unless they asked)\n- Skipping global install without `--project-only` / an explicit “this repo only”\n- Treating a zip download in Make as a substitute for running this in Cursor\n  when they actually have a shell\n",
"ux-prototyping": "---\nname: ux-prototyping\ndescription: >-\n  Prototype-phase orchestrator for any harness (Cursor, Claude Code, Claude\n  chat, Figma Make, Copilot/Codex): installs missing Brand/Partner tools when\n  a shell exists, launches a live Brand or Partner preview (localhost, Make\n  preview, or Claude preview), then offers to publish a new GitHub repo on\n  the designer’s account. Use when the user says ux-prototyping, start a\n  prototype, Brand or Partner prototype, localhost, Figma Make prototype, or\n  GitHub Pages.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: prototype\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Prototype Scaffolder\n---\n\n# UX prototyping (any harness → live preview → GitHub)\n\n**Stay in this harness.** Do not send the designer to Cursor (or any other\ntool) to finish Prototype. Detect the harness, get a **live Brand or Partner\npreview** running here, then ask about GitHub.\n\nThis skill does **not** scaffold a random Vite/Next app. The prototype is\nalways the Brand UI shell or the Partner UI shell — via bootstrap, Make\npreview, or Claude preview.\n\n| How | See |\n|---|---|\n| Per-harness steps | [references/harnesses.md](references/harnesses.md) |\n| Figma Make clone + `.figma/make` | [references/make.md](references/make.md) |\n| GitHub ownership / Pages / fork | [references/github.md](references/github.md) |\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Finish in the current harness.** Cursor, Claude Code, Claude chat,\n   Figma Make, Copilot/Codex — all valid. Do not bounce them.\n2. **Exclusive shells** — Brand → Brand shell. Partner → Partner shell.\n   Never `npm create vite` / Next as a substitute.\n3. **Shell harnesses:** run `ensure-harness.sh` then that shell’s\n   `bootstrap.sh`. Do not hand a git/npm checklist.\n4. **No-shell harnesses (Make, Claude chat):** do not pretend `bootstrap.sh`\n   ran. Use that harness’s live preview (Make canvas or Claude artifact).\n5. **Do not create repos under someone else’s GitHub user** (including\n   `lesley-spec`) unless `gh api user` / the connected GitHub login **is**\n   that user. Designers publish to **their** account (or an org they can\n   create in).\n6. **Do not push to the template remotes** (`one-program-1`,\n   `PartnerUI-shell`).\n7. GitHub is **opt-in** after the live preview works.\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use `ux-prototyping`” / `/ux-prototyping`\n- After mocks / `prototype-handoff`, in **any** designer harness\n- `design-thinking-ux` Prototype phase\n\n## 0. Detect harness (do this first)\n\n| You have… | Harness | Path |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shell + Cursor `move_agent_to_root` | **Cursor** | A |\n| Shell, no Cursor root-move | **Claude Code / Copilot / Codex** | A |\n| Figma Make canvas / `figma.com/make` | **Figma Make** | B |\n| Claude.ai / Claude chat, no shell | **Claude chat** | C |\n\nIf mixed (e.g. Claude with a terminal), prefer **A**.\n\nAsk Brand vs Partner only if unknown.\n\n## Path A — shell (Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot, Codex)\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Harness script\n- [ ] Brand vs Partner bootstrap\n- [ ] Live localhost\n- [ ] Workspace (Cursor)\n- [ ] GitHub prompt\n```\n\n1. Sandbox off (`required_permissions: [\"all\"]`):\n\n   ```bash\n   bash \"/absolute/path/to/ux-prototyping/scripts/ensure-harness.sh\"\n   ```\n\n   Parse `IMPACT_HARNESS_READY`.\n\n2. Read `brand-prototype` or `partner-prototype` `SKILL.md`. Run **its**\n   `scripts/bootstrap.sh` (sandbox off).\n\n3. Parse `IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY … path=… url=…`. Verify the URL. Tell the\n   designer it is live.\n\n4. Cursor: `move_agent_to_root` with `path`. Claude Code: File → Open `path`.\n\n5. GitHub prompt (below). Path A uses `publish-repo.sh`.\n\n## Path B — Figma Make\n\nDo **not** run bash bootstrap. Make runs the preview.\n\nFollow [references/make.md](references/make.md):\n\n1. Confirm Brand vs Partner.\n2. **Preferred:** Clone the public template into Make (desktop / codebase\n   session) so the real Vite shell is the preview.\n3. **Always available:** New Make file → implement Brand/Partner chrome from\n   the source Figma file + selected mocks + `design-system`. Preview = live.\n4. Confirm the designer can click the preview. That is READY.\n5. GitHub prompt — Make **Settings → GitHub → Create Repository** on *their*\n   connected account (Make cannot create under someone else’s user).\n\nPrint:\n\n`IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY shell=brand|partner harness=figma-make url=<Make preview or file URL>`\n\n## Path C — Claude chat (no shell)\n\nDo **not** run bash bootstrap. Do **not** send them to Cursor.\n\n1. Confirm Brand vs Partner.\n2. Build a **live interactive preview in this chat** (Claude artifact /\n   preview) of that shell: chrome, nav, tokens from `design-system` + the\n   source Brand/Partner Figma file. Use selected mocks if they pasted links.\n3. Confirm they can click through it. That is READY.\n4. GitHub prompt — no `gh` CLI. Walk **fork** of the public template onto\n   *their* GitHub user, then Pages. See [references/github.md](references/github.md).\n\nPrint:\n\n`IMPACT_PROTOTYPE_READY shell=brand|partner harness=claude-chat url=<artifact or preview URL>`\n\nIf this Claude later grows a terminal, switch to Path A for the Vite shell\n**in addition** — do not block Path C on that.\n\n## GitHub prompt (every path, after READY)\n\n> The prototype is live here. Do you have a GitHub account? If yes, I can\n> publish a **new repo under your GitHub user** (not the DesignOps template\n> account) so you can share a Pages URL.\n\n| Answer | Do |\n|---|---|\n| No / later | Stop. Live preview is enough. |\n| Yes | Path A: `publish-repo.sh`. Path B: Make GitHub Create Repository. Path C: fork + Pages on github.com. |\n| Yes, org `X` | Owner = `X` if they can create repos there. |\n\n**Never** create under `lesley-spec` unless that is the logged-in / connected\nGitHub user.\n\n## Prompt examples\n\n1. `Use ux-prototyping — Partner, get it running here.`\n2. `Use ux-prototyping in Figma Make for Brand.`\n3. `Use ux-prototyping in Claude — I don’t have Cursor open.`\n4. `Preview works — I have GitHub, publish it.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Sending Make or Claude users to Cursor to “finish” Prototype\n- Asking the designer to install Node / Homebrew / `npm install` when a\n  script or Make install exists\n- Scaffolding a new Vite/Next app instead of Brand/Partner\n- Pushing to `lesley-spec/one-program-1` or `PartnerUI-shell`\n- Creating a repo under another person’s user\n- `gh repo create` / Make Create Repository before they confirm GitHub\n- Claiming Path B/C ran `bootstrap.sh`\n\n## Success criteria\n\n- [ ] Harness detected; work finished **here**\n- [ ] Live Brand or Partner preview (localhost **or** Make **or** Claude)\n- [ ] GitHub asked; if yes, repo is on **their** login (or their org)\n",
"ux-research-master": "---\nname: ux-research-master\ndescription: >-\n  One front door for the whole impact.com UX pack. First run installs all 32\n  skills plus the eight /ux-* starter prompts and writes the routing rules, so after\n  that a plain-English message gets matched to the right skill on its own.\n  Research-led: it asks what decision this unlocks and what the Frame already\n  says before it asks anyone to run a study. Use when the user says\n  ux-research-master, /ux, install the UX skills, set me up, get started,\n  which skill should I use, or opens a UX or product question without naming a\n  skill.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: pipeline\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: UX Research Master\n---\n\n# UX Research Master (front door)\n\n**One skill to remember.** It installs the pack, prints the starter prompts,\nand routes everything after that. It does not do research itself — it names\nthe skill that should, loads it, and gets out of the way.\n\nThree masters sit under this one. Know the difference:\n\n| Master | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `ux-research-master` (this) | Install, routing, starter prompts. Entry point. |\n| `ux-research` | A guided **research** run: intake → Frame → plan → build |\n| `design-thinking-ux` | A guided **design** run: Empathize → Define → Ideate → Prototype → Test |\n\nRouting table: [references/routing.md](references/routing.md) ·\nStarter prompts: [references/prompts.md](references/prompts.md) ·\nRule block: [references/rules.md](references/rules.md).\n\n## Integrity\n\n**Lead with the insight, not the evidence.** What the person gets back is\nshort: the finding, what it changes, and the next thing to do. A few lines, not\na report — they came for a decision, not a download.\n\n**Offer the depth, don't deliver it.** Close with one specific follow-up they\ncan run — \"want the evidence trail behind finding 2?\" — not a menu of five. The\ndetail stays available; it does not arrive unasked.\n\n**A number earns its place by changing a decision.** Everything else goes in the\nartifact (the claim, the card, the one-pager) where someone can find it when\nthey need it.\n\n1. **Route, don't rewrite.** When routing says invoke `skill-name`, Read\n   `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (fallback `.claude/skills/` or\n   `.cursor/skills/`) and run that skill's workflow in **this** chat. Never\n   paraphrase a child skill from memory, and never ask the designer to type\n   `use skill-name` themselves.\n2. **Name the pick, then proceed.** One line — the skill, why it fits, and the\n   runner-up — then start. Only stop and ask when the top two lead to\n   genuinely different outcomes.\n3. **Evidence before fieldwork.** A question that may already be answered goes\n   to `user-context` or `research-intake` before `research-planner`. Never\n   route straight to a new study because someone asked for one.\n4. **Label Verified vs AI interpretation.** Never invent a metric, a quote or\n   a participant. A synthetic persona pass is a hypothesis, never a finding.\n5. **Honour child stop conditions.** Jira, Confluence, Outset and Pendo stay\n   draft-only until a human confirms. Never launch recruitment or publish a\n   guide to live traffic.\n6. **Install once, silently after that.** Only re-run the installer when the\n   pack is missing or the designer asks.\n7. **Say what is degraded.** If a connector the chosen skill needs is not\n   authorised, say so before running, name what the skill will fall back to,\n   and continue — do not pretend the data is there.\n\n## When to use\n\n- \"use ux-research-master\" · `/ux` · \"install the UX skills\" · \"set me up\"\n- \"which skill should I use for…\" · \"what can this pack do\"\n- Any UX, research or product question where no skill was named\n\nIf they named a **single** skill, run that one. If they asked for the full\nresearch run, hand to `ux-research`. If they asked for the full design run,\nhand to `design-thinking-ux`. Do not wrap a named skill in this one.\n\n## First run in a project\n\nDetect first run: `.agents/skills/ux-research-master/` is absent, or\n`.claude/commands/ux-frame.md` is absent, or the rule block is not in\n`CLAUDE.md`.\n\n```\nTask Progress:\n- [ ] Run install-master.sh (sandbox off)\n- [ ] Parse UX_MASTER_READY\n- [ ] Connector preflight\n- [ ] Print the menu, then stop\n```\n\n1. **Install.** Run with the sandbox off (Cursor Shell:\n   `required_permissions: [\"all\"]` — it writes to `~/.claude` and `~/.cursor`).\n\n   ```bash\n   bash \".agents/skills/ux-research-master/scripts/install-master.sh\"\n   ```\n\n   Not on disk yet? Bootstrap the whole pack, master included:\n\n   ```bash\n   curl -fsSL https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/install-all.sh | bash\n   ```\n\n   This installs 32 skill folders, nine `/ux-*` commands (project + global,\n   Cursor + Claude Code), and writes the routing block into `CLAUDE.md` and\n   `AGENTS.md`. Flags: `--project-only` · `--global-only` · `--no-rules` ·\n   `--target PATH`.\n\n2. **Parse** `UX_MASTER_READY skills=32 commands=9 rules=…`. Do not claim a\n   count the script did not print.\n\n3. **Connector preflight.** Check which MCP servers are authorised in this\n   harness and report in one short block — what is live, what is not, and\n   which prompts degrade. Mapping: [references/routing.md](references/routing.md#connectors).\n   Never ask for tokens; tell them to authorise in connector settings or\n   `/mcp`.\n\n4. **Ask the question and print the menu** from\n   [references/prompts.md](references/prompts.md) — *\"How can UX research help\n   you today?\"* and the eight prompts, verbatim. Do not add, reorder or\n   re-word them, and do not append skill names.\n\n5. **Stop.** Wait for a number, a `/ux-*` command, or a sentence.\n\nNo shell (Claude.ai chat, Figma Make): do not pretend an install ran. Point at\nthe [skills page](https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/skills/) and the\nzip, say the `/ux-*` commands need Cursor or Claude Code, and keep routing by\nhand in this chat — routing works without an install.\n\n## Every run after that\n\n1. Read the message. Match it in [references/routing.md](references/routing.md).\n2. Say the pick in one line: *\"That's a Frame question — loading `user-context`\n   (runner-up: `research-synthesis` if you want the sources triangulated).\"*\n3. Load that skill and run it.\n4. At its stop, name the next step and wait. Never auto-advance past a human\n   gate.\n\nIf nothing matches, ask one question — what decision this unlocks — and route\non the answer. If five things match, pick the one that answers the decision\nsoonest and name the rest.\n\n## The four questions behind the routing\n\nResearch-led means these come first, in this order, whatever was asked:\n\n1. **What decision does this unlock?** No decision → no study. Say so.\n2. **Do we already know?** → `user-context`. A question already answered does\n   not need a method.\n3. **If not, how big?** → `research-intake` routes MICRO (48h) / SPRINT (1–2w)\n   / DEEP (4w+), and who runs it.\n4. **How will we know it worked?** → the measure gets built **before** the\n   study, not after. `research-builder`, then `adoption-check` post-ship.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Printing the menu again on every turn — once per session is enough\n- Putting more than the eight prompts on the menu, or renaming them to skills\n- Routing to `research-planner` before anyone checked the Frame\n- Re-running the installer because the designer said hello\n- Answering a research question from this skill instead of loading the child\n- Reporting a connector as missing without saying which prompts it degrades\n- Listing five candidate skills and asking the designer to choose\n",
"ux-research": "---\nname: ux-research\ndescription: >-\n  Master UX research orchestrator for impact.com. Routes research requests\n  through intake, the Frame (user-context), personas, planning/builder skills,\n  study summaries, assumption and measurement checks, recruitment, experiments,\n  evaluations, publishing, and the Empathize pack. Use when the user says\n  ux-research, /ux-research, start research, research request, what do we know,\n  triage this study, or wants the full research skill set rather than a single\n  phase skill.\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: pipeline\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: UX Research\n---\n\n# UX Research (master)\n\nOn-demand **research orchestrator**. Loads when someone wants research done\nwell — not when they only named a single child skill.\n\nDoes **not** replace child skills. Reads and runs one child at a time, then\nentices the next step. Stops at human gates (route confirm, study launch,\nproblem-worth-solving).\n\nChild routing: [references/routing.md](references/routing.md).\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. **Orchestrate, don’t rewrite.** When this skill says invoke `skill-name`,\n   Read `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (fallback `.cursor/skills/<name>/`)\n   plus any references that step needs, then execute that skill’s workflow in\n   **this chat**.\n2. **One child skill at a time** unless that skill itself calls another.\n3. **Check the Frame before planning fieldwork.** Prefer\n   `research-intake` → `user-context` over jumping to a new study.\n4. **Label Verified vs AI interpretation.** Never invent metrics or quotes.\n5. **Honor child stop conditions.** Do not launch Outset recruitment or create\n   Jira tickets unless the child skill allows it after designer confirm.\n6. **Join anywhere.** If they already have a routed request, Frame answer, or\n   study plan, start at that step.\n\n## When to use\n\n- “Use `ux-research`” / `/ux-research` / “start research”\n- Research request, triage, “what do we already know”\n- Unclear which research skill to start with\n\nIf they named a **single** child (`research-intake`, `user-context`,\n`user-research`, …), run that skill directly — do not wrap it unless they also\nasked for the full research run.\n\n## Session start (always)\n\n0. If sibling research skills are missing, invoke `ux-install-all-skills` first.\n1. Show the **research board** (template below). Fill what you already know.\n2. **Intake** — batch these:\n\n   - What decision does this unlock?\n   - Audience: Brand / Partner / Shared?\n   - Do you already have a Frame answer, Dovetail link, PostHog insight, or study?\n   - Is this a new request, a deep-dive on a monitor finding, or post-ship check?\n\n3. Route with [references/routing.md](references/routing.md). Confirm the\n   landing skill in one sentence, then run it.\n\n## Default path\n\n```\nassumption-mapper (when a brief exists)\n                 →  research-intake  →  (already answered? stop with Frame link)\n                 →  user-context / user-personas as needed\n                 →  research-planner (+ panel-planner / research-builder)\n                 →  user-research / study-summary / research-synthesis\n                    / data-analyze / synthesize-research\n                 →  findings-publish\n                 →  (after ship) adoption-check\n                 →  (quarterly) frame-maintenance\n```\n\nCross-phase lenses from Nihal’s pack when relevant: `competitive-analysis`,\n`measurable-check`, `talk-to-users`, `heuristic-evaluation`,\n`persona-check`, `ab-test-planner`, and `adoption-check`.\n\n## Research board\n\n```markdown\n# UX Research — [title]\nAudience: Brand | Partner | Shared\nStep: Intake | Frame | Plan | Empathize pack | Pre-test | Adoption\nStatus: INTAKE | IN STEP | GATE | DONE\n\n## Board\n| Step | Status | Artifacts | Gate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Assumptions (`assumption-mapper`) | | | Load-bearing guesses named |\n| Intake (`research-intake`) | | | Route MICRO/SPRINT/DEEP or closed |\n| Frame (`user-context`) | | | Gaps named |\n| Personas (`user-personas`) | | | Persona named if needed |\n| Measure (`measurable-check`) | | | Measurable / partly / not yet / not countable |\n| Plan (`research-planner` / `panel-planner` / `research-builder`) | | | Study + audience + instruments ready |\n| Empathize pack | | | Problem-worth-solving / fieldwork |\n| Pre-test (`heuristic-evaluation` / `persona-check`) | | | Hypotheses only |\n| Publish (`findings-publish`) | | | Human-approved destinations |\n| Adoption (`adoption-check`) | | | Outcome written to Frame |\n\n## Next\n[one sentence]\n```\n\n## Site\n\n- Frame (DesignOps): `https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/frame/`\n- Claude live Ask: https://claude.ai/artifact/FHc7oAPoyohgxo87VdxCGw\n- Insight Monitor: `https://lesley-spec.github.io/uxdesignops/v1/research/`\n",
"write-spec": "---\nname: write-spec\ndescription: >-\n  Turns confirmed Empathize research into an impact.com UX one-pager and linked\n  UX Jira epic (Confluence draft + research context). Use for Define-stage\n  problem framing, write-spec, one-pager, PRD draft, or Problem Definition\n  Generator handoffs after “problem worth solving.”\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  stage: define\n  pipeline: impact-ux-designops\n  author: impact.com-ux\n  figjam-agent: Problem Definition Generator\n---\n\n# Write spec (Define)\n\nOn-demand Define skill. Implements the FigJam **Problem Definition Generator**:\nconfirmed research → one-pager (+ optional PRD depth) → auto-linked Jira/Confluence.\nDoes **not** approve framing — Designer signs off when happy, then chats with a PM before Ideate.\n\n## Integrity\n\n1. Require Empathize gate: designer confirmed **problem worth solving** (or explicit override).\n2. Label **Verified** vs **AI interpretation**. Never invent metrics/quotes.\n3. Pressure-test with dual marketplace lenses (`brand-operator-ux-persona`, `creator-affiliate-ux-persona`) + `anti-sycophancy`.\n4. Draft first; **create Confluence/Jira only after the designer confirms** the draft.\n5. Minimize PII in Confluence/Jira.\n6. **Atlassian Rovo MCP** — fetch existing tickets/pages with `getJiraIssue` / `search` yourself. Do not ask the designer to paste a Jira issue or run `/mcp` until a tool call fails.\n\nSee [references/integrity.md](references/integrity.md), [references/one-pager.md](references/one-pager.md), [references/publish.md](references/publish.md), [references/atlassian-rovo.md](references/atlassian-rovo.md).\n\n## When to use\n\n- Empathize finding confirmed; need a one-pager + UX epic\n- “Write a spec / one-pager / problem statement” for a UX initiative\n- Link research pack evidence into Jira so tickets are not orphaned\n\n## Context gathering (always)\n\nPull before drafting — do not rely on chat memory alone:\n\n| Source | How |\n|---|---|\n| UX DesignOps FigJam | Figma `get_figjam` on board `slhZ9uzMTmaekjmbEosyzb` (Define column + pipeline) |\n| Prior chats | `SearchConversations` for the topic / finding |\n| Research pack | `research-agent/docs/research/latest.json` + related HTML report |\n| Empathize outputs | Pasted synthesis, or re-run `research-synthesis` / `user-research` briefly if missing |\n| Personas | Load `brand-operator-ux-persona` and/or `creator-affiliate-ux-persona`; Confluence Brand UI Personas / Partner Personas |\n| Existing specs | Atlassian Rovo MCP `search` in space `ux` + Product Shaping template |\n| Named Jira key | `getJiraIssue` immediately — do not ask them to paste the issue |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Gate check** — Confirm problem worth solving. If not, send back to Empathize skills.\n2. **Gather** — FigJam + chats + research pack + personas (table above).\n3. **Frame** — Problem/opportunity (no solution), audience (Brand / Partner / Shared), JTBD, ranked pains.\n4. **Pressure test** — Apply anti-sycophancy: weakest evidence, missing side of marketplace, goal ≠ feature.\n5. **Draft one-pager** — Chat markdown using [references/one-pager.md](references/one-pager.md) (UX short form by default; expand to full Product Shaping sections when PM-facing).\n6. **Designer review** — Show draft + open questions. Do not publish yet.\n7. **Publish (on confirm)** — Confluence page in space `ux` (draft OK) + UX **Epic** with research context and one-pager link. See [references/publish.md](references/publish.md).\n8. **Stop** — When the Designer is happy with framing, they chat with a PM before Ideate (`ideate` skill). Do not invent solutions boards.\n\n## Output template (chat draft)\n\n```markdown\n# One-pager draft — [title]\nStatus: DRAFT · Audience: Brand | Partner | Shared\nSources: [FigJam / chat ids / latest.json / Confluence links]\n\n## Problem / opportunity (Verified + AI interpretation labeled)\n…\n\n## Audience & JTBD\n- Audience:\n- Jobs-to-be-done:\n- Brand lens:\n- Partner / creator-affiliate lens:\n\n## Evidence (Verified)\n- PostHog / Pendo / Dovetail / Outset / Freshdesk — with links\n- Discarded / weak evidence:\n\n## Goal (customer + business — not the feature)\n…\n\n## Success criteria\n- Quant:\n- Qual:\n- Exit / Designer + PM gate:\n\n## Scope\n- In:\n- Out:\n\n## Risks & open questions\n…\n\n## Proposed publish\n- Confluence: space `ux`, title “…”, parent [UX Roadmap or ask]\n- Jira: project `UX`, type Epic, labels e.g. `ux_roadmap`\n- [ ] Designer confirmed — ready to create\n```\n\n## Prompt examples (tested)\n\n1. `Use write-spec on UPS Step 2 (details → send proposal). Empathize gate OK for draft. Pull FigJam + latest pack; draft only — do not create tickets.`  \n   → Draft with Verified ~7110→1810 (−74.5%); weak qual flagged; stops for confirm.\n2. `Use write-spec on PublisherPro link gen (14→0). Confirmed worth solving — draft one-pager.`  \n   → Should refuse full Define framing (tiny N) → back to Empathize.\n3. `Use write-spec: turn this Empathize synthesis into a Confluence one-pager under Impact UX and a linked UX Epic — ask me before creating.`\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Jumping to Ideate / mocks inside the one-pager “Approach” before Designer sign-off and a PM chat\n- Creating Jira/Confluence without an explicit confirm\n- Asking the designer to paste a Jira issue or enable Atlassian Rovo before calling the MCP tools\n- Collapsing Brand and Partner into one “users” statement\n- One-pagers with no Verified research links (violates “100% tickets with research context”)\n"
};
